      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <p>4. 44.40.2.4.+4.44-1401.1444.44444.+4. ,i. .1. .1. .1. POULTRY PEN $ .1. 44 Hints and Interesting Facts and .I. 44 Chat for Amateurs. .14 j..1.44.1014.1..1.44.1014.1..1.÷.1.4.491.401.4.44</p>
        <p>EGG INFORMATION. A series of tests and observations has fur- nished the following information relating to the average weight and yield of eggs produced by some of the more popular breeds of poultry:— Geese,, 4 to the lb., yield 20 per annum; turkeys 5 to the lb., yield 30 to 60 per annum; ducks, 6 to the lb., yield 30 to 60 per annum; black Span- ish, 7 to the lb., yield 150 per annum; Le Fleche, 7 to the lb., yield 130 per annum; Houdans, 8 to the lb., yield 160 per annum; Langshans, 8 to the lb., yield 150 per annum; Brahmas, 8 to the lb., yield 130 per annum; Plymouth Rocks, 8 to the lb., yield 120 per annum; Hamburgs, 9 to the lb., yield 200 per annum; Leghorns, 9 to the lb. yield 200 per annum; Polish, 9 to the lb., yield 150 per annum; Gi11,13 fowl, 9 to the lb., yield 130 per annum; Guinea fowl, 11 to the lb., yield 160 per annum; Lantams, 16 to the lb., yield 60 per annum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>HOUSES AND HOUSING.</head>
        <p>HOUSES AND HOUSING.</p>
        <p>While elaborate, costly houses are not neces- sary for poultry, at the same time, substantial structures are recommended, those that are well made and free from draughts. It is sometimes asked why poultry should be housed at all, because it has been proved over and over again that the birds are more healthy and more vigorous when allowed to roost in the hedge- rows and trees than when confined in houses. A farmer in Suffolk allows all his fowls, consisting of four to five hundred, to live as near to nature as possible, supplying them with no houses in which to sleep, and making them forage for their own food. His birds are invariably in ex- cellent health, being strong and of a hardy con- stitution, besides which the plumage is always in fine condition. He confesses, however, that he never obtains any eggs from the beginning of October to the end of February. Herein lies the whole point. If the only aim we have in keep- ing poultry is that they shall be strong, healthy, in good condition, and of fine plumage, irre- spective of their profitableness, then his plan may be recommended. But when one is anxious to procure eggs in winter, then it is necessary to provide warm and comfortable houses in which they can sleep. - -</p>
        <p>Materials. A point of foremost importance is the material of which the house is made. If there is already an existing building suitable for the purpose, then it will be unnecessary to erect one specially. When, however, one has to be made, the best material is undoubtedly wood. Only so.ind tim- ber should be employed, and for permanent houses the thickness should be not less than an inch, whilst for portable houses five-eighths is sufficient. A heavy portable house defeats its own purpose. The boards should be tongued and grooved, placed in an upright position, and not parallel to the ground. The best roof is made of thatch, hut as this is an expensive and somewhat unsuitable roof for a poultry house, corrugated iron is generally advised in its place. Thatch, however, maintains the most equable tempera- ture, being warm in winter and cool in summer. Iron alone is insufficient, and is a rapid conduc- tor of heat and cold, and when used by itself, the interior of the house is too hot in the sum- mer, and too cold in the winter. It _should be lined with some non-conducting, material, and ' thin matchboarding is excellent for the purpose. Perhaps the best roof that can be used for a poultry house is composed of corrugated iron with matching underneath, and between the two a layer of felt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>Floor.</head>
        <p>The Floor.</p>
        <p>The ground makes the best form of floor it is possible to have, and with both permanent and portable houses it is quite easy ,to use the around. If the house has to stand in a damp position, the interior should be filled up a few inches with dry earth or gravel, and this, if well beaten down, makes a most excellent floor. Cement and bricks are both unsuitable for floor- ing, the former as it is too cold, and the latter as the bricks are absorbent, and absorb some of the liquid portion of the manure. If for any reason the ground can't be used, wood is the next best material. Peat moss litter is the most suit- able covering, as it is cheap, easily procurable, a deodoriser, and an absorbent, holding the am- monia from the manure in suspension. When it is finished with in the house it makes an excel- lent manure for the garden if used in small quantities, but being almost indestructible, it must not be used on the land in large quantities, since this makes the land somewhat boggy. Fine, dry earth or sand scattered over the floor does very well, hut peat moss litter should always be employed when possible.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Gather young pods of French and runner beans. The crop will be all the greater for being thinned. If there are more beans gathered than can be used at the time preserve the overplus in salt pickle. They will come in very handy later on when the last of the beans are gathered.</p>
      </div>
