      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>HOW FARMERS CAN HELP.</p>
        <p>Recommendations as to the Foods to Increase. I The Board have received the following from , the Agricultural Consultative Committee : I The following recommendations must in all , cases be dependent upon:— (1) Local condi- tions, and (2) the circumstances of the indivi- dual holding. But, generally, the Agricultural Corisultative Committee are of opinion that, in the existing circumstances, agriculturists should do all in their power to secure that the supply of home-produced foodstuffs may be in excess of the normal. In this respect, the re- quirements of the future with regard both to cropping and to maintenance of the fullest complement of live stock that holdings can usefully carry, must be carefully borne in mind. 1. The acreage under wheat should be largely increased wherever practicable. In this direction it should not be forgotten that on clean land, and by the aid of suitable artificial manure, good crops of wheat can be obtained in successive years. Attention is drawn to Section 26 of the Agricultural Holdings Act, 1908, which permits any system of cropping subject to the holding being protected from deterioration.</p>
        <p>2. Where wheat cannot be grown, the sowing of winter oats, winter barley, and rye might be substituted. These crops ripen early, and allow the labour on harvest to be evenly dis- tributed. 3. The cabbage crop is also one to be con- sidered where land can be spared. It provides a considerable weight of food suitable for either human or animal consumption. 4. There is much land of a certain class now under grass which would probably pay for breaking up. If this land is schednled as- arable in the farm agreement, the tenant has the option of ploughing it up. If it is scheduled as grass the Agricultural Consulta- tive Committee suggest co-operation between owner and occupier as to the advisability of breaking up certain fields in view of the national question of increasing home-grown food-stuffs. 5. Where a surplus of grass or clover exists, ensilage might be made. Particulars of the best methods can be obtained from the Board of Agri- culture Leaflet No. 9. 6. The slaughter of immature or breeding stock of every description should be avoided. Where circumstances permit, the total head of live stock should be increased, particularly animals such as pigs, which multiply quickly. Ewe lambs might with advantage be put to the ram towards the end of the year. 7. While there should be no diminution in the numbers of live stock kept, the strictest economy (subject to proper conditioning) and foresight with regard to feeding is advocated. The cheapest efficient forms of food should be used, and no waste spaces capable of producing food for animals should be allowed. No recom- mendation is attempted as to the exact descrip- tion of the foodstuffs to be grown, as this must depend upon the special circumstances of each case, of which generally the individual farmer will be the best judge; if in doubt, he can obtain advice gratis from the recognised agricultural college in his "province," or from the county agricultural organiser. But the following crops among others are worthy of consideration : Trifolium, vetches, rye for spring feeding, and Italian Tye grass. By adopting such measures, the more valuable foods would, so far as possible, be freed for human consumption. Board of Agriculture and Fisheries, August 18th, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>FARMER'S SUGGESTION. War and Wheat.</head>
        <p>A FARMER'S SUGGESTION. War and Wheat.</p>
        <p>I would suggest that, as our soil can roduce more wheat per acre than any other soil in the world, it would be good policy to grow more wheat next year. By doing this we shall be helping ourselves, and rendering good service to the nation. Food will be dear, therefore every- body should do all he can to increase the sup- plies, for the people must be fed. No farmer with any real fellow feeling desires to get rich through the misfortune of his brethren and country. However, here is an opportunity for the farmer to increase his wheat area profitably, and I have not the least doubt a big effort will be made—and rightly so, too.—Sheergraves in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Government Guarantee?</head>
        <p>A Government Guarantee?</p>
        <p>Why, when the growing of increased sup- plies of food is of national importance, should not the Government stimulate it by undertak- ing to give the farmer a minimum price for his crop? asks" Farm and Home." Assuming this price to be reasonable, it is obvious that, whatever may happen, the cost to the Government will be inconsiderable. The war may come to an end soon, but in France, Germany, Belgium, Austria and Russia, and perhaps elsewhere, the sowing of the crops for next year's reaping will be terribly interfered with, and the harvest next year must in the most favourable circumstances be short. This probability, it may be said, will alone be suffi- cient inducement to the English farmer to grow as much wheat as he can.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>SOW AND PLANT MORE VEGETABLES. Put out some strong plants of early cauli- flowers, to come into use in late October. Give a good soaking of water after planting. An excellent lettuce for both winter and sum- mer growth is All the Year Round lettuce, and is quite hardy. It is of the cabbage variety, compact, and of small size. A small pinch sowed at intervals is better than sown in bulk.</p>
        <p>Sow turnips for winter use, making the soil fine, and water well before sowing. Potatoes are better out of the ground when they are ready, and the space can be filled with cabbages and turnips. As advised last week, fill all available ground with vegetables. After cabbages are cut it is customary in most gardens to pull up the stumps. In such times as the present economy must be practised, and instead of throwing the stump away let it re- main after cutting the beads of cabbage. as high as possible up the stem so as to leave a tuft of leaves on the old stump. Cut a cross in the top of the stump, and in due course nice secondary growths will result.</p>
        <p>THE GAILLARDIA SUNFLOWER. The new red sunflower, which was introduced last year, was acclaimed as a floral novelty of the first importance. It was then anticipated that even finer forms might be obtained. This has proved correct, for the raiser has recently staged many hybrids, with striking variations in colour. The original form bore a striking resemblance to a Gaillardia, hence the name. In the new hybrids there are variations, Langley Gem being , rosy claret on a lemon ground; Bronze King is wholly dark bronze; while Langley Diamond has a bronze zone, and is dwarf in habit. These new varieties will be most acceptable by garden- ers. The ordinary sunflowers have been looked upon as not being of much class of late years.</p>
        <p>LAVENDER AS HEDGING OR STANDARD.</p>
        <p>Those on the look out for something different to what other people grow for dividing flower gardens from the vegetable portion might do worse, and certainly not much better, than to utilise lavender for the purpose. A few plants here and there along a row soon extends until a most serviceable hedge is secured, and one also which is the more pleasant on account of the sweet smell which emanates. Lavender may also be grown in standard form hy keeping the plant to one main stem. Huh off all side shoots until the desired height is attained. Lavender should he pruned after flowering, when new growths are made. Lavender is not difficult to root from cuttings of even old wood.</p>
        <p>-INOMIIMI...- A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>intercession Z.".iervices by all Denominations.</head>
        <p>intercession Z.".iervices by all Denominations.</p>
        <p>INSPIRING LETTER FROM THE BISHOP.</p>
        <p>Services of prayer and intercession continue to be made in the Warrington churches. The Catholics have had a special intercession service in all their five churches, the Nonconformists muted in another service, which was held in Golborne-street Baptist Church on Wednesday evening, and in the churches on Sunday an in- spiring letter was read from the Bishop of Liverpool. Below we give reports of the repre- sentative services.</p>
        <p>Probably he the cause of overwhelming the city, and the innoCent would there suffer with the guilty. To take the same lesson in a domestic sense, they found that the drupken father in- volved his whole family in his poverty, Shame and disgrace. The wife, the mother, might or might not have known what the husband was before she married him, but the children, with- V any chance of protest, were brought into the lie and compelled to share in the shame to which they were no agents. So strange are our lives woven ' together that the innocent and guilty suffered, together. It was patent there must be some great Divine purpose in the scheme of Providence. It showed God's love for humanity, for He had knitted the whole race , together into one brotherhood and Made us in- dispensable to one another.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>BISHOP'S LETTER. Read in the _Warring lon Parish</head>
        <p>A BISHOP'S LETTER. Read in the _Warring lon Parish</p>
        <p>Churches:</p>
        <p>The Right Rev. Dr. Chavaste, Bishop of Liver- pool, has addressed the following letter anent the European War, to all Anglican Churches throughout' the diocese, and on Sunday it was read in the local churches, as in all others in the diocese : My dear Brethren of- the Clergy and Laity. ' The thought uppermost in all our minds, and very seldom absent from them, is the War, which, like a bolt from the blue, has suddenly burst upon us. In a moment, Europe, which for years has been an armed camp, has become the scene of a conflict more widespread in its extent, more terrible in its character, and more far-reaching in its results than any war in modern times, and perhaps in the history of the world. Our Lord's description of the last days, when "nation shall rise against nation and kingdom against kingdom," when "there shall be dis- tress of nations with perplexity; men's hearts failing them for fear and for looking after those thin g 4 which are coming upon the earth," applies with extraordinary accuracy to the pres- ent condition of affairs. At such a time there comes to us a fivefold call.</p>
        <p>1. A Call to Repentance.—As individuals, and as a nation, we have grievously sinnid against God; and our sins seem to cry against us for vengeance. Let us search and see what our sins are—our personal sins, our home sins, our busi- ness sins, our national sins. Let us confess them to God. Let us put them from us. Let us plead for pardon through the merits of our atoning Lord. Let us walk humbly with God. "When the host goeth forth against thine enemies, then keep thee from every wicked thing." 2. A Call to Prayer.—We have forgotten God as a nation. We have neglected His worship. We have set our own, interests and pleasures before His honour. A large part of our people have had religious sentiments, but very few have been mOved by religious convictions. A poet of our own has exposed our state:—</p>
        <p>The inbred fault and meanness of our ,time In art, in thought, in polity, in trade, I charge directly to the ruined will That neither takes nor leaves the omnipotent Creator, the immortal soul of man, Heaven, Hell, the Cross of Christ, and all that once Was great in Christendom when God meant God. We must get back to God. We must reinstate Him. We must put Him first in our daily life, our homes, our business, our nation. We must fall to prayer. God has heard our cry as a nation in the past. He will hear us again. We must beg His mercy and implore His help, and that He will be a defence to us against the face of the enemy. We can ask His aid with a clear conscience. We did not seek this war. We did not provoke it. We did not desire it. Our statesmen laboured hard for peace until the last momeut. It was only when every effort to avert it had failed, that we unsheathed the sword, for our own honour's sake, for the liberties of the weak, which we had solemnly pledged ourselves to secure, for the peace of Europe, for the defence of our own shores. With a clear conscience we can beseech God "to take the cause into His own Hand, and to judge between us and our enemies." Let our churches and parish halls stand open unceasingly for prayer, and let us lift up our hearts continually on behalf of our country, Christendom and the world. 3. A Call to Patience.—None can foretell how long the war will last. We must be prepared for the worst. A great, brave and resolute nation like the Germans are not likely to yield at once, though the whole world be against them. Enormous losses of life and money may lie before us. Hardship and suffering must be the lot of us all before peace dawns again. We must be prepared to exercise long patienge and persistent self-denial; to be calm under reverses and humble in the time of success; to face sor- row, suspense, and suffering without murm'uring for the sake of our country and our fellow men. " In your patience ye shall win your souls." 4. A Call to Self-Sacrifice.—We must be pre- pared to give up luxuries and comforts, to live simply, to help to bear the burden of others, and to share what we have with those who have less. W. e must keep from panic, and keep others from it. We must neither believe nor spread idle rumours set afloat by the enemy as to dis- asters by sea and land. Our duty is To steel our hearts against the lust of ease; To find our welfare in the general good; To hold together, merging all degrees, In one wide Brotherhood.</p>
        <p>To teach that he who saves himself is lost; To bear in silence, though our hearts may bleed; To spend ourselves, and never count the cost, For others greater needs.</p>
        <p>To go our quiet ways. subdued and sane, To hush all vulgar clamour of the street; With level calm to face alike the strain, Of triumph or defeat.</p>
        <p>5. And not least a Call to Thankfulness.—Let us thank God for the wisdom, the decision, and the spirit of calm dignity with which our rulers have met the crisis; for the courage, the self- control, the unity and devotion of our people; for the heightened sense of duty, for the seri- ousness and silent resolution which characterise the nation. The imminence of peril has awak- ened us out of sleep, has caused us to cast off the slough of self-indulgence, frivolity, indiffer- ences, and disunion, and has cast us upon. God. " In God let us put our trust, and not be afraid. What can man do unto ,us?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>" TIIE JUSTICE OF GOD."</head>
        <p>" TIIE JUSTICE OF GOD."</p>
        <p>Rev. 11. Ogden and the War.</p>
        <p>The justice of God was the subject of the Rev. H. Ogden's discourse at the Golborne-street Bap- tist Church on Sunday evening, when he took for his text "Shall not the God of all the earth do right?" Genesis xviii. 25. The situation of the text was certainly in strange environments. The chapter revealed that the only state of justice that then existed over sin was the extermination of the sinner; it was not only au eye for an eye, a life for a life, but the total extermination of the whole clan or of the whole city. Those two ancient cities of Sodom and Gomorrah were as unclean as they were wealthy, but God was willing to spare them if ten righteous people could be found in them.: Surely it was an advanced state of decomposition' in anybody when ten bones could not be found without decay. However, Abraham was plead- ing for his nephew and his family. It was the same nephew who sent his aged uncle into an unknown land, whilst he, as a man without am- bition and without vim, lazily sought after the nearer land that flowed with milk and honey. Such a temperament and such a character was not likely to escape the pest. Morally, he was anemic, and was not a mortal to toy with typhoid. Mon Who Do Not Fight. Oneoft he of men oe fn hai tm t ha s o tni emtel uEg vhet no ft 1: t msaunnyi day afternoon one saw a group of a dozen stal- wart young men, fit for service for their country, happy and contented playing marbles, whilst a nation and a Continent were in peril. In think- ing about the justice of God one was faced with the fact that the innocent suffered with the guilty. Disaster overtook the whole of those two ancient cities, which, as bad as they were, must have contained one solitary virgin soul. In Brussels that day a stray shot from some obscure ally, directed by some obscure individual, would</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>International Sense.</head>
        <p>International Sense.</p>
        <p>What was true in the domestic sense was equally true in the international sense. "God made of one blood all the nations of the earth." Germany had contributed her share to the world's brotherhood, even the Kaiser, in his best work, had been a consolidating force for his own people, until he had violated the great spirit of brotherhood and co-operation by the enforcing of his 'lndividuality upon the world. Individual pride and vain glorious pomp were now out to wreck the work of ages. The German masses would now suffer for the individnal assertion of the military clique.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>tVORKING MEN'S MISSION, Devon Minister's Sermon.</head>
        <p>tVORKING MEN'S MISSION, Devon Minister's Sermon.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. Dawson, of Hartland, Devon, who is on a visit to Warrington, conducted the morn- ing service at the Working Men's Mission on Sunday. His text ("Take your share of the hardships as a good soldier of Jesus. Christ," 2 Tim. ii. 3), he said, could be related to the crisis of the present day. After .alluding to the peculiar parallel between military and Christian. service, and pointing out several necessary quali- ties needed by the soldiers both on the material and spiritual battlefields, the preacher said that hardships were a necessary part of the spiritual training of the nations. In the study of the history of Israel this was found to be the case, and in our consideration of this crisis was it not possible that on the unseen plan of God's equip- ment of the races this war could be marked as a part of the nations' spiritual training? Surely it could not be regarded as a visitation of God on account of our sins. The Scriptures did teach men that sin must ever bring punishment; but they did not teach them that public calami- ties involving all classes, good and bad, like the wreck of the Titanic, or the present war, were a judgment of God for some particular sin. God made no blunders like that. It simply meant that natural law works, and all men were sub- ject to it irrespective of virtue or vice. It only behoved all to be ready. To illustrate his point the reverend gentleman said that Christ's refer- ence to the eighteen people who were killed by the fall of the Tower of Siloam was made in order to show that it was not an actual judg- ment for sin, and that such a calamity, in a lesser or greater sense, might come upon men at any time, but He warned them to be prepared by being followers of Him. All calamities then were really only normal events of life, to be met and endured; and if this war was a part of our spiritual training it behoved all men to recognise the call.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Personal Righteousness.</head>
        <p>Personal Righteousness.</p>
        <p>It was a call to personal righteousness. How- ever many good men there were in the world it was impossible to stay the evils of the war; but through the event all men would see that they were expected to save their souls by entering upon a holy life of active, strenuous service. "We must open our eyes wide to our national sins," said the 'preacher,. "our pride, our self- satisfaction because of our wealth, power and prosperity; our growing indifference as a people to public worship; our want of compassion and organised care for the poor." Was it not this pride, fostered by an abundance, which made many wealthy employers of to-day keep the labourer at his trade, the weary seamstress at her needle, not only through the heavy hours of the night, but also through the sacred hours of the Sabbath Day? Pride of wealth and power had made many men think they were.independ- ent of God, and this national calamity called upon them to consider these weaknesses, and thus to save their souls from them." There was also in the call a demand upon all men to take their share in the hardships attend- ant upon this war. There were many burdens to be borne and shared, and no matter whether it be the merchant or the shopkeeper who placed prohibitive prices on their foodstuffs, or those who amassed vast stores of goods for themselves at the expense of the poor and needy, all were called upon to learn the lesson of taking their share of the hardships and thus become good soldiers of. Jesus Christ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>NONCONFORMIST SERVICE. The Rev. H. Ogden and the Catastrophe.</head>
        <p>NONCONFORMIST SERVICE. The Rev. H. Ogden and the Catastrophe.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Free Church Council held a meeting for intercession in the Golborne-street Baptist Church on Wednesday evening. Coun- cillor Henshall presided over an, attendance ,of about 200, and the ministers present were the Revs. H. Ogden, W. T. Jaye, A. J. Chessum and J. S. Chedburn.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that the meeting had been arranged primarily for intercession. He was not going to say anything about matters relative to war, but he asked the members of the various churches present to join in beseeching the Almighty that He might look down in mercy upon the English nation, and upon all the nations in Europe, and that He would vouchsafe speedy pekice that would be as lasting as the world deserved.</p>
        <p>PRAYER FOR 2,000 KILLED AND INJURED. After the reading of Psalm xlvi., "God is Our refuge and strength," several of the people present joined in heartfelt prayer, particularly for the relatives of the 2,000, who, by latest ad- vices, had been killed or wounded in the battle at ions. The tone of .the meeting and the fer- vour of the prayers revealed the' deep sense of responsibility prevalent amongst the Free Churches.</p>
        <p>In the unavoidable absence of the Rev. Wm. Reid, who should have given the address, a few words were spoken by the Rev. H. Ogden, the secretary of the Council. He said that alien forces must haye been at 'work to bring this awful catastrophe upon Europe. It could not, surely, he the will of God that civilised men should be engaged in such deadly conflict; one had only to consider the traits and characteris- tics and national sins of peoples to find that such traits woutd lead to war.</p>
        <p>A Hideous Practice. Jerome K. Jerome, in a recent article, had pictured for us the hideous practice of duelling in Germany- Students resorted to some wayside ,inn, a room of which NV;IS splashed with beer and blood. They hewed and hacked at one another until their faces and limbs were fearfully and frightfully disfigured. The healed .wounds were marks of beauty and evidences of bravery. If it all ended there in the wayside inn, it would be well; .no great harm would result. But such a practice, and such ideas, wrought an unholy ambition in the mind of the nation. Strangely, side by side with Jerome K. Jerome's picture of German student life was a view of the Kaiser's upon English drinking habits. He was amazed at the English demand for fiery liquids; he was astonished that the pnglish nation should be so debased in its desire for alcohol. Surely, duelling was had, and so was a national craving for intoxicants; low ambitions, cruel desires and vile habits heeded to be purged from a national life.</p>
        <p>Blessing Out of War.</p>
        <p>We all hoped that in some gracious and won- derful way, God would bring a blessing even out of the present curse. Job, in the midst a bis</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 29, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 29, 1914.</p>
        <p>TIT CYCLISTS' NOTEBOOK. Best Time to Buy a New Machine.</p>
        <p>PLACES WORTH SEEING.</p>
        <p>R huddlan Castle.—At Rhuddlan, near Rhyl, is to be seen the massive, ivy-cvered structure which one can easily believo was, in the old days, practically impregnable. Placed at a con- siderable height it stands out clearly from its surroundings, and presents itself to the view as the most prominent object in the landscape; The date of its foundation is uncertain, though the structure is acknowledged .to be aged a thousand years or so. It was burnt, one author- ity records, two or three hundred years before the Norman Conquest, and another notes its occupation by Llewellyn ap Cyssylt, a'Prince of Wales of the 11th century. Rhuddlan. Castle is particularly interesting in its connection with Edward I. The story is knOwn to everyone, how Edward delivered an address to the Welsh chieftains promising to give them a prince born amongst them, one who had never spoken a word of English in his life, and whose conduct had always been irreproachable. Here, in 1284, he received tidings of the birth at Carnarvon of the son who succeeded him, and who was the first Englishman. to hold the title of Prince of</p>
        <p>Wales. At Rhuddlan Edward held a Council or Parliament, at which was enacted the memor- able "Statute of Rhuddlan "—a new code for the government of Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>CLUB RUNS.</head>
        <p>CLUB RUNS.</p>
        <p>W.W.C.C. on the Sands.—Only twelve miles from Warrington the cyclist will find a beauti- ful stretch of firm clean shingle and sand at ' Hale Cliff, on the north of the Mersey estuary. Twenty-six on an autumn run into Lancashire does not speak badly for the life of the club, and we were excellently catered for at the village of Hale by Mrs. Stonehouse, Pavilion Cottage, almost the first house as you approach the vil- lage from the Warrington side. The remark- able thing about Hale at present is the abund- ance and the cheapness of the garden flowers, and Pavilion Cottage is no exception. Whih't tea was preparing the club ran down a lane to the water's muddy edge, opposite Runcorn, not at all a pleasant experience, but after tea a delightful run towards Speke soon landed Us at Hale Cliff, where the sands begin. The direct road down the cliff has been blocked up lately, but a short diversion is permanently open, and the splendid range of sand and beach extends round almost to, Garston. The view across is very fine, and the air is quite sea-sidy, for Che- shire is three miles away from that point in the sister county. (The road down to the lighthouse has now been blocked up for two years.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>DING DONGS.</head>
        <p>DING DONGS.</p>
        <p>Ordering New -Mounts.—Strange to say, the very best time to get a new bicycle is just now, at the, end of the season. This year it is patriotic as well. The editor of "Sport and Play" writes: "We do not wish to appear un- gallant, but it is a fact that most ladies are inclined to look first at the price, and then at the article. Itne this is the reason why so many of the ladies' machines which one meets are of the low-priced variety. It also happens that ladies seldom try any other mount except their own, and so they miss altogether the higher pleasure of the pastime." A Suitable Cycle.—When ordering either a male or female cycle, state your weight and in- side length of leg, in order that the machine may be suitable for your "reach." Who Was to Blame?—" Stop pushing; there's no hurry !" said the front, wheel to the rear, and the latter blamed the hub. The hub put it on the sproket wheel, who said it was the chain; the chain kicked to The crank, and the crank located the trouble in the pedals. The pedals said, "hush, it's a scorcher," and they all groaned.</p>
        <p>CLUB RUNS TO-DAY. Warrington C.C.—Knutsford. Wesleyan C.C.—Mobberley. Sankey Briet'es W.G.—Barnton. St. George's C.C.—Northwich.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>LAMP TIME. To-day, 8.15; next Saturday, " SILVER-STREET."</head>
        <p>LAMP TIME. To-day, 8.15; next Saturday, " SILVER-STREET."</p>
        <p>THE CHURCHES AND THE WAR—continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <p>extremity, was advised to curse God, and to die; just at the moment it seemed the only pos- sible advice. D4rived of his estate, robbed of his family, and blighted in body, it would seem at the moment as if there was no hope for him, and no light for his days. Yet after his severe discipline we learned that Job's estate was re- turned to him twofold, his domestic happiness was doubled, and health returned to his body.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Moment of Affliction.</head>
        <p>The Moment of Affliction.</p>
        <p>The moment of affliction was a severe moment. We were tempted to lose faith. But let us be- lieve that the future held a blessing that should return to us twofold, even outweighing the curse of the present. As those who believed in God, we could sing with Cowper : His purposes will ripen fast, „Unfolding every hour; " The bud may have a bitter taste, But sweet will be the flower. The service concluded with the hymn "God bless our native land."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>CATHOLICS INTERCEDE.</head>
        <p>CATHOLICS INTERCEDE.</p>
        <p>Services in the Warrington Churches,</p>
        <p>Throughout the Roman Catholic arch-diocese, Sunday was devoted to intercession Services in connection with the war, as directed by their Archbishop of Liverpool. The following "prayers to be recited during the war at Mass and Benediction," extracted from the "Mass in Time of War," were offered at all the five ser- vices held in the town in the course of the day</p>
        <p>0 God, Who dost make all wars to cease, crushing by the might of Thy strong defence the foes of them that put their trust in Thee, succour us Thy servants, who entreat Thy mercy, and break down the savage power of our enemies, that with unceasing thankfulness we may give praise to Thee. Be appeased, 0 Lord', by the sacrifice which we offer; may it be our safeguard in all the dangers of war, and may it enable us to count upon the sureness of Thy defence. 0 God, the Sovereign Lord of kingdoms and of kings, Who, in chastising dust hear, and in forgiving, save; show forth upon us Thy mercy, and restore to us the quiet times of peace, that we may make use of them to our amendment. Through Jesus Christ, Thy Son, Our Lord, Who liveth and reigneth with pee in the unity of the Holy Ghost, world without end. Amen. Spare, 0 Lord, spare Thy people, and be not angry with us for ever. Private visits for purposes of prayer were also made to the churches during the afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>P.S.A.-ERS AND THE WAR.</head>
        <p>P.S.A.-ERS AND THE WAR.</p>
        <p>The meeting of. the Wycliffe P.S.A. on Sun- day, which was presided over by the Secretary (Mi.. B. Farrar), was specially devoted to the European War. Mr. Llewellyn delivered an /address upon: "What manner of !persons should' we be?" This was a question, said the speaker, which could only be answered after a study of God's Word—literature which should be read as eagerly as any other book. Every man pos- sessed gifts, and these he should guard ard keep as if they were precious jewels. for Even the most precious jewel could not compare withithe gifts of God. "What shall it profit a man if he gain the whole world and lose his soul?" Truly the soul was the priceless gift to be treasured above all things, and man could only treasure it by affording it peace. The accumu- lating of riches could not give peace to the soul; this was illustrated by the great war at present in progress. Only could the souls of men obtain peace when those men realised to the full the great love which God bestowed upon them, and the gift of Christ for man's salvation. When such was realised by men, war would have become impossible. A portion of the meeting was devoted to prayer for the intercession of God, in His mercy, that the awful slaughter of men by their fellow-men might speedily be brought to a conclusion. The Chairman led the prayer. The lesson was read by Mr. Yearsley, and the meeting closed with the singing of Mr. Alfred Corney's " Hymn of Intercession.'!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORING. AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHY'</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING. AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHY'</p>
        <p>Econcmies and Changes the Suggests.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>Hints on the Care and the Repair of Machines</head>
        <p>Hints on the Care and the Repair of Machines</p>
        <p>LADIES' HEADGEAR.</p>
        <p>The necessity of suitable' headgear is a matter that is frequently overlooked by cyclists of the gentler sex. Some of the hats which one sees worn by lady cyclists are more suitable for a garden party than for the road. The imuort- ance of the matter was emphasised a short time ago by a terrible accident to a lady in Ireland. The fatality may be attributed entirely to the wearing of unsuitable headgear. A gale was blowing at the time, and the lady found it neces- sary to remove one hand from the handlebars in order to hold on her hat. She was passing a steam roller at the time, and her machine skidded OR the wet road. Being unable to re- cover her balance, with only one hand on the machine, the unfortunate lady was thrown , under the roller and met a terrible death. Apart from considerations of appearance and comfort, it is absolutely necessary from the point of view _ _ _ . . . _</p>
        <p>of .safety that a lady should make quite sure before setting out for a ride that her headgear is of the proper kind, then she can devote her whole time to looking after the control of the cycle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>Tram Cars.</head>
        <p>Tram Cars.</p>
        <p>Useful as the average tramcar is to the general public, there Is no doubt that it is an intolerable nuisance to other users of the road. It takes up a great amount of space, it is a large bulky vehicle, it is not easy to see round, while the use of centre standards for carrying the overhead wires is another source of danger. It is not likely that any amount of objection will ever cause their removal from our midst, but it can be insisted upon that companies and committees owning these tramway systems should obviate at least one big danger to cyclists by seeing that the roadway is kept in proper condition. The road surrounding the rail has a tendency to sink and the road surveyors should put matters right whenever this occurs with the least possible delay. A raised rail in the centre of the road has before now been the cause of numberless accidents, and as these are entirely preventable by the exercise of proper supervision and the expenditure of a little money, no excuie should be listened to from them for allowing their tracks to get out of repair.</p>
        <p>Single Tube Tyres. Those who are able to carry their minds back over a matter of fifteen years or so will remem- ber that at that period there was a considerable craze for the single tube American tyre. I always held the opinion that this type was faster than when an inner and outer tube are used, but judging by the small number of these tyres that are in use to-day, others evidently do not believe the same. It is a mistake, however, to imagine that this type is extinct, since I was consider- ably surprised the other day to find that a num- ber of riders have them fixed to their machines. I have been receiving some inquiries from readers asking where these can be bought and fixed. I regret that I cannot tell them, and surely it is a surprising thing that those who have them for sale do not advertise them. That they can be purchased I know, but where? That is the difficulty. It would be advantageous if it were possible to bring buyer and seller in touch with each other at the present moment.</p>
        <p>Cycle Adjustment. So far as comfort when riding goes—and com- fort means increased power both as regards speed and distance covered—the true secret lies in the adjustment of the machine to the rider. Years ago when people paid big prices for bicycles and were anxious to spend money. on them, a fad arose for having the bicycle built to the measurement of the rider, in the same way as a high-class gun is built for its pros- pective owner. Nowadays, no one wourd go in for this sort of thing, and it is doubtful indeed whether any maker would undertake so to dis- arrange his factory as would be requisite for the building of such special machines. At the same time, the capacity for adjustment in machines has now been so increased that there is no reason why any rider should not adjust entirely to his requirements, especially as regards the distance which the saddle should be from the pedals.</p>
        <p>Repair Charge's. There are occasions when it is necessary for the average motor cyclist to make use of the services of a repairer for his machine, since the work to be done is beyond his abilities. When this is the case I would advise all to obtain an estimate for the work before it is begun. If nothing is said on this point and an arrangement come to by both parties, the repairer can legally charge a reasonable sum for his time, labour and skill. The difficulty is that it is difficult for both to agree as to what is a reasonable price to charge. Owner and repairer both have definite ideas on the subject. The latter has power to retain the machine until the debt is liquidated, but he cannot dispose of it in any way. To bring the law to bear on the matter, especially as such a mall sum is concerned, is a costly and bothersome business. Obtain an estimate before the work is commenced, and it will save a considerable amount of trouble in the future.</p>
        <p>The Care of The Cycle. The typical motor cyclist is always eager to adjust and clean the engine on his machine, tak- ing pleasure in the workt but very few of them .ever seem to think that the cycle itself requires attention if the whole is to give the best re- sults. It is accounted a trouble to attend to any part of the machine with the exception of the engine. This is a great mistake, since if the cycle parts are not working smoothly it will cause a strain on the engine and the best can- not be obtained. I would advise all motor cyclists to give a look to the whole machine whenever they are starting off for a ride. If this is done it will save a considerable amount of money in the long run for renewals. Attention should be bald to the brakes, tyres, chain or belt, spokes, and the lubrication. Looking at it in another way, such attention may prevent a serious accident happening to the rider. The life of the rider depends on the minutest part of the machine as well as on the greater parts, and no one can say when something may happen which will bring a greater strain to bear on the working parts. If they are out of order there may be trouble in store for the cyclist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>CAUTION TO FARMERS. Vampires Who Would Trade on Their Couniry.'s Need.</head>
        <p>CAUTION TO FARMERS. Vampires Who Would Trade on Their Couniry.'s Need.</p>
        <p>It has come to the notice of the authorities that persons purporting to represent the pur- chasers who are buying horses that have been impressed for the Army, are either obtaining or seeking to obtain commission from farmers on the price paid for the horses taken. Action of the kind is quite irregular and unauthorised. Farmers are informed that all impressing is done by constables, who issue an order in accordance with a magistrate's warrant to each owner whose horse and vehicle is impressed. Without this order served by a constable owners need not give up their animals and Vehicles, and they should insist on seeing it. During the recent mobilisation, however, the rush of willing people to help has often meant that this procedure was not followed. Voluntary sale and purchase of horses will, of course, now continue in the ordinary course of business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>N,!tMUR.</head>
        <p>N,!tMUR.</p>
        <p>Namur, a strongly foiitified city, ranks with Antwerp and Liege as one of Belgium's great fortresses. The same famous military engineer, General Brialmont, planned the thtee, con- structing round .them about thirty years ago a ring of self-contained and well-armed forts. Liege kept the. middle Meuse where it ap- proaches the German and Dutch borders; Namur, standing where the Sambre and Meuse join, barred the way along the river valleys into France. Namur has nine forts placed between two and five miles from the city. The Population numbers about 30,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>CARBON PRINTING.</head>
