      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>"IS DEAD AS QUEEN ANNE:"</head>
        <p>"IS DEAD AS QUEEN ANNE:"</p>
        <p>H 4 the Accession of the Georges as First Anaounccd in a Dissenting</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Chapel.</head>
        <p>Chapel.</p>
        <p>IHE BLCENTENARY OF OUR REIGNING DYNASTY.</p>
        <p>aPileaval of the past few days has Nid d the attention which might have been lilt (Cc' au interesting anniversary. It is two thellsra Years since Queen Anne died, and left Itacu,(Le"sian to the present reigning .house of hui,en'''r• Queen Anne had nineteen children, their-,8 alaY be learned from the long list of klihe,-4211es upon the 'stones of Westminster 4.55 , 'hey all died in childhood, and there . reatatled, to the throne a prince who was so `3‘eq from us as not to know the Eng- ilgaage, the monarch known to history -rirst of the Georges.</p>
        <p>THE DEATH OF AN ERA. Ay the advantage of those who habitually ntn.,‘ As dead as Queen Anne," it may be ,14.`11 out that a great deal died, or appeared (11e, With her, and that though she may have he More effectually than any other queen, death marked the closing of a dynasty, the o' an era, and the beginning of new hopes pi, hew programmes. eattiithe Nonconformists, in particular, the 4,essi°of Anne was an outstanding event. Her 1rki,..11 to the throne had darkened what was</p>
        <p>117 a Clouded sky. The Queen lost no op- IlltS of showing them that she purposed • bv"3(l s tow•ar d theiti. When the Dissenting ees,; ra Presented a loyal address on her she listened in discourteous silence, jeat them away without a word of thanks. "" first address f • the Throne she laid re&amp;,__ ress from</p>
