      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>CHAUFFEUR'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>CHAUFFEUR'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>A Doctor's Story of the Accident to the Late Harry Howarth</p>
        <p>CORONER AND JURYMEN.</p>
        <p>There was a peculiar incident at an inquest held in Warrington on Tuesday. The inquiry was upon Harry Howarth, who was employed by Messrs. garland, Bridge-street, and who died over a week ago as the result of injuries he sustained in a collision with a motor car, which he was bringing from London to War- rington, with a military cyclist, near St. Albans, on 23rd April. He was so injured that he lay for several days unconscious in the St. Albans Hospital. Later he was able to come home to his residence at 3, Willis-street; War rington, but a week past on Monday he had a relapse and died. The inquest was adjourned from last week, in order that fresh evidence might be obtained from St. Albans. At the adjourned inquest on Tuesday a St. Albans doctor made an important statement.</p>
        <p>CORONER AND JURY. The inquest was called for 9.30 a.m. on Tues- day, and some time after that the Coroner (Mr. S. Brighouse) discovered that two of the jury- men had not attended.' He said lie felt-very indignant at the way the two jurymen had treated him. He had been coming to War- rington for 30 years, and never before had any jurymen treated him with so much discourtesy. The two jurymen had entered into recognisances of £lO each to attend that day and he would be compelled to estreat that sum. He thought it was altogether 'too bad of the jurymen to treat him as they had done. There was him- self, ten jurymen, two solicitors, and a doctor, who had come at considerable inconvenience from St. Albans, and all had been upset by the absence of two jurymen, who. lived near'' the Co nit.</p>
        <p>The Coroner then asked Detective-Sergeant Holland'if •there were not two spare jurymen. 'Sergeant Holland : Yes, but both have en- listed. The Coroner: I will s-z,y nothing about them, hut about the other two I shall say something. Sergeant Holland said they were warned to attend the inquest by the polite on Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>ONE JUP,YMAN ARRIVES.</head>
        <p>ONE JUP,YMAN ARRIVES.</p>
        <p>After a long wait one juryman presented him- self and a policeman' was sworn in to complete the jury of "twelve men good and true." The Coroner: What has the juryman who has just arrived got to say for himself? I am wait- for him to say something. He will have to say something or I shall say something. The Juryman : I am sorry. To tell the truth, I have only wakened up. The. Coroner : Yo knew you had to be here at The Juryman: I have had a•rough night. The Coroner: Have you been on the booze? The Juryman : No; I have been out of town. The Coroner Said he would not estreat that particular juryman's recognisances, but in the case of the man who had not turned up he would report to the County Council that lie bad estreated him.in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>DOCTOR'S STORY.</head>
        <p>A DOCTOR'S STORY.</p>
        <p>Dr. Cecil Gilbert Pearse, St. Albans, said on 23rd April he was motoring behind the car driven by Howarth. The latter was going at about 15 miles an hour. He saw Howarth's' car strike a. cyclist who was coming in the opposite direction. The motor car was on the wrong side of the road. Howarth seemed as if he tried to avoid the collision, and as a result the car ran into a bank and came to a stand- still. Howarth was badly injured and witness gave him all the attention he could. Mr. Steel: What was the width of the road?— About 18 feet.. The Coroner said that the police measure- ment, of the road showed that it was 19ft. Bin. wide, and the car ran 108 feet after the colli- sion.</p>
        <p>The doctor said that his impression was that Howarth was temporary fatigued and momen- tary went to sleep and thus caused the accident. He had felt similar symptoms when driving his car, but had always stopped. Mr. Steel: Did the cyclist carry a lamp?—He carried a lamp, but.it was not lighted, as it was not lighting up time. The Coroner added that the cyclist, a soldier, seemed to have been driven right through the wind-screen and may have struck Howarth. The soldier then seemed to have fallen on the roadside. He had ascertained that the soldier had recovered and was now on active service. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death," and passed a vote of sympathy with the relatives.</p>
      </div>
