      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <p>TIM EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 29, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>HARVEST OF THE SEA.</head>
        <p>HARVEST OF THE SEA.</p>
        <p>Food Supply that Needs no Sowing</p>
        <p>BY C. E. PARKER. I have lately returned from a holiday trip in Scotland. We were away before the Euro- pean situation had become serious, and spent most of our time in the little town of Stone- haven, about twelve miles from Aberdeen. Wherever you go in Scotland you find historic associations. Stonehaven as a town has been in existence since the sixteenth century. Its town cross and old toll-booth or prison still stands. A mile along the coast is situated Dunnottar Castle. The ruins of this castle are on a rocky eminence and date back to the twelfth century. In 1296 it Was held by the English, but was besieged and taken by the famous Scottish chieftain, Sir William Wallace. In the stormy seventeenth century the regalia of Scotland was stored there for safety. Cromwell's troops, under Lambert, besieged and took the castle, but the regalia- was removed by a Mrs. Ogilvie, who hid the crown under her cloak. The castle was also used as a State prison for Covenanters in the reign of Charles the Second. In Dun- nottar Churchyard, near by, is the Covenan- ter's stone, whose preservation is due to the veneration of Robert Pattison, a stonemason, who was seen by Sir Walter Scott in 1796, on one of his journeys, repairing the lettering on the stone. This man became the original of "Old Mortality." During the first Week of our stay ultimatums were flying about almost as plentiful as picture postcards. By the middle of the week six European nations were at war. , Stonehaven faces the North Sea, the sea upon which great naval conflicts were daily expected. All ves- sels were ordered into port, almost daily we sighted cruisers and gun-boats hastening north or south. We often gazed eastward over the stretch of waters and wondered what was hap- pening beyoad our sight. As time went on permission was given for vessels to sail at their own risk, and the fishing boats were allowed to go cut on the understanding that they were in port by dusk, and every evening the trawlers might be seen steaming homewards with their day's catch. The little fishing town was not behind in its preparations to receive the wounded should they be brought in from the anticipated naval engagement. The Town Hall was turned into a hospital, furnished with the necessary beds, and a trained nursing staff was ready, but, like the tribe of Asher in days of old, the Germans "remained in their breaches," hence the hospital has not so far been needed. We saw the local Territorials set out for. camp; their dress included Glengarry cap, khaki jacket, and green tartan kilts. The Provost, deuce man, addressing them, said that the eyes of Stonehaven, of England, and of the Empire were upon them, that he was sure they would play the man and bring glory to the country. Three cheers were given for the Mayor,. and for the King, and off they marched to their training ground, to the inspiring, if not very musical, strains of the bagpipes. Tleen there was a church ',parade of the Army Reserves. I think there were about 60 of these, amongst them old men who could scarcely totter, but who, if they could not go to the front, were Proud to take their place in the church parade. Then there were the children, quick to imitate their elders, who, with old sacks and sticks, had made tents, forming an encampment. They had their sentry posted, who marched to and fro and challenged all who came that way. What matter that he had neither shoes nor stockings? He marched as proudly and wheeled as scientifically as the sentry guarding the camp of the Territorials. Then we had a Cycle Corps, 36 of whose members were quar- tered in our hotel. Then the hotel 'bus horse and man were commandeered, and we thought we would have to borrow barrows and each wheel his box to the station. But a neigh- bouring hotel came to our aid and our party got safely away.</p>
        <p>Coast Dependent Upon Fisheries.</p>
        <p>What struck me most in this town and dis- trict was the dependence of all these coast towns and villages on the harvest of the sea. The herring fishing was at its height. The chief marketfor _salted herring is Germany. We send annually to Germany 200,000 tons, valued at about two and a quarter million pounds. The North of Scotland is the chief source of supply. v The cutting off of this mar= ket and the danger of fishing brought this industry to a sudden stop, and all the curing establishments shut down. Possibly the fish- ing may be resumed, but it is a season industry and the season will soon be passed. It will mean considerable privation, not alone to the fishers themselves, but to the curing and all subordinate industries connected with it. Altogether the harvest of the sea is valued at over £6,000,000, of which the share of England is about £4,000,000, Scotland £1,500,000, and Ireland £600,000. This, however, is the value on the quay. Probably the value as retailed is twice this amount.</p>
        <p>Methods of Fishing. Of the various methods of fishing. trawling is the most important. The trawl is a conical bag of netting dragged along the bottom of the sea. The trawler while fishing moves slowly from two to six miles an hour, dragging the net astern at the bottom of the sea. The modern steam trawler has a net tonnage of from one to two hundred tons, and an engine of eighty horse- power and u,.wkirds, and costs from ,t 6,000 to ~4.1.‘1.10 to build. The duration of a haul is about hours. The net is hauled in, cleared, and again shot. Trawling is posethle at a depth of a&lt; much as two hundred fathoms. Another method of fishing is with a drift net, which catches fish swimming on or near the sur- face. Both steamers and sailers are used. The drift net is supported on a line to which cork floats are attached. The size of the net varies according to the character of the fish sought. The fish strikes the net, its head slips through and its increaking diameter fixes it. There is also a good deal of mixed fishing along the shore with fixed nets. The nets used are something like a well-known form of mouse-trap, a net within a net. The mouth of the net gapes wide and the inside net gradually narrows both at the top and bottom as well as the sides, until there is only a small aperture. The fish follow- ing along this net enter through this aperture and find themselves in the second net and unless they are lucky enough to find again the small opening they are effectually trapped. This class Of net is also used in the river estuaries for catching salmon. Another method of fishing, which is now fall- ing off, is that of a long line or rope, in some eases as much as six miles in length, furnished with baited hooks. The hooks are hung upon pieces of line attached to the main line. The line is usually shot in the evening and taken up in the morning, its position at sea being marked with buoys.</p>
        <p>Natural Food Supply. Attention at the present time ought to be given to the harvest of the sea; the supply is drawn from international waters, and the, supply is constantly replenished by ordinary national laws. The consumption of fish involves no deple- tion of our national food supply. The harvest reaped requires no sowing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>DOES YOUR SCALP ITCH AND</head>
        <p>DOES YOUR SCALP ITCH AND</p>
        <p>HAIR FALL OUT?</p>
        <p>It is so easy to get rid of dandruff with Cuti- cura Soap shampoos and to allay itching and irritation of the scalp with light touches of Cuticura Ointment, that it is a duty to use them in order to promote and maintain natural hair-growth conditions. Treatment: On retiring, comb the hair out straight all around, then begin at the side and make a parting, gently rubbing Cuticura Oint- ment into the parting with a hit of soft flannel held over the end of the finger. Anoint addi- tional partings about half an inch apart until the whole scalp has been treated, the purpose being to get the Cuticura Ointment on the scalp skin rather than on the hair. It is well to place a light. covering over the hair to protect the pillow from possible stain. The next morning; shampoo with Cuticura Soap and hot water.</p>
        <p>Shampoos alone inay be used as often as agreeable, but once or twice a month is gener- ally sufficient for this special treatment for women's hair. Cuticura Soaps and Ointment are sold by chemists throughout the world. Skin book will be sent post free on request. Address post card to F. Newbery and Sons, 27, Charterhouse- square, London, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>AN AWFUL INDICTMENT.</head>
        <p>AN AWFUL INDICTMENT.</p>
        <p>Belgian Minister's Harrowing Details.</p>
        <p>FULLY•AUTIIENTICATED.</p>
        <p>The Press Bureau has communicated a detailed statement by the Belgian Minister concerning German Violations of the customs of civilised warfare. We take the following extracts from this document :</p>
        <p>When German troops invaded our country the Belgian Government issued public state- ments which were placarded in every town, v,illage, and hamlet, warning all civilians to abstain scrupulously from hostile acts against the enemy's troops. Nevertheless the German authorities have issued lately statements con- taining grave imputations against the attitude of the Belgian civilian population, threatening us at the same time with dire reprisals. These imputations are contrary to the real facts of the case, and as to threats of further vengeance no menace of odious reprisals on the part of the German troops will deter the Belgian Govern- ment from protesting before the civilised world against the fearful and atrocious (Tittles com- mitted wilfully and deliberately by the invad- ing hosts against helpless non-combatants, old men, women, and children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>APPALLING LIST OF OUTRAGES.</head>
        <p>APPALLING LIST OF OUTRAGES.</p>
        <p>Long is the list of outrages committed by the German troops and appalling the details of atrocities, as vouched for by the Committee of Inquiry recently formed by the Belgian Minis- ter of Justice and presided over by him. This committee comprises the highest judicial and university authorities of Belgium, such as Chief Justice Van Iseghem, Judge Nys, Professors Cottier, Wodon, &amp;c. The following instances and particulars have been established by careful investigations based in each case on the evidence of reliable eye- witnesses : German cavalry occupying the village of Linsmeau were attacked by some Belgian Infantry and two gendarmes. No one of the civilian population took part in the fighting. Nevertheless, the village was invaded at dusk on August 10th, and two farms and six outlying houses were destroyed by gun fire awl burnt. All the male inhabitants were then compelled to come forward and hand over whAtever arms they possessed. No recently discharged fire- arms were found. The invaders divided these Peasants into three groups, those in one group were bound, and 11 of them placed in a ditch where they were afterwards found dead, their skulls fractured by the butts of German rifles. Farmer Jef Dierickx, of Neerhespen, bears witness to the following acts of cruelty com- mitted by German cavalry at Orsmael and Neerhespen on August 10th, 11th and ‘l2th</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>TOO HORRIBLE TO DESCRIBE.</head>
        <p>TOO HORRIBLE TO DESCRIBE.</p>
        <p>An old man of the latter village had his arm sliced in three longitudinal cuts; he was then hanged head downwards and burned alive. Young gi4s have been raped and little children outraged at Orsmael, where several inhabitants suffered mutilations too horrible to describe. A Belgian soldier 4aelanging to a battalion of cyclist carabiniers, who had been wounded and made prisoner, was hanged, whilst another, who was tending his comrade, was bound to a tele- graph pole on the St. Trond-road and shot. On Augnst 19th, after a Belgian resistance on the previous day, German troops entered Aerschot without a shot having been fired from the town and without any resistance whatever having been made. The few inhabitants that remained had closed their doors and windows in compliance with the general orders issued by the Belgian Government. Nevertheless, the Germans broke into the houses and told the inhabitants to quit. In one single street the first six male inhabi- tants who crossed their thresholds were seized and shot at once, under the very eyes of their wives and children.</p>
        <p>The -German troops then retired for the day, only'to return in greater numbers the next day. They then compelled the inhabitants to leave their houses and marched them to a place 200 yards from the town. There, without more ado, they shot M. Thielemans, the Burgomaster, his 15-year-old son. the clerk of the local Judi- cial Board, and 10 prominent citizens. They then set fire to the town and destroyed it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WOMEN USED AS A SCREEN.</head>
        <p>WOMEN USED AS A SCREEN.</p>
        <p>The following statement was made by Com- mandant Georges Gilson, of the 9th Infantry of the Line, now lying in hospital at Antwerp :</p>
        <p>"I was told to cover the retreat of our troops in front of Aerschot. During the action fought there on Wednesday. August 19th, between six and eight o'clock in the morning, suddenly I saw on the high xoacl, between the German</p>
        <p>and Belgian forces, which were fighting at close range, a group of four women, with babies in their arms, and two little girls clinging to their skirts. Our men stopped firing till the women got through our lines, but the German machine- guns went on firing all the time, and one of the women was wounded in the arm. These women could not have got through the neighbouring German lines and been on the high road unless with the consent of the enemy.</p>
        <p>"All the evidence and circumstances seem to point to the fact that those women had been deliberately pushed forward by the Germans to act as a shield for their advance guard, and in the hope that the Belgians would cease firing for fear of killing the women and children." This statement was made and duly certified in the Antwerp hospital on August 22nd by Commandant Gilson, in the presence of the Chevalier Ernst N. Bunswyck, Chief Secretary to the, Belgian Minister of Justice, and M. de Cartier de Marchienne, Belgian Minister to China.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>TERROR AS REARGUARD.</head>
        <p>TERROR AS REARGUARD.</p>
        <p>In publishing-ilie—zi-boye statements the only comment the Press Bureau can offer is that these atrocities 'appear to be committed in villages and throughout the countryside with the deliberate intention of terrorising the people and so making it unnecessary to leave troops in occupation of small places or to protect lines of communication. In large places like Brus- sels, where the diplomatic representatives of neutral Powers are eye-witnesses, there appear to have been no excesses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ENGLISHMAN'S FATE. Shot by Germans in a Belgian Village. SCOTSWOMAN TELLS OF GERM ‘N BRUTALITY.</head>
        <p>ENGLISHMAN'S FATE. Shot by Germans in a Belgian Village. SCOTSWOMAN TELLS OF GERM ‘N BRUTALITY.</p>
        <p>The following thrilling and circumstantial account of the escape of an English lady and her children from Germany, and of the murder of an English gentleman and the burning of a Belgian village by German troops, has been furnished to us, says "The Times" of last Satur- day, by Mr. George Bonar, of the firm of Low and Bonar (Limited), of Dundee, and 53, New Broad-street, E.C. Mr. Boner reached London with his wife and children on Thursday night. He says:— , In July I left my wife, with two little boys aged 9 and 7, and a nurse, at Bad Ems, and went to Norway for a fortnight's fishing. When war was declared I could not get back to them in time. The hotel people advised my wife not to run away, but to wait until the mobilisation was over, and the railways free again. On the after- noon of Monday, August 3, things got so bad that she decided to leave. With the children and nurse she left Ems by the 4 p.m. train, which she was assured would take her through 'to Brussels. At Herberthal, the last town in Germany, they were peremptorily ordered to get out, as there was no longer a connection with Belgium. The only thing for them to do was to walk over the frontier to the nearest Belgian town, which was Wilkenraad. It was a miser- able night, and rain fell heavily. At Wilkenraad hundreds of people of all nationalities were walking about the streets in the pouring rain unable to get accommodation. A woman took pity on the plight of my children ' and put them up for the night. My wife had no money, but gave her some jewellery. It was thought that if they could get to Verviers they would be able to reach Ostend. A cart was ob- tained and a hood was put over it to protect them from the rain. The party was composed of my wife and two children, the nurse, three American ladies, and an English gentleman named Mackenzie, aged 78. Mr. Mackenzie was a man of wide culture, and I believe was well kiiown in London, but I have not yet been able to trace his relatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Stopped by the Germans.</head>
        <p>Stopped by the Germans.</p>
        <p>On the road from the German town of Eupen they met the first German troops marching on Belgium. They were stopped, and the waggon was commandeered. The whole party walked to the little village of Baelen-Dolhain, where a Belgian Custom-house officer, M. Michel Blaise, gave them shelter. On Tuesday, August 4, the three Americans and two young men from the Chilean Legation in Berlin, realising the impos- sibility of making their way through Belgium, decided to return to Germany. My wife could not move because, owing to exposure, the elder boy had developed a severe attack of bronchitis; and Mr. Mackenzie was too old and lame to undertake a long ,walk. On the night of Sunday, August 9, firing was heard in the village. My wife took fright and with the little boy who was ill, the other, and</p>
        <p>the nurse, went down into the cellar. • -Mr. Mackenzie and M. Blaise accompanied them. As they ran downstairs my wife noticed that a similar house on one side of them was ablaze. Almost immediately after they had reached the cellar they heard firing through all the windows overhead. After some time the doors and win- dows were broken in and they were hoirified to find that the soldiers were setting fire to the house.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>An Appeal to the Officer.</head>
        <p>An Appeal to the Officer.</p>
        <p>With one of the boys in her arms and followed by the other, my wife ran into the street in front of the troops, who were facing the house. "Are we to be shot?" she cried, speaking in Ger- man, and for answer she was ordered to stand on one side. They were followed by M. and Mme. Blaise. The minnent M. Blaise appeared he was shot down and killed. Three bullets entered his body. Mr. Mackenzie was then seen coming along the passage. My wife ran up to the officer in charge of the troops, who was on horseback, and clutching him by the leg, cried, "For God's sake, don't shoot that man. He is an English- man merely taking refuge here." " Das macht nichts ans " (that does not matter), the officer replied. He gave the order to shoot, and Mr. Mackenzie fell, with a bullet in his chest. One shot killed him. My wife is perfectly certain the officer heard what she had said.</p>
        <p>The ground floor of the house was by this time burning fierce. My children were standing in the street in pyjamas and with bare feet, and my wife asked permission to go into the house ' to rescue some clothes for them. At first the officer refused; but at last he consented and ' allowed two soldiers to go with her. Three times my wife entered the burning house and reached the first floor, and threw down clothing to the soldiers. Her hair, eyelids, and eye- brows were singed, and my children are now wearing scorched clothing. My wife afterwards searched Mr. Mackenzie's body and took about 100 marks and his watch, which she gave to the poor Belgian woman who had lost both home and husband. She retained his rings, which she has brought home to give to his relatives when they can be found. She also took some papers from his pocket. Mr. Mackenzie had told him that he had valuable papers from the Japanese Embassy.</p>
        <p>Bread and Water from the Soldiers.</p>
        <p>The soldiers in the street were very kind to her and gave her bread from their rations and water from their bottles. She saw two of them at the corner of the street weeping at what was taking place. All the soldiers she talked to were under the impression that the British and French were already in Belgium, and that they were fighting them. My wife decided to return to Germany. But she feared that she might be searched on the frontier, and that if Mr. Mackenzie's documents, which were probably of a diplomatic nature, were found upon her, she might have been taken as a spy. So she threw the documents in a ditch before reaching the frontier. She managed to reach Eupen, and there got by electric tramway to Aachen. She decided to return to Ems, where she was known, but could not get further than Cologne, where she was for eight days before I was able to discover her. On her return to Ger- many she was extremely well treated. She had no money. One poor German woman who saw her obviously distressed condition gave her 10 marks, which she could ill spare. My wife had a necklace which cost £3OO, and she was offered only 100 marks for it. The proprietor of the Hotel Monopole at Cologne advised her not to sell it, and kindly offered to trust her until the war was over. The nearest I could get to Ger- many was Diest. I telegraphed in all direc- tions without result. But my wife, who was born in Denmark, telegraphed to Copenhagen, and the message was sent to me at Dundee and thence to Diest. I got a Dutch gentleman. to go into Cologne to bring out my wife and children and the nurse.</p>
        <p>direc-tisayi</p>
        <p>British Subjects in Germany—A Suggestion. I feel under an obligation, Mr. Bonar added, to do -something for the numerous other British subjects stranded in Germany. The men are not now allowed to leave, but I am convinced the German Government would not keep the ,vomen and children if the money were found to bring them out. The bulk of these womeh can- not speak German, and the feeling is getting very bitter against English people. The British Legation at The Hague is doing all it can; but it is limited by the efforts of the American Em- bassy and Consulates in Germany, whose hands are full with the colossal number of Americans still in Germany. The subjects of some neutral nations should be sent in to bring the English out. The most suitable nation is the Dutch. The Germans are doing all they can to keep on friendly terms with Holland, from whom they</p>
        <p>FRENCH PROTEST.</p>
        <p>Prisoners .Sliot and Dunrdurn Bullets Used.</p>
        <p>The French. Government have brouiht, to the notice of the Signatory Powers to The. Hague Conventions several instances in which the provisioris of the Conventions have been con- travened by Germany.</p>
        <p>_ German troops are accused of putting wounded soldiers to death and bombarding the open town of Pont-a-Mousson, in French Lor- raine. A captured German officer has been detected carrying in his cartridge-belt dum- dum bullets. Gperal Joffre has ordered his trial by court-martial, so that the French authorities may "be able to send to the Ger- man Emperor the formal verdict by which the German officer will be condemned to be shot." Dum-dum bullets have an expanding head, and are meant to inflict exaggerated wounds. Their use in warfare between civilised nations is forbidden by international law.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>APPEAL -.TO TIIE CIVILISED</head>
        <p>APPEAL -.TO TIIE CIVILISED</p>
        <p>WORLD.</p>
        <p>Mr. 1). M. Mason (Coventry, Min.) asked the Prime Minister in the House of Commons whether the statement of German atrocities, issued through the British Press Bureau, and drawn up by a Belgian committee of inquiry, was true, and if so, what action his Majesty's Government proposed to take to protest against so flagrant a violation of the rules of civilised warfare.</p>
        <p>Mr. Asquith : The statements referred to are , the result of an inquiry by a 'committee consti- tuted and presided over by the Belgian Minister of Justice, and composed of the highest judicial and university authorities of Belgium. They have been officially communicated to his Majesty's Government by the Belgian Minister at this Court. His Majesty's Government under- stand that the Belgian Government are taking all the necessary steps to bring the facts estab- lished by their official court to the knowledge of the civilised World. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ZEPPELIN OUTRAGES. Strong Condemnation in the United Stales.</head>
        <p>ZEPPELIN OUTRAGES. Strong Condemnation in the United Stales.</p>
        <p>New Yon, Thursday. The cabled accounts of the killing of harm- less civilians at Antwerp by bombs dropped from airships have caused \universal indigna- tion throughout the United States, and, follow- ing upon the formal protest lodged yesterday at Washington by the Belgian Minister, are certain to have a profound efiect upon American public feeling. „</p>
        <p>The "Sun," commenting on the Zeppelin outrage, says: "Every nation which still be- lieves that humanity should be maintained in the usage of warfare should raise its voice against this arch-deed of pitiless savagery, and against the repetition of such a senseless, un- forgivable, blind massacre."—Central News.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>AUSTRIIN OUTRAGES. Reprisals Threatened.</head>
        <p>AUSTRIIN OUTRAGES. Reprisals Threatened.</p>
        <p>The Exchange Telegraph -CompanY is in- • formed that M. Pashitch, the Servian Minister for „Foreign Affairs, has addressed a Note to the Spanish Minister at Bukarest requesting him to inform the Austrian Government that the atrocities which the Austrian Army while re- treating has committed by the express orders of the commanders upon old Men, women and children are a most obnoxious infringement of the laws of war. The Spanish Minister is further requested to state that the Servians will be constrained to use all the possible measures and reprisals within the limits of the laws of modern warfare.</p>
        <p>A protest has been lodged with the friendly Powers against the bombardment by Austria of undefended and unfortified cities, such as Belgrade, Shabatz, Gradishte, and other cities and villages situated on the bordering rivers of the Save and the Danube.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>GERMANY'S DEFENCE TO OUTRAGE CHARGES. "Examples" Necessary.</head>
        <p>GERMANY'S DEFENCE TO OUT- RAGE CHARGES. "Examples" Necessary.</p>
        <p>Thursday's official wireless message from Ber- lin contained the following reply to the charges made by the Belgian Government against the Germans of outrages upon non-combatants : "The distribution of arms and ammunition among the civil population of Belgium had been carried out on systematic lines, and the authori- ties enraged the public against Germany by assiduously circulating false reports. They were under the impression that with the aid of the French they would be able to drive the Ger- marts out of Belgium in two days. "The only means of preventing surprise attacks from the civil population has been to interfere with unrelenting severity and to create examples which by their ` frightfulness ' would be a warning to the whole country'. "The increased war contribution levied on the province of Liege has also had an excellent effect."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>RUNCORN GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Fees of Vaccination Officer.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun.,. presiding. The minutes of the Children's Home Commit- tee contained a vote of thanks to the children of the Home for their contribution to the Prince of Wales' Fund. It was dated that the chil- dren were anxious to contribute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>VACCINATION OFFICERS' FEES.</head>
        <p>VACCINATION OFFICERS' FEES.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Local Government Board approving of the payments to vaccina- tion officers. The communication continued that as regarded future remuneration the Board would be willing to consider a proposal from the Guardians to pay fixed salaries per annum to these officers instead of fees. The matter was referred to the Finance Com- mittee.</p>
