        <p>The Very Be3t at the Loweat Rates.</p>
        <p>see that the call upon their patriotism is urgent and must be urgently obeyed. As to the progress of the war, there has not been over-much during the last week or ten days to Indicate that the Allies had the measure of their opponents. The offensive movement against the Germans in Belgium, made with the object of shortening the war, failed, and we have been thrown upon the defensive in France. But it is a defence that will leave its permanent mark upon the aggressors. Some of that deterioration in the ,ranks of the enemy, upon which LORD KITCHENER counts, has already taken place. Be- sides, while the Germans are moving westward towards Paris the Russians are moving westward towards Berlin, a grim race that may, sooner than the GERMAN EMPEROR allowed for, necessi- tate the return of a portion of the Army that is now concentrating its attention upon France. We have another powerful ally. Our opponents stand before the World convicted not only of waging unneces- sary war and shedding blood wantonly, but of committing outrages that even 'war, hideous as it is, cannot countenance. The murder of inno— cent villagers and of English visitors, the torturing of old men, and the out- raging of women, are some of the mildest of the atrocities that have been committed in the last month of this twentieth century. We, like other English papers, have abstained from heeding the tales of th(s.e outrages ; we can do so no longer when the charges are formulate) by Belgian judges and statesmen for the consider- ation of civilisation. We publish in anot'her column authenticated accounts of acts of barbarism, com- mitted in the name of the arch-villain in this world-wide,, tragedy that would make the Bashi BazOuk appear in the light of an amateur in the work of murkier and rapine. It is indeed strange that at the moment we were congratulating ourselves that Eu rope had got rid of the unspeakable Turk, his place should be taken by the un- thinkable German. The Turk had the decency to try to hide his misdoings and to deny them, but the German has had no such scruples. On the con- trary, he has sent out a justification, and holds that it was not only neces- sary to commit these deeds, but that their " frightfulness " had to be such that the examples would be a warning to the whole country, We are glad to think the GERMAN EMPEROR has divested himself of the British titles he held ; the uniforms that go with our titles are made for better stuff.</p>
      </div>
