      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>BIRTHS. MARRIAGES. DEATHS.</head>
        <p>BIRTHS. MARRIAGES. DEATHS.</p>
        <p>• Deaths. B AYLEY.—Cissie, the beloved wife of George Bayley, of Fairfield-road, Stockton Heath, at Black pO2l, on Wednesday, August 25th.</p>
        <p>' .4</p>
        <p>_ In Memoriam. BIRCHALL.—In ever-loving memory of Nellie, who f'2ll asleep August 30th, 1912. and little Tom, who died September 4th, 1913. Loved in life, not forgotten in death by Her loving TOM. HILL.—In loving memory of a dear- wife and tuot:ier, who passed away on 27th August, 1913. God took her away, it was His will, But in our hearts she liveth still. HUSBAND and FAMILY.</p>
        <p>-• Acknowledgment. -174/3" R. BAYLEY desires to THANK all friends IVA. for the kindness and sympathy extended towards him during his recent sad bereavement.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>THREE PHASES OF THE WAR. There are three natural divisions into which our thoughts upon the war divide themselves. First and fore- most, we have determined to go on to the end, however distant that end may be, or however difficult the road we must traverse in gaining that end. We have no alternative. Unless we and the cause for which we have drawn .the sword triumph, the German will be paramount, and we now know What that would mean. Relentless, ruthless cruelty ,is the instrument Which our foe is brazen-faced enough, even in these early days, to press into his service to attain his unsavoury purpose. We embarked on a war we did not want to maintain the liberties of Small nations and to safeguard our own position, and whatever the price we pay we must make the sacrifice in the assured knowledge that the price of failure would be infinitely worse: Fortunately we have a manat or histhfehead Of affairs who is able to speak country, and knows how to do so. LORD KITCHENER, providentially called to the War Office, has asked for 100,000 i men and has got them. He now asks ' for more, and he will get them. The message he sends to the world is that we have the reserves that will wear (town our enemies, who have put their full strength forth now and must diminish while we improve. He has ' shown us, in half a dozen lines, how • we can, must, and will win. The 1 • speech has heartened England, encour- , . aged the Empire, and re-assured our Allies. "England will persevere to , . the end" is the refrain of the leading , articles of the French papers. We iL. shall' get the men; but they ought to</p>
        <p>JJIiOUNFIELD &amp; SONS carry out Funerals • in tow, and country. Every equipment. Office: 15. Church-street. Tot. 95.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 29, 1914.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>c..</p>
        <p>i</p>
        <p>( • . ; i.‘ i' , t. (0, , ; , V</p>
