      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>13012 FUNERAL NANISHING</head>
        <p>13012 FUNERAL NANISHING</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. AUGUST 29, 1914.</p>
        <p>THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>HELP FOR BELGIUM!</p>
        <p>The beautiful towns, the happy villages, and the smiling countryside of Belgium, so well known and so largely enjoyed in happier days by thousands of Englishmen and English- women, have been sadly outragd byk the Ger- man soldiery. Belgians, old and young, rich and poor, strong and infirm, have been griev- ously afflicted by the common enemy. There will, in God's own time, come an adequate, if terrible, rvenge. But our immediate concern is not for the fate of the traitor to civilisation; it is for the relief of the sufferings of our friends whose heroic and gloriously successful stand against the oppressor has left them torn and bleeding and dispossessed not onfy of all they held dear, but, in many cases, of the bare means of livelihood. We have just felt the outside tremors of the earthquake that has temporarily shattered a large portion of Belgium. Our banks closed for a day or two, our works went on short time, and the price of food went up, but -came down again when the first panic sub- sided. In Belgium, banks, works, food and homes 'Ave been wiped out of existence alto- gether ; Where thecrash has cost ps a few coppers, the price in Belgium has been extracted by fire and sword. Can we.who have suffered so little do something for those who have suffered so much? Those of us who can will find an appeal in another column, which gives particulars of how that something may be quickly and safely —because officially—done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS IN WAR TIME.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS IN WAR TIME.</p>
        <p>During the early part of the week much was heard oi a suggestion that this year there should be no municipal elections, the old mem- bers being allowed an additional year of office because of the delicate state of affairs at the present time. When everybody is trying to convince himself and others that business is going on as usual it was rather trying to be told, in effect, that we are not equal to the'ex- citement of a NoVember election. The idea was effectually undermined on Thursday when, in the House of Commons, Mr. Herbert Samuel said the Government had considered the ques- tion of introducing a Bill for the postponement of this year's municipal elections, and had de- cided that there was not sufficient reason for such legislation. Local arrangements could„.be made by agreement for the avoidance of party contests, and the House would no doubt agree that under the present circumstances such ar- rangements should wherever possible be made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>AN ACCOMMODATION.</head>
        <p>AN ACCOMMODATION.</p>
        <p>_ The avoidance of party strife over seats on local councils is far different from upsetting all routine and custom by missing the elections altogether. Last week we drew attention to the fact that the Labour Party had decided to attack three unstated wards. Perhaps, in view of the recommendation of the Government and in fur- therance of the general desire that there shall be all-round good-will until we have slain the enemy at the gate, the Labour men will delay their attack, which ras so long lain dormant, for one more year. A national " walk-over " in November would be a hint to Germany as to the mood we were in.</p>
        <p>EVENING CLASSES FOR ADULTS The evening schools and institutes of the Education Committee will open for the winter on dates commencing September 7, and to the old scheme, which has been brought to a state approximating to perfection, there will be added schools for men and women. Only students over 21 years of age will be admitted to these schools, which are designed to meet special cir- cumstances. The classes for women, to be held at Evelyn-street, Bolton and Beamont Schools, will offer instruction in dressmaking, cookery and millinery. The school for men, whfch will i be at Evelyn-street, and has been started in response to a request, will be for adults desirous of improving themselves in elementary reading, writing and arithmetic. These are innovations well worth trying, and we hope they will meet with success. Other classes, fees, scholarships, Fcc., will be upon the lines that have been in force for some ;ears, and have been found to answer the needs of the town. Full prospec- tuses may be had from the Director of Educa- tion.</p>
        <p>OUR MUSEENI AND LIBRARY. Mr. Madeley's annual report, which always makes interesting reading, has been published for the year ended June 30th. The twelve months saw the addition of 2,790 specimens to the various collections in the Museum, 1,689 being by purchase and 1,100 by gift. In addi- tion more than 3,000 wild flower specimens were received for temporary exhibition. That the Museum is increasingly appreciated is proved by the fact that over 50,000 visitors inspected its collections. In the Library de- partment 2,147 new books were added, and, after allowing for withdrawals, our stock of books numbered 58,318, against 56,894 in the previous year. There were 91,030 issues, and the •average number of books "out" was 2,351, oVer 71 per cent. of them being fiction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>SOLE REASON-AND TIIE BEST!</head>
        <p>THE SOLE REASON-AND TIIE BEST!</p>
        <p>One of the Territorials, Mr. Robert F. Ander- ton, Of the L. and N.-W. Railway, now on duty with the Warrington Battalion, writing to the Musical Society Opera Committee resigning , because he has volunteered for foreign service; says:-- Of course. I am disappointed at not being able to take part in "Rip," but then eine must give up some pleasures at a time like the present. I wish you all every success in the forthcoming production, and if every- thing goes well I hope to have the pleasure of renewing my acquaintance with the opera next season. No doubt you wonder what caused me to join the Territorials. Well, the sole reason is that I consider every able-bodied young man like myself should do something for his country in such a crisis, and I sincerely trust I shall be able to dizy my share.</p>
        <p>That is the British spirit that will, in the long run, beat the Kaiser, with his millions of con- scripts, to his knees.</p>
        <p>TEACHERS WITH THE COLOURS.</p>
        <p>Elementary school teachers who have volun- teered for foreign service with the Territorials, and have gone into camp with that really formidable force, number upwards of 1,000. To this number Warrington has given its share. Teachers will be glad to know that the Govern- ment have taken steps to prevent them losing financially by their patriotism. This week a Bill was introduced by Mr. J. A. Pease (Presi- dent of the Board of Education) to enable cer- tificated teachers to reckon services in connec- tion with the present war as seconded services for the purposes of the Acts relating to elemen- tary teachers' superannuation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>"THE MIRSEILLAISE."</head>
        <p>"THE MIRSEILLAISE."</p>
        <p>Last week we gave the legends which sur- round the origin and history of our own National Anthem, and it may be equally inter- esting if we now refer to the Story of the National Anthem of our allies, the brave French. No such uncertainty of authorship applies tO the " Marseillaise " as applies to "God save the King." "The Marseillaise," words and music, were the product of one brain, inspired by the atmosphere of the Revolution—words and music being poured forth in a single flood of enthusiasm. The author and composer,. of this popular</p>
        <p>_ French hymn is onq Claude Joseph Rpuget de Lisle, a young captain of ngineers quar- tered at Strasburg in 1792, when the Volunteers of the Bas Rhin received orders to join Luck- ner's army. Dietrich, the Mayor of Strasburg, having expressed his regret that the French as a nation had rib patriotic song to sing as they marched out, de Lisle, with whom Dietrich was on terms of intimate friendship, appears to have been fired with an ambition to repair the defect. Returning to his lodgings, he in a fit of enthu- siasm composed during the night of April 24th, 1792, the words and music of a martial song which has immortalised his name. Alas! that</p>
        <p>fir was also destined to be a hymn of terror to many, including de Lisle's good friend; Dietrich himself ! De Lisle was popular with his comrades as a song-writer, though none of his works, except "The Marseillaise," have survived him_ Lamartine tells us that •Dietrich's daughters appear to have had a great opinion of de Lisle's ability, for they suggested that he should write a song that would "excite enthusiasm in the bosoms of the people," and the father produced the last bottle of wine from his cellar to complete the fire his daughters' promptings had begun. "It was Midnight and very cold," says Lamartine ; "do Lisle was a dreamer; his heart was moved, his head was heated. The cold seized on him and he went staggering home to his lonely chamber endeavouring by degrees to , find ins iration in the palpitations of his citizen heart ; and on his small clavicord now composing the air before the words and now the words before. the air, combined them so intimately in his mind that he could never tell which was first produced, the air or the words, so impossible did he find it to separate the poetry from the music and the feeling from the impression. He sang everything, wrote noth- ing. Overcorhe by the effort his head fell sleep- ing on his instrument, and he did not awake until daylight. The song of the overnight re- turned to his memory with difficulty, like the recollections of a dream. He wrote it down and then ran to Dietrich." _ _</p>
        <p>When the "Chant de Guere," as "The Mar- seillaise" was at first called, was tried over at Dietrich's house on the morning of April 25, to the pianoforte accompaniment played by his eldest daughter, the emotion of those assembled to hear it was such that all countenances are Said to have turned pale at the first verse, tears to have flowed at the second, while great 'en- thusiasm, we are told, marked its conclusion. The hymn of the country was found! The following day the music was arranged for a military band, and, the parts having been copied out, it was performed by the Band of the Garde Nationale at a review on Sunday, April 29. Later the song was called "Chant des Marseillaise," and, subsequently, and with every prospect of finality, "La Marseillaise." The new song spread from city to city, Mar- seilles adopted it and the Marseillais spread it all over the country, singing it on their way, and so giving it its name. De Lisle's old mother, a royalist and religious, wrote to her son, "What is this revolutionary song sung by bands 'of brigands who are traversing France, and with which our name is mingled?" The Revolu- tion, becoming insane, did not recognise its own voice. Dietrich, a few months later, went to the scaffold 'to the sound of the notes produced at his own fireside from the heart of his friend and the voices of his daughters. De Lisle met the same end. It has been denied over and over again that Rouget de Lisle composed the music of "The Marseillaise," and it is strange to say, in this connection, that Castil-Blaze, who should have recognised the vigour and dash so characteris- tic of the French, declared it to have been taken from a German hymn. But this and many other lines of doubt have long since been care- fully and rigorously traversed, only to estab- lish firmly and for all time the authenticity of France's National Anthem in the way already stated. "The Marseillaise" has often been made use of by composers. Salieri, in the opening chorus of his opera " Palmira " (1795), and Grison, in the introduction to the oratorio "Esther "—a work still, we believe, in MS.—have both laid the tune under tribute in quite a serious and intentional way. Schumann has also made good and effective use of it, while most recently, and under conditions which give the air a -grandeur and a sublimity which- never fails to touch the most phlegmatic listener, we have the instance of Tschaikowsky's incorporation of this stirring hymn tune in his world-renowned " 1812 overture."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>NAVAL VICTORY.</head>
        <p>A NAVAL VICTORY.</p>
        <p>GERMAN CRUISERS AND DESTROYERS SUNK.</p>
        <p>The Press Bureau last night reported a sub- stantial naval victory for the British Fleet in the North Sea.</p>
        <p>Early yesterday morning our Fleet made con- certed operation against German Fleet in Heli- goland Bight, intercepting and attacking Ger- man destroyers and cruisers. We sank two destroyers and damaged many others. Our light cruiser squadron sank the German cruiser Maingh.</p>
        <p>- Our Ist Battle Cruiser Squadron sank an- other German cruiser; and a third German cruiser disappeared in the mist all aflame and in a sinking condition. All the German cruisers, said to number five, were disposed of. Very little damage was done to the British vessels and we suffered no loss of life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>LYMNI RELIEF FUND.</head>
        <p>LYMNI RELIEF FUND.</p>
        <p>Last night it was reported that remarkable success was attending the Lymm War Relief Fund, and it was estimated that up to the ' middle of the week over .NOO had been received. The following have been elected on the com- mittee: Mr. A. Natkin, chairman; Mr. T. F. W. Dixon, Clifilands, treasurer; Mr. W. Mullard and Mr. 11. T. Mercer, hon. joint secretaries. The chief subscribers are: .2100 each, Mr. A. Watkin, Mr. John Coxon, Vice-Chancellor Stewart Smith; £5O, Mr. T. J. Ridgway; £2O, Mr. W. N. Moulsworth ; ..tlO, Mrs. Stewart Smith. Church collections—Higher-lane Bap- tist, £3 3s. 4d.; Parish Church, .L6O 19s. 7d.; Statham Mission, 35.; St. Peter's, Ough- trington, a 6s. id.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>TIIE WAR OFFICE ACcEPTS THE</head>
        <p>TIIE WAR OFFICE ACcEPTS THE</p>
        <p>INFIRMARY ‘ArARD.</p>
        <p>I. Last night we were informed by Mr. J. H. Smethurst that at a meeting of the Board of Management of the Warrington Infirmary, held yesterday- afternoon, a letter was read from Lord Derby stating that the offer of the Board to give the use of ft ward of their institution for treatment of wounded soldiers had been accepted by the Government, but the date when the ward would come into use would be decided later.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>SOUTH LANGASHIRES AT MOMS.</head>
        <p>SOUTH LANGASHIRES AT MOMS.</p>
        <p>Gallant Bayonet Charge. GERMANS DIDN'T WANT MORE.</p>
        <p>The South Lancashires, whose headquarters are at Warrington, have not been long before they started making history, and the Battle of Mons will now have to be added to the long list of great battles in which this famous regi- ment have figured. . Mr. h. Hamilton Fyfe, the special war corre- spondent of the " Daily Mail," writing from Rouen on Wednesday about one of the best ' charges of the Mons battle, quotes a soldier to have said: "The South Lancashires made it (the charge). The Germans don't like that sort of fighting. They won't come out, or they wouldn't come, at any rate, to meet us. Once we got within seeing distance of a whole bunch•of them. They were in close formation, masses of 'em. Our machine guns did 'ern up proper. When we do catch them they don't ask for two helpings." "The Scots Gres ran into a patrol of their cavalry—seventeen. They finished off the lot. They kept on enfilading our trenches, and they didn't take long to get fresh ranges. But their rifle fire is rotten."</p>
        <p>ABOUT 1,200 WARRINGTON RECRUITS. Still More Men Are Wanted. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Yesterday the officer in command of the re- cruiting office at Cairo-street Schools informed "The Examiner" representative that, during this week, 309 men from Warrington and the , immediate district had enlisted. • This brings the total number of recruits in Warrington alone, since the inauguration of Kitchener's Army, up to 884, the total for the town and district being about 1,200. But al- though these numbers are good, the more they are increased in the immediate future the bet- ter everybody concerned will be pleased. As we stated last week, men can join Kitchener's Army either for three years or for as long as the war lasts, and .their wives and children Will be given the Service allowance. Recruits between 19 and 30 years are welcomed, and ex-soldiers of the Regular Army can be enlisted up to the age of 42 years. As soon as possible after the war is over they will get their discharge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>WARRINGTON TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>Effort to Provide the Men with Socks, Belts and Helmets.</p>
        <p>£3OO REQUIRED.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln response to a desire expressed by Col. B. Fairclough, T.D., the officer commanding the above, that extra woollen garments should be sent to our men, we are writing to ask if you will kindly make it known that at a meeting held at the Drill Hall, Warrington, on August 25th, it was unanimously resolved that an effort should be made to provide every man of the Battalion with two pairs socks, one body belt, and one sleeping helmet. The cost of these would be approximately .MOO, and we feel sure that we can rely on the support of the town and district to raise this amount.</p>
        <p>Any sum, however small, will be most wel- come, also any assistance in the providing of material or the making of the above articles. Yours truly, for the Committee, ETHEL C. PAIRCLOUGH, President. JAS. HY. SMETHURST, Hon. Treas. HELEN FAIRCLOUGH, Hon. Sec.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>ACCESSION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ACCESSION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>There has been a large accession to the ranks of the officers of the Warrington Territorials. The following have been gazetted second lieutenants:— Carlton Collinwood, Newton-le-Willows, late a lieutenant in the Battalion; C. L. Williamson (son of the late Vicar of Thelwall), late a captain in the Battalion. Thomas Ridgway, solicitor, late second lieu- tenant, 3rd V.B. Cheshire Regiment, and served in the Boer war; K. C. G. Wray, son of a former curate of St. Paul's and vicar of St. Peter's; W. K. Armstrong, A. H. Hatton, son of Mr. Hatton, of Messrs. Robert Davies and Co., solicitors. They left Warrington on Thursday to join their regiment.</p>
        <p>:Second-lieutenant C. H. Robertson, who has been in charge of the Drill Hall since the -de- parture of the regiment, is promoted to lieutenant</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>THITECROSS WARD. Liberal Candidate Elected.</head>
        <p>THITECROSS WARD. Liberal Candidate Elected.</p>
        <p>The nominations for the filling of the vacancy in Whitecross Ward, occasioned by the elevation of Councillor Peacock (the Mayor) to the Alder- manic Bench, were received at the Town Clerk's office on Monday. There was only one—that of Mr. James Thomas Barton, commission agent, of 100, Lovely-lane, who had been invited to fill the vacancy by the unanimous vote of an execu- tive meeting of the Whitecross Ward Liberal Committee held on Tuesday evening of last week. Messrs. Thomas Dale and Henry Osborne were the proposer and seconder. As this was the only nomination received, Mr. Barton enters the Council unopposed.</p>
        <p>THE ECLIPSE OF THE SUN. 21st August, 1914. What is this sudden shade which glides Across the fair face of the sun, And all the smiling landscape hides In twilight when the night has gone?</p>
        <p>It spreads and deepens till the day Grows unimaginably drear, And round about on many a spray Birds twitter with unwonted fear.</p>
        <p>Even so the gathering horror creeps Over sweet Europe's smiling face, And unimagined darkness steeps A continent in Hell's embrace.</p>
        <p>And still the awful shadow grows Until men's hearts turn faint with dread, But still behind the darkness glows The sun, not wholly overspread.</p>
        <p>And so the sun of Freedom shines Uptarnished in the heaven afar, While here her troops in bleeding lines Move onward to earth-shaking war.</p>
        <p>And, though the darkness grows apace Until the very world turns pale, It cannot hide the shining face Of the strong sun, which shall prevail.</p>
        <p>Eternal as the sun in heaven I see the light of Freedom burn, And soon the shadow shall be eriven From Europe's face, and peace return.</p>
        <p>Already, look, the darkness flies, The yellow orb resumes his reign, And from the gloom the flowers arise, The birds begin their songs again.</p>
        <p>And, over cities seared to-day With blood, and fire, and wrong, and shame, Where armies move and vampires prey, I see the sun of Freedom flame. I watch the darkness change and fade, And, where brave blood was gladly spilt, New nations rising unafraid, And more majestic cities built.</p>
        <p>Where tyrants wade through tears and blood And cannons thunder even now, Fair Freedom, with the eyes of God, Lifts her unalterable brow,</p>
        <p>The fight, alas! must still be won, But, while our armies bleed and die, Shines out once more the shrouded sun Presaging Freedom's victory. ARTHUR BENNETT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
