        <p>Trams as Recruiters. Warrington tramcars have been fs'i service to assist in the recruiting hcs)a men for Kitchener's Army go to enlist are prominently'dis.plafed °net] London was the first to adopt this 51.-nc Warrington is said to be the first 13r01„1- to follow the example set by Londo'' Warrington Food Prir,es' fo( Yesterday it was reported that 0,!)„„ remained normal with the excelal—tb land butter, whicl are still den,reresf were before the war. Sugar I"—, the rise, but the announcement thsAfe-est ment had arranged with the rpea plasters to supply 60,000 tons hetw- December may tend to prevent thell ' ; p price. Fish is slightly 'dearer, 1), vegetables are cheap and plentiful.</p>
        <p>Spy Scares. nr All kinds of rumours, says n c" have been in existence since the lvti and we have had our share locallY, foolish one we have heard of basrarcx annoyance to the spy"" and his ~ brothers John and Richard Tantte,:e:e nishers, of Winwick•street, have cl to be Germans. As a matter of ,3 faf long to an old Gloucestershire b grandfather being Mayor of Malinesv time. When Mr. Richard Tanner (who is, by the way, the eldest dal/ea Alfred Tilling, of Lower Walton, tln brother serving in the Mediterrsl went to Llandudno last week it vii°4 rumoured that Mr. Tanner had Tot and was being kept prisoner. Wrinliftt all be anxious to put a stop t° aZer nature of espionage or furnishing, noi information, we should be care. -,,a alarmist rumours of such a folis. one mentioned above.</p>
