      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Procession of Motors.</head>
        <p>Procession of Motors.</p>
        <p>A stream of drays rushed through War- rington this week. Most of the yell:cies, which were in command of soldiers, belonged to Scot- tish firms, and were evidently commandeered for service abroad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Cheshire Potatoes for the Front.</head>
        <p>Cheshire Potatoes for the Front.</p>
        <p>A large consignment of potatoes grown in that part of Cheshire near Warrington has this week been despatched to France for the purpose of feeding the troops at the front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Jurymen Enlist.</head>
        <p>Jurymen Enlist.</p>
        <p>The services of two spare jurymen were re- quired at an inquest in Warrington on Tuesday. When the Coroner asked where they were, the police informed him that they had enlisted in Kitchener's Army. Mr. Brighouse congratulated them upon their patriotism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>To Waterloo.</head>
        <p>To Waterloo.</p>
        <p>On one of the baggage transports which left Warrington this week for the South was a small label bearing the word "Waterloo." Inquiries showed that it was to the London Station of that name the military baggage was consigned, not to the famous battleground near Brussels.</p>
        <p>Latest War Catch.</p>
        <p>This week's war "catch" in Warrington takes the form of the following question: Have you heard the trams are stopped now at nights? The innocent naturally ask why. Then comes the answer: To let passengers get on and off the trams. Who invents these catches?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Not to Worry Germans.</head>
        <p>Not to Worry Germans.</p>
        <p>It is stated that a Warringtonian eager to join Kitchener's Army, found himself rejected because he had not a full set of teeth. "Blow me," he was heard to remark, "I thought they wanted men to shoot Germans, not to worry them." Is this an old Boer War joke revived?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Rev. E. C. E. Carleton in Switzerland:</head>
        <p>Rev. E. C. E. Carleton in Switzerland:</p>
        <p>Although it is known that he is quite safe in Switzerland, the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton cannot yet return home. On Saturday evening 400 English people left Lucerne for Paris and London by the first train of a special series being run by the Government, and it had been hoped that the reverend gentlquian was in- cluded in that number. Such, however, has proved not to be the case.</p>
        <p>Warrington Territorials. The local Territorials, it is said in letters sent home, are undergoing very hard training jr the purpose of making them fit for active service. A march of 40 hours' duration, in the course of which the men slept on the open moorland, was recently undertaken. The majority of the men are reported to be in the "pink of condition." A detachment, it is stated, are going to be sent back to a Lancashire depot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Farmers and the War.</head>
        <p>Farmers and the War.</p>
        <p>_ Many farmers in the Warrington district are now employing middle-aged men as harvesters in place of the younger men who have been called up for service. One farmer told "The Examiner" representative that although they could not expect the older men to do the same amount of work as younger men, they were pay- ing the full harvest wages. A number of volun- teers are also assisting on some farms.</p>
        <p>Thought He Could Beg. Challenged by a Warrington policeman for I begging. John Williams. or Winlow, a tramp, of no fixed abode, drew himself up to attention and , informed the officer that he was a Government official about to, proceed to the front, and was therefore entitled to beg if he liked. At the Police Court on Monday the Chief Constable said he had absolutely nothing to do with the Government, and had not worked for years. His condition was so bad that it would be an act of charity to send him to prison. The Mayor sent him to gaol for a month with bard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Guardians and Distress.</head>
        <p>Guardians and Distress.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Board of Guardians on Wednesday, Mr. A. J. Jolley the chairman) said that on the previous day Colonel Wood, acting on behalf of the Lancashire County Council, called a meeting at the Earlestown Town Hall with reference to distress caused by the war. Mr. Furness, Mr. Bottomley-, Mr. Sutton and himself represented the interests in this district. The primary business was to deal with distress outside the county boroughs, and a Provisional Committee was appointed, which will include representatives from indus- trial concerns.</p>
        <p>Proper Young "John Bulls."</p>
        <p>- -• According to Lord Kitchener, some splendid young men are now joining, .and it seems that Warrington is supplying its quota. One young man, the son of a well-known local manufacturer, volunteered as .a motor cyclist dispatch rider. At his examination it was discovered that he had a knowledge of aviation and of aeroplane motors. He was promptly drafted into the Aviation Corps, where he is reported to be doing good work. Another who offered to enlist in any regiment was discovered to be an expert motor engineer and lie is now located somewhere in France as a motor mech- anic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Joked Even in Danger.</head>
        <p>Joked Even in Danger.</p>
        <p>A Warring-t-onian just returned from holiday- ing in North Wales informs us that the visitors generally were so confident of their country's safety that they even joked at the expense of the soldiers, who spent a portion of each day drilling in the neighbourhood. One morning, about twenty ladies and gentlemen marched out of an hotel on the front, all hearing Union Jacks. Then, at the command of their "colonel "—a scarlet-visaged gentleman of considerable rotundity—they "formed twos," and proceeded along the promenade, pausing at length to per- form some hitherto unknown drills, in the pres- ence of an interested number of spectators. The return to thej hotel was made via the main streets of the town, to the amusement of many shopkeepers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>War Office Secrets.</head>
        <p>War Office Secrets.</p>
        <p>A young man charged under the Official Secrets Act was before the local magistrates again on Tuesday, when a further remand was ordered. The prisoner, Harold Dutton, a native of Hough Green, Widnes, offered no objection to the remand. The offence was that "on the 11th August, being a person who had held office under His Majesty, he unlawfully retained in his possession a certain document which he had no.right to detain." The Chief Constable stated that prisoner was before the Court on Monday of last week, when the case was adjourned until that day. Inquiries had been continued during the adjournment, but he *as not prepared to offer any evidence that day. The Director of Public Prosecutions had received the fiat from the Attorney-General that the case should be proceeded with, but a further week's adjourn- ment was necessary. There were a number of witnesses they desired to call, but they were on foreign service. However, they intended to lay the case before the Bench on Tuesday.</p>
        <p>A Detained Warringtonian. Mr. George Monks, son of Mr. and Mrs. Wil- liam Monks, of Lovely-lane, Warrington, who has resided in Hamburg for some years, is quite safe, and will shortly return to Warrington: Originally in the employ of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., he gained an appoint- ment with Messrs. Holler, shipping agents, and went to Hamburg. By entrusting a letter to an American leaving the city far Holland, who kindly , posted it for him, Mr. Monks was enabled to communicate his plans to his parents and, needless to say, the letter tells of many difficulties experienced by the English. He had tried twice previously to write home. "I have met several Englishmen who have been here a long time," he says. "Last Saturday a notice was put up to the effect that the English citi- zens might be able to leave on the Sunday, but that day came without any permission to leave being received. lam very hopeful of being able to leave some day this week, as the cap- tains of the steamers in the harbour have been instructed to keep steam up to leave at almost any moment."</p>
      </div>
