      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>UNEMPLOYMENT AND THE WAR Not Much Distress in Warrington.</head>
        <p>UNEMPLOYMENT AND THE WAR Not Much Distress in Warrington.</p>
        <p>From inquiries we have made there would net appear to be any abnormal conditions of dis- tress through unemployment in Warrington. With true patriotism the Warrington em- ployers are doing their very best to keep all hands fully employed, but where this may be found impracticable, shorter hours will be adopted, though we are pleased to say there are at present very few employers forced to this position. We are asked to emphasise the following advice:— (1) If unemployed, register at the Labour Exchange, Sankey-street, and at the Office of the Director of Education, San- key-street, between the hours of 10 and 12 daily. (2) The charitably disposed will assist in preventing any organisation from being imposed upon by refusing to give relief indiscriminately.</p>
        <p>TO ELIMINATE UNDESERVING CASES. A general register is being opened next week by the Town Clerk, as the hon. secretary of the Local Committee, at the Parr Hall, of all cases receiving assistance from any source whatever, whether Poor Law, Soldiers' and Sailors' Families' Association; or through the Borough Treasurer's Department, and other charitable organisations are being asked to co-operate so as to provide a certain means of preventing what undoubtedly would 'become an abuse of charity. It is hoped by this 'means to eliminate all un- deserving cases, and to prevent overlapping of efforts. The hours of payment to dependents and families of Territorials at the Borough Treas- urer's Office, are from 2 to 4.30 p.m. on Fridays.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 29, 1914.</p>
        <p>"A REVOLUTION."</p>
        <p>German Emperor Says English</p>
        <p>Workers are in Revolt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>HE HAS BEEN MISINFORMED.</head>
        <p>HE HAS BEEN MISINFORMED.</p>
        <p>BY "A WORKER."</p>
        <p>When Mark Twain was asked by telegram from an American newspaper if it was true that he was dead that great Yankee humorist replied that the report of his death was " grossly exag- gerated." One is inclined to think that the story the German Emperor has assiduously cir- culated in his official Press in Germany comes Within a similar category. William, the lord militant, has caused to be published the announcement that the British workers are up in revolt against Britain's share in the war. One could scarcely believe such a thing possible if one had not seen the extract translated from leading German papers and published in some of the English papers. The impression this Berlin-made revolution amongst the workers of Britain may serve a purpose in Germany, but we wakers of War- rington and elsewhere can afford lo laugh at it as a flight of imagination. If there is a revolu- tion brewing anywhere amongst the workers, it is, as I stated last week, in Germany.</p>
        <p>WORKERS' SOLIDARITY. I have been in touch with the workers in different parts of Great Britain for many years, and I cannot recall a time when the workers, be they of Warrington or of any other district, showed .more solidarity in a national cause than they are doing at present, so for anyone to say we are on the eve of a workers' revolution, be he even the Emperor of Germany, is a gross attempt to circulate a grave falsehood. Fortun- ately, the Germans are tired of this style of Kaiser canards, and it is scarcely likely that the latest will be believed by the people who are supposed to swallow such yarns—the social democrats.</p>
        <p>The object is palpable. It is an attempt to deceive the democrats into the idea that they should support the Kaiser, and by so doing they would be supporting the alleged wish of the British workers to end the war. The Kaiser's scheme may, like many of his previous ones, miscarry, and he may yet find the social demo- crats seeking to follow what he says the British workers are doing, and they may revolt against his autocratic war. There is more likelihood of a revolution in Germany than in Britain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>POSITION OF THE WORKERS.</head>
        <p>POSITION OF THE WORKERS.</p>
        <p>One is compelled to ask where would Kitchener's Army be without the trade union- ists? The Minister for War has just announcedl that he has practically got his extra 100,0001 men, and I am sure that at least 50 per cent. are I drawn from the ranks of trade unionists, not I to mention the thousands who have gone up With the Army Reserve and the Territorials. I have learned from the secretaries of the trade union branches in Warrington that there iS not a branch of any union which has not lost members through the war, hence the reason why the branches have decided to pay the members' subscriptions and thus keep them in benefit until their return. What Warrington trade unionists have experienced in this respect, much greater is the experience of branches in such cities as Manchester, Liverpool, Birmingham, &amp;C. This war has demonstrated that trade unionists are as much patriots as any member of the nobility or as any member of other sec- tions of the community in this time of crisis. There has never been a war in which the Empire has been so firmly knit together, and there has never been a war in which trade unionists have rolled up to the colours as they have done at the present time. We are not fighting a capitalist war; we are out to save such democratic nations as gallant little Belgium, France and Britain from being strangled on the altar of autocratic militarism. The workers of Warrington, and elsewhere, plainly recognise that militarism a la the Kaiser must be slain once and for all, and hence their willingness to join in fighting their nation's battles.</p>
        <p>THC MINERS' EXAMPLE. The miners have probably the strongest union in the country, their leaders are Socialists, the great bulk of the members are Socialists, and men who detest war when it becomes mere fight- ing for self-aggrandisement on the part of the nations, but the miners have realised the in- ward meaning of this Germati-made war, and these men of peace are rushing to join the colours, not by the score, but almost by the thousand. When the Miners' Union has time to make up a list of members who have become soldiers, I believe they will be able to show a record of patriotism that will be hard to equal. The Railwaymen's Union, the Postal Servants, in a word, every great union will have lost hun- dreds of their members. Not one member, not even the most advanced Socialist leader, has raised a protest. They have set themselves to a more noble task—to entrench their funds so that they can give speedy assistance to the depend- ents of their members who have gone to the front.</p>
        <p>IF THE KAISER ONLY KNEW. If the Kaiser only knew what is happening in our trade union circles, as you workers know, he would never have been so misguided as to have said the workers of Britain were in revolt..</p>
        <p>Yes, of course, we are in revolt, not against our country, but' against the military autocrats, the arch-apostle of whom is Kaiser William 11. of Germany, who have plunged Europe into an ocean of blood and who have for years ground down all nations, and chiefly the workers, with senseless taxation to provide more massacring machinery. The British workers realise that such a state of affairs must end, hence the splendid solidarity at present displayed in con- nection with this war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>ON THE BATTLE EVE.</head>
        <p>ON THE BATTLE EVE.</p>
        <p>Letter Received from a Warrington Soldier in Belgium.</p>
        <p>LOCATION NOT STATED. Written presumably in Belgium on the eve of the recent great battle, in which British troops received their baptism of fire, a letter from the front was received in Warrington on Thursday morning. The writer of this interesting epistle was Private Patten (8,354), of the Ist Battalion King's Liverpool Regiment, who is with the Infantry Brigade of the British Expeditionary Force. The recipient was his wife, Mrs. Patten, of 6, Heaton-square, Orford-street, Warrington. Private Patten was for some time a carter employed by a Warrington firm, but later, owing to a scarcity of work, he found. employ- ment in the nightsoil department of the Cor- poration. He was called up on the declaration of war and re-joined his regiment at Seaforth Barracks, Liverpool, soon after August Bank Holiday. Although no official information is available ' it is presumed that his battalion was connected with the force which made such a gallant fight against the Germans this week. The letter has been passed by the Censor and bears no post- Mark except that of Warrington. The postage stamp is a special one printed for the use of the Arnry and Navy. THE LETTER. The letter, which conveys no information as to what is taking place, states : Dear Wife,—Just a few lines, hoping to find you and the children in good health, as it finds me at present. I ara sorry I could , not write sooner because we could not get paper or envelopes. We could not get any money until Sunday, and now we cannot spend it. We cannot get fags or bacon, and they cannot change the money. I cannot tell you exactly where we are, but we are in Belgium; therefore I will conclude my letter by wishing you all good things, and God bless you all. If I write any more you may not get the letter, as we have to be very careful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>WHAT WE HAVE DONE AND WHAT REMAINS TO BE DONE.</head>
        <p>WHAT WE HAVE DONE AND WHAT REMAINS TO BE DONE.</p>
        <p>Earl Kitchener, who on rising was greeted with cheers from both sides of the House, said: As this is the first time I have had the honour of addressing yOur Lordships, I must ask for the indulgence of the House. In the first place I desire to make a personal statement. Noble lords on both sides of the House doubtless know that, while associating myself in the fullest degree for the prosecution of the war with my colleagues in His Majesty's Government, my position on this bench does not in any way imply that I belong to any political party, for, as a soldier, I have no politics. (Hear, hear.) Another point is that my occupation of the post of Secretary of State for War is a temporary one. The terms of my service are the same as those under which some of the finest por- tions of our manhood, now so willingly stepping forward to join the colours, are engaging—that is to say, for the war, or if it lasts longer than three years, then for three years. It has been asked why the latter limit has been fixed. It is because should this disastrous war be pro- longed—and no one can foretell with any cer- tainty its duration—then after three years' war there 'will be others fresh and fully prepared to take our places and see this matter through. The very serious conflict in which we are now engaged on the Continent has been none of our seeking. It will undoubtedly strain the re- sources of our Empire and entail considerable sacrifices on our people. These will be willingly borne for our honour and the preservation of our position in the world, and will be shared by our Dominions beyond the seas, now sending contingents and assistance of every kind to help the Mother Country in this struggle. (Cheers.) If I am unable, owing to military consideration for the best interests of the Allied Armies in the field, to speak with much detail on the present situation of our Army on the Continent, I am sure your Lordships will pardon me for the necessary restraint which is imposed upon me. (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>THE EXPEDITIONARY FORCE. The Expeditionary Force has taken the field on the French north-west frontier and ad- vanced to the neighbourhood of Mons, in Bel- gium. Our troops have already been for 36 hours in contact with a superior force of Ger- man invaders. During that time they have maintained the traditions of British soldiers and have behaved with the utmost gallantry. The movements which they have been called upon to execute have been those which demand the greatest steadiness in the soldiers and skill in their commanders. Sir John French telegraphed to me at mid- night as follows:— In spite of hard marching and fighting the British Force is in the best of spirits." And I replied:— - -•-</p>
        <p>"Congratulate troops on their splendid work. We are all proud of them." (Cheers.) As your Lordships are aware, Euro- pean fighting causes greater casualties than the campaigns in which we are generally engaged in other parts of the world. The nation will, I am sure, be fully prepared to meet whatever losses and sacrifices we may have to make in this war. Sir John French, without having been able to verify the numbers, estimates the loss since the commencement of active opera- tions at rather more than 2,000 men hors de combat. As to the work of the last few weeks, I have ,to remark that when war was declared mobili- sation took place without any hitch whatever, and our Expeditionary Force proved itself wholly efficient, thoroughly well equipped, and immediately ready to take the field. The Press and the public have in their respective spheres lent invaluable aid to the Government in preserving a discreet silence which the exigencies of the situation obviously demanded, and I gladly take this opportunity of bearing testimony to the value of their co-operation.. •The hands of the military authorities were also strengthened by the readiness with which thel civilian community faced and accepted the novel situation created by the issue of requisitions for horses, transports, supplies, and billets: The railway companies in the all-important matter of the transport facilities have more than justi- fied the complete confidence reposed in them by the War Office, all grades of railway services having laboured with Amtiring energy and patience. And it is well to repeat that the conveyance of our troops across the Channel was accomplished, thanks to the cordial co- operation of the Admiralty, with perfect smoothness and without any untoward incident whatever. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>FACTOR OF HIGH MILITARY SIGNIFICANCE.</head>
        <p>FACTOR OF HIGH MILITARY SIGNIFICANCE.</p>
        <p>We know how deeply the French people ap- preciate the value of the prompt assistance we have been able to afford them at the very out- set of the war; and it is obvious that not only the moral but the material support our troops are now rendering must prove to be a factor of high military significance in restricting the sphere and determining the duration of hostili- ties. Had the conditions of strategy permitted every one in this country would have rejoiced to see us ranged alongside the gallant Belgian Army in that superb struggle against desperate odds which has just been witnessed. (Loud cheers.) But, although this privilege was per- force denied to us, Belgium knows of °lir sym- pathy with her in her sufferings, of our indig- nation at the blows which have been inflicted on her, and also of our resolution to make sure that in the end her sacrifices will not have been unavailing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>VAST RESERVES OF THE EMPIRE.</head>
        <p>VAST RESERVES OF THE EMPIRE.</p>
        <p>While other countries engaged in this war have, under a system of compulsory service, brought their full resources of men into the , field, we, under our national system, have not' done so, and can therefore still point to a vast reserve drawn from the resources both of the Mother Country and of the British Dominions across the seas. (Cheers.) The response which has already been made by the great Dominions abundantly proves that we did not look in vain to these sources of military strength, and while India, Canada, Australia, and New Zealand are all sending us powerful contingents, in this country the Territorials are replying with loyalty to the stern call of duty— (cheers)—which has come to them with such exceptional force. Sixty-nine battalions have, , with fine patriotism, already volunteered for service abroad, and when trained and organised in the larger formations will be able to take their places in the line. The 100,000 recruits</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <p>Lord Kitchener's Inspiring Call to Arms.</p>
        <p>A LIFE-AND-DEATH STRUGGLE</p>
        <p>In which Great Britain will Win on Staying</p>
        <p>Power.</p>
        <p>The slight check which the Allies sustained last week-end in Belgium, put to its proper use, gives a powerful setting to the mag- nificent appeal Lord Kitchener made to his countrymen when he delivered his first speech in the Lords as Secretary for War, on Tuesday night. Lord Kitchener has got his extra 100,000 men, but he needs more and he must have them. Other nations, he says, have their whole manhood in the field; we have almost our whole Manhood in reserve. As our adversaries wear away we shall be drawing upon our reserves at home and throughout the Empire, and so we shall, once again, be able to save Liberty in Europe from perishing.</p>
        <p>But the men must come forward as they have been coming forward since we threw down the glove to the insolent, and, as events have proved, the barbarous foe. There is no doubt that German success on the Continent would seriously menace our position as a nation; and our every nerve must be strained to ensure that that unthinkable thing shall not happen.</p>
        <p>"The Toronto News" says that Lord Kitchener at the War Office is worth an Army. Let us see what that Army personified In one human being has to say to the other Army not yet in being which he calls to his aid :</p>
        <p>for which, in the first place, it has been thought necessary to call, have been already practically secured. tCheers.) This force will be trained and organised in divisions similar to those which are now serving on the Continent. Be- hind these we have our Reserves. The Special Reserve and the National Reserve have each their own part to play in the organisation of our national defence.</p>
        <p>A STRIKING CONTRAST. The Empires with whom we are at war have called to the colours almost their entire male Population. The principle we on our part shall observe is this, that while their maximum force undergoes a constant diminution, the reinforce- ments we prepare shall steadily and increasingly flow out until we have an Army in the field which in numbers not less than in quality will not be unworthy of the power and responsibili- ties of the British Empire. (Cheers.) I can- not at this stage say what will be the limits of the forces required, or what measures may eventually become necessary to supply and maintain them. The scale of the Field Army which we are now calling into being is large and may rise in the course of the next six or seven months to a total of 30 divisions continu- ally maintained in the field. But if the war should be protracted, and if its fortunes should be varied or adverse, exertions and sacrifices beyond any which have been demanded will be required from the whole nation and Empire, and where they are required we are sure they will not be denied to the extreme needs of the State by Parliament or the people. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>REV. W. BRACECAMP'S APPEAL. Men Wanted to Fight in a Righteous</head>
        <p>REV. W. BRACECAMP'S APPEAL. Men Wanted to Fight in a Righteous</p>
        <p>Catise.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you kindly allow me to appeal most earnestly to all my comrades in War- rington, who are eligible, to join the Army at once?</p>
        <p>_ Never before, perhaps, has there been such a need for men. Your King and country want you—it is a matter of life and death. Our great leader tells us he wants 500,000 men. Now, then, come along lads! I thank God for the brave men who have already volunteered, and are still offering themselves; but as one walks down Bridge-street or Sankey-street on a Saturday, or a Monday night, one cannot help feeling that there are still such a number who are deaf to their country's call. You can only die once, as far as your bodies are concerned, and to die on the field of battle fighting for a righteous cause is one of the best deaths, in my humble opinion, a man can possibly die. I am not asking you to do what I am not prepared to do myself; I have offered my services as chaplain to go anywhere, and at any time; I hope I shall be called up; I have given my boy, and if I had a dozen I would persuade them to think of King and country before anything else. If I were younger (un- fortunately they are not taking men at 51), I would join as a "Tommy." Victory must be won; no sacrifice must be too great; it is a fight for liberty, freedom, and true religion. Hurry up, do your duty. We do not want conscription, but we must have the men. Yours sincerely, • WM. BRACECAMP (Chaplain 4th South Lancashire Regt.). St. Paul's Vicarage, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>CALL TO ARMS.</head>
        <p>THE CALL TO ARMS.</p>
        <p>More Recruits Wanted for Lord Kitchener. HOW_ALL CAN HELP.</p>
        <p>• ro the Edito-.of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>_ Sir,—Many inquiries are being received from retired officers and civilians asking how they can help and what they can tell men who apply to them for the conditions of enlistment. With regard to the first question. At this present crisis no one can be of greater assist- ance or do better work for the country than by interesting herself and himself in recruiting for Lord Kitchener's new Army. Although recruiting agencies have been estab- lished throughout the kingdom, it is not neces- sary for individuals to be specially nominated as recruiters or to obtain authority from the War Office or the headquarters of a command, but those who wish to help, and there are thousands of them, should begin straight away with the intention of obtaining recruits, going , on the principle that "every little helps," and with the conviction that by so doing they are serving their King and country to the utmost of their ability. Some of us must stay at home and our motto should be "Keep smiling and recruiting."</p>
        <p>The conditions of service are : Men are required for all branches of the Regular Army, and if medically fit will be accepted on the following conditions :- 1. Height, sft. 3in. and upwards. Chest, at least 34in.</p>
        <p>Age, 19 to 30 years. Ex-regular soldiers can join the Special Reserve between the ages of 30 and 42. They can enlist for one year or for the duration of the war, i.e., if the war ends in less time, men will be discharged with all convenient speed.</p>
        <p>Married men Or widowers with children will be accepted and will draw separation allow- ance. 2. Men can also enlist for the following periods according to which arm they join : Cavalry, Infantry, and Army Service Corps: Seven years with the colours and five years with the Reserve. Royal Artillery and Royal Engineers: Six years with the colours and six years with the Reserve. Height, sft. 3in. and upwards. Chest, at least 34in. Age, 18 to 25 years. Married men are not accepted under this heading. Having explained the conditions, the next step is to tell a man where to enlist. There should be no difficulty about this, as every Post Office and Police Station in the Command can give the information. This will be the nearest recruiting agency, and as Lord Kitchener has already suggested it will be of real assistance if those who are in possession of motor cars and</p>
        <p>other vehicles will convey the men to the place of enlistment.</p>
        <p>There is one other very important point. We have had several telegrams and letters lately to the effect that men are waiting to be enlisted at various places owing to works having closed, and it is presumed that our informants knew beforehand that these works would close. If those who are in a position to warn us of such happening and would let us know as long be- forehand as possible the information would be invaluable.</p>
        <p>It is suggested that retired officers who are helping in this direction should wear uniform. In conclusion, I wish to ask all those who have complaints with regard to the way recruit- ing is being carried on in their own particular district to address their remarks, in so far as the Western Command is concerned, to me personally, and not to the War Office, as this will save a day's delay. PHILIP ALLEN, Major, Assistant Inspector of Recruiting, Western Command. Headquarters Western Command, Watergate 'House, Chester. August 20th, 1914. PREMIER'S STATEMENT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>AVant All The Recruits We Can Get.</head>
        <p>AVant All The Recruits We Can Get.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday Mr. Lee asked whether the Prime Minister was 'aware that, owing to the terms of the Procla- mation calling for recruits, there was a pre- valent impression that only 100,000 men were required. Mr. Asquith: That is a mistaken impression; and I hope it will not go abroad. We want all the recruits we can get.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>RED TAPE TO GO.</head>
        <p>RED TAPE TO GO.</p>
        <p>In reply to Mr. Norton Griffiths, Mr. Tennant said that instructions had been issued to all Army medical examiners that no man who was organically sound was to be re- fused enlistment on account of bad teeth, un- less that defect was believed to be the cause of his malnutrition. Many highly-qualified dental surgeons were patriotically giving their services for the free treatment of recruits. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>NO COMPULSORY SERVICE.</head>
        <p>NO COMPULSORY SERVICE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hamersley asked the Prime Minister whether it was not necessary, in order to secure the safety of the United Kingdom, that some measure of compulsory service should be put into force. Mr. Asquith: The answer is in the negative. (Ministerial cheers.) I would refer hon. mem- bers to what my noble friend the Secretary for War said yesterday in another place. (General cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>CANADA'S READY AID.</head>
        <p>CANADA'S READY AID.</p>
        <p>The Canadian War Budget, laid before the Dominion Parliament last Thursday, proposed to raise during the next seven months an addi- tional .£7,000,000 of revenue by increased Cus- toms duties on sugar, coffee, spirits, and tobacco. The preference to British goods is to apply to these increased duties. The Government's proposals were heartily approved by Sir Wil- frid Laurier and the Liberal party. Ontario Province has offered .4:100,000 to be spent in any way the Imperial Government chooses.</p>
        <p>Nora Scotia will give 100,000 tons of coal for the use of the Admiralty.</p>
        <p>A Toronto citizen has given £20,000 for the purchase of a battery of machine guns, and has offered the authorities the use of his yacht and his Marconi station at Toronto.</p>
        <p>Montreal's contingent comprises nearly 2,500 men, including over 1,000 Highlanders. Crowds numbering 100,000 lined the streets to the sta- tions and cheered the departing volunteers. Over 15,000 picked volunteers for war service are now training at Valcartier Camp, and a few days will see the full contingent of 25,000 men getting ready for the work. In Toronto nearly 200 chauffeurs have volun- teered for service. The Board of Control in Winnipeg has re- solved by an emphatic resolution not to pur- chase German goods. The Niobe will soon be in commission. Her full complement of 720 men has been raised in Canada, all former members of the British Navy.</p>
        <p>TEA FROM COLOMBO. Colombo tea-planters have given a million pounds of tea for the troops.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>WARRINGTON ATHLETES</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ATHLETES</p>
        <p>THE WAY.</p>
        <p>SHOW</p>
        <p>Systematic Drilling Started.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Athletic Volunteer Force had an "opening meet" on Thursday afternoon, when thirty tradesmen, shop assistants, ezc., met at the headquarters of Warrington Athletic Club. A Church Lads' Brigade member, Mr. Albert Spencer, undertook the duties of instruc- tor, for the occasion, and with an enthusiastic company soon mastering the preliminaries of drill necessary for marching, a start was made. Splendid order was maintained through Stretton to Hatton, where a wayside halt was called, and more drill undergone prior to returning through Walton to headquarters, where, on dismissal,l all promised to induce, if possible, at least one friend to join in rime for next Thursday's mach. As many cricketers, cyclists, football players, rowing men, athletes, golfers, swimmers, and other sportsmen are "not already attached to any organisation of national defence" or "not eligible to join the Regular Army, the Terri- torials, or any other existing recognised force," the local company should be quickly augmented by those willing to spare a few hours a week for their country's serVice, with a view to forming an additional line of defence.</p>
        <p>It must not be overlooked that if the need arises, the country will call up all its men, therefore, the facilities must be provided to: . .</p>
        <p>(1) Learn the rudiments of rifle shooting; (2) drill. .</p>
        <p>Even if a man does not believe in national ser- vice or the "conscription bogey," lie should realise that taking the trouble to prepare him- self for eventualities is now a national duty. Secretaries of local organisations, particularly junior football clubs, should prevail upon their members to co-operate at once, and individuals interested may glean further information from the lion. secretary of the local Athletic Club: Mr. Reuben Bennett, 194, Gorsey-lane, Warring- ton.</p>
        <p>There is no membership fee and no State aid, but members must do their share and pay for their own ammunition, which will be provided at a special cheap rate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>AS GOOD AS A HOLIDAY.</head>
        <p>AS GOOD AS A HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>The one object of a holiday is to gain renewed energy for another year's work, so that one may return with sound nerves and a clear brain, full of vigour and ready for business again. Un- fortunately, that object is not always attained. _</p>
        <p>The whole matter is one of blood. If your blood has become impoverished or impure the holiday is probably too brief for you to obtain proper benefit; you should start your holiday in reasonable health, though very few people can claim to be free from the dregs of some serious winter ailment, which has clogged the system and drained the blood of its natural strength. That is why you feel fagged out, brain weary and incapable of much exertion. Then do not wait for your holidays, but begin now to build up your blood with a true tonic, and so invigorate and refresh your system. Nothing is more valuable for this purpose than Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. Over and over again they have been proved to be as good as a holiday to jaded men and women. If you can- not take a holiday, the value of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills is even greater, for they make good the waste caused by illness and the wear and tear of life. Any dealer can supply Dr. Williams' Pink Pills; or, if preferred, you can obtain them direct from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, E.C., for 2s. 9d. one box, or 13s. 9d. for six boxes. A splendid little book of useful information for ailing people is "The Blood and its Work." Send along a Postcard to-day for a free copy to Book Dept., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London. Business as</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Scarlet Fever Epidemic.</head>
        <p>Scarlet Fever Epidemic.</p>
        <p>Twenty-three new cases of scarlet fever were reported in Warrington last week. There were still 94 cases in the hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>BRAVE BELGIUM.</head>
        <p>BRAVE BELGIUM.</p>
        <p>What We Owe our Sorely Tried</p>
        <p>Allies.</p>
        <p>HELP HEEDED.</p>
        <p>The storm of war has once more broken over brave little Belgium. Bier fair fields are trodden down by the tramp of armies; her prosperous industries are at a staaidstill; her sons are perishing by the hundred on the battlefield.</p>
        <p>But for the courage of her King and the staunchness of her soldiers, the Teuton hosts might already be far on their way to Paris, and the cause of freedom in Europe desperate. _ _</p>
        <p>Once more we stand by her side, fighting for liberty, for justice, and for the right to live. We shall suffer much, but the Belgian people will suffer more. No shot will, we believe, be fired on our soil, and we have the whole neutral world behind us to support our commerce and our industries.</p>
        <p>Surely, then, despite the heavy calls made upon our resources, we can find something to send to the brave Belgians in their hour of need, as a token of brotherhood in war and in peace, and as an earnest that the memories of great deeds done in common have not perished! Ramillies, Quatre-Bras, Waterloo! On such fields was the compact made with British and Belgian blood. Quite recently, the Duke of Wellington and Field-Marshal Earl Roberts4nd others have been trying to raise a fund to save the field of Waterloo from the hand of the desecrator. A more urgent call has come. It is a call to succour those who have offered their all in the quar- rel which is ours as well as theirs.</p>
        <p>Once again, as at Quatre-Bras, only over a far more extended area, the cornfields are trodden down and the harvest destroyed. Once more, as at Waterloo, The earth is covered thick with other clay, While its own clay shall cover, heaped and pent, Rider and horse, friend and foe, in one red burial blent. We must spare something for the Belgians. Sore as our own need may be, theirs is more sore than ours. They have placed us in their debt, and we must not be laggards in paying. All contributions to be sent to— THE BELGIAN LEGATION,</p>
        <p>15, WEST HALKIN-STREET, LONDON, S.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>NATION TO NATION. Parliament Says What We Think and</head>
        <p>NATION TO NATION. Parliament Says What We Think and</p>
        <p>'Will Do.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thursday the Premier, the Leader of the Opposition and the Irish Leader paid tributes to our Belgian Allies. An address was passed expressing admiration for the heroic resistance offered by Belgium to the invader, and pledging the unending support of this country. The House of Lords passed the same resolu- tion.</p>
        <p>THE PREMIER'S FIRE. Mr. Asquith raised the House to great enthu- siasm by his praise of the Belgians and his advocacy of their cause.</p>
        <p>"Sir/' he began, "very few words are needed to commend to the House the terms of this Address. The war, which is now shaking to its foundation the whole European system, origi- nated in a quarrel in which this country had no direct concern. We strove with all our might, as every one now knows—(cheers)—to prevent its outbreak, and when that was no longer possible, to limit its area. It is of im- portance, and I think it is relevant to this motion, that it should' be clearly understood when it was and why we intervened. It was only, sir, when we were confronted with the choice between keeping and breaking solemn obligations in the discharge of a binding trust I and a shameless subservience to naked force that we threw away the scabbard." (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>"We Do Not Repent." He gripped the box tightly, faced the House and continued: "We do not repent our decision."</head>
        <p>"We Do Not Repent." He gripped the box tightly, faced the House and continued: "We do not repent our deci- sion."</p>
        <p>When the demonstration had spent itself he went on:</p>
        <p>" The issue was one which no great and self- respecting nation, certainly none bred and nurtured like ourselves in this ancient home of liberty, could without undying shame have de- clined. (Cheers.) We were bound by obliga- tions, plain and paramount, to assert and main- tain the threatened independence of a small and neutral State. Belgium had no interest of her own to serve—(cheers)—save and except the one supreme and overriding interest of every State, great or little, which is worthy of the name—the preservation of her integrity and of her national life. (Cheers.) History tells us that the duty of ascertaining and maintaining that great principle, which is, after all, the well-spring of civilisation and of progress, has fallen once and again at the most critical moments in the past on States relatively small in area and in population, but great In courage and resource—(cheers)—on Athens, Sparta, the Swiss Cantons, and not less gloriously three centuries ago, on the Netherlands." The House did not rise to Athens and Sparta, but the reference to the great war of the Netherlands against Spain drew a long and deep cheer of approval. "Never, sir, I venture to say," continued Mr. Askoith, "has the duty been more clearly and bravely acknowledged—more heroically discharged than during the last weeks by the Belgian King and the Belgian people." The House as it cheered knew that the speech was rising to its climax. Faced War's Horrors Without Flinching. "They have faced without flinching and against almost incalculable odds the horrors of eruption—devastation—spoliation "—the three words fell like blows, and the fourth will ring through Europe—" outrage." The charge was flung out like a challenge. The House cheered that challenge to the echo. "They have stubbornly *itlistood and suc- cessfully arrested the inrush of wave after wave of gigantic and overwhelming force. The de- fence of Liege—(loud cheers)—will always be a theme of one of the most inspiring chapters in the annals of liberty. The Belgians have won for themselves the immortal glory which be- longs to a pvple who prefer freedom to ease . and security even to life itself. We are all proud of their alliance and of their friendship. (Cheers.) "We salute them with respect and honour. (Renewed cheers.) We are with them heart and soul because by their side and in their company we are defending at the same time two great causes—the independence of small States and the sanctity of International obliga- tions. We assure them to-day, in the name of this United Kingdom and of the whole Empire, that they can count to the end on our whole- hearted and unfailing support." (Loud and , prolonged cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>WE SHALL NOT FORGET.</head>
        <p>WE SHALL NOT FORGET.</p>
        <p>- - • • -••-- • - Mr. Bonar Law seconded the motion. One point in his speech particularly roused the House.</p>
        <p>"The Belgian people' , " said he "are enduring the horrors enduring of war, and after making every allowance for the sources from which our in- formation comes we do not doubt that they are g them in a form which ought to be im- possible amongst civilised nations." Liberals and Conservatives, Nationalists and Labour members joined in the cheers. "It is," he added, "a struggle between moral influences and brute force—(cheers)—force not the less brutal because it has at its disposal all the materials of invention and of science." (Cheers.) "Belgium has placed us under an obligation which as a nation we shall not for- get." IRELAND STIRRED TO ENTHUSIASM.</p>
        <p>• ••--- "I associate Ireland to the full with this said Mr. Redmond. Ireland was islitiortrieodn'';o enthusiasm by the gallantry of Bel- gimp], and was sending forth her sons to fight i for Belgium. "It s not," cried he, "a loan that Belgium should have from us, but a gift" —and the House responded to the appeal. Aji sections of Nationalists are behind Mr Redmond in his support of Belgium.</p>
      </div>
