        <p>1.1-4</p>
        <p>Street Traffic Regulation. I will try to put in a nutshell my answer to Mr. George Chamberlain's courteous letter. That gentleman now explains that his "major point" was that I should state a case either for or against better street traffic regulation. If I "quite ignored " , this my plea is that I have previously dealt with it in this column—as I think the complainant would have remembered ilk° had been a regular reader. Let me restate my 'tbsition in the fewest possible words: it may serve as my reply to Mr. Chamberlain's latest criticism. I hold that the imposition of a speed limit is more likely to hinder than to help the police in proceedings against reckless motor- ists,: and that these latter would therefore be well-advised in submitting to it quietly wher- eVes it is proposed to substitute it by way of amendment of the existing law. In my opinion, it is in every way better that the authorities should remain free to charge offenders with driving to the danger of the public." Under a bylaw limiting the rate to so many miles an -hour, the police must, of course, prove that the speed in, question has actually been exceeded, and so far as my observation goes that in prac- tice is generally more difficult than might be supposed; I mean that it is comparatively easy to raise doubts to the full benefit of which a de- fendant is entitled. Moreover, if there were a legal restriction to ten miles an hour, even plat speed would be highly dangerous in narrow and congested streets. The old statute leaves the Matter to police judgment and discretion, and 'obliges all concerned to take into account the circumstances of the moment. A driver must exercise reasonable care, and must not jeopar- dise other users of the highway, riding or a-foot —as, I repeat, he might conceivably do while by no means exceeding the speed limit which Chamberlain would fain see incorporated in the borough bylaws. Ages on Death -Certificates. Last week's paragraph under this head line, . announcing the local Coroner's determination to punish fraudulent people who cleliberately give wrong ages for death certificates with the object of benefiting through the insurance, brought to my mind instances that have come my way from time to time. I have no intention of setting them forth in detail, but I may just mention an , amusing circumstance arising out of the habit ' of illiterate Irish folk of stating their ages hap- hazard—that is, of ,course, in former times, before the spread of education. I have pre= . viously shown in this column that the older people of this class seldom knew their own ages; • and a colony of them in the district which I now have in mind would give insurance agents doing business among them such approximate figures as happened to strike their fancy. Once in those , parts I came across the local superintendent Of ' a big assurance company fresh from an en- counter of the kind. He had received a proposal from one of the male inhabitants, and on asking the man's age received ,the ansWer, "Iv ye put me down the same age az yersilf, that'll just be , rbight, share." What was more, he had accepted' this without further question—as he told me he invariably did all such. As he hastened to ex- plain, matters turned out all right in the end. When a claim came to be made, a cop Y of the birth certificate as well as one of death was re- quired, and on that basis final adjustment was made. If the deceased had under-stated his age, and so had paid all along at a lower rate than otherwise.would have been demanded, a simple calculation, involving proportionate reduction from the amount of the claim, enabled the com- pany's representative 'to rectify the error,. or whatever might be the correct descriptive term. • The insurance people took a charitable view of the original transactipn, and were not a penny the worse after all. The " Breeks." . The recurrence of the Hatton Horticultural Society's Show and the prominent part played by ,a lady in the opening formalities somehow reminded me of a little experience of my own in the days when such exhibitions were much more common throughout Lancashire and Yorkshire than is now the case. At one of which lam non- thinking particularly, the gardener for a wealthy local lady made almost a clean sweep of the prizes in the first class. Many of these consisted of.,..money, but in a few- instances tradesmen had given samples of the things they, dealt in. A tailor, for example, had oared a pair of those nether garments variously called or known as " continuations," " unwhisperables," and so on. When as reporter I came to the section in which these figured, I duly copied from the card, "2nd prize, one pair of trousers, Mrs. —," and, being then a young man, I could not refrain from add- ing a word or two of comment intended to be facetious, and actually, as I flattered myself, rather smart. Looking back upon it now, I fear 'I did but joke " wi' deeficulty." Anyhow, those . chiefly concerned entirely failed to appreciate !‘: "the humour o't." The lady was mightily offended by my bit of satire, and, as she peremp- torily forbade entries by her man for the next show, the officials who had openly enjoyed my clumsy attempt at a little harmless fun, ultim- ately turned to rend me as the cause of the dis- aster that had come upon them. Ever since then I have been very grave and serious indeed. Music Made In Germany. I was glad to find that the promoters of the recent musical festival here had not gone the ' 'absurd length of absoltitely boycotting German music, and had wisely declined to follow the lead of the intolerant few elsewhere who have for the time being refused to have anything to do with Wagner. If those who indulge in such petty and contemptible methods are to be consistent, they Must exclude from their programmes not only Wagner and Strauss, but Bach, Beethoven, Han- del, Mendelssohn, and other immortals of the like nationality. At the same time, I think there is a line to be drawn, and I will tell you where I am inclined to draw it so far as I am personally affected. A friend of mine, a Wes- leyan minister, said last week-end, "I was glad to hear of a congregation singing heartily the ' Austrian ' hymn, which, I believe, is also the German National Anthem." Well, now, candidly I cannot share the pleasure thus avowed. I cannot fancy any German congregation singing ,our National Anthem at present, and I think .;,graszre 'might be excused a hymn tune which is the 4,-.1 3 same as their National Anthem. Individually, . it all events, I have no use for it at this period, "•and I have no doubt that most of our organists • and choirmasters will agree to give the " Austrian " hymn a rest for some time to come. My next-door neighbour is ordinarily a good enough fellow, but he has suddenly developed an unaccountable partiality for "The Watch by the Rhine "—which, alternately with "The Mar- aeillaise," we boisterous youths were wont in our enthusiasm to roar at each other in the early '7o's, accordingly as we admired the then all-conquering Prussians or sympathised with the unfortunate French. And now here is this misguided musician on my right strumming day • by day "The Watch by the Rhine" to my in- tense irritation and indignation. The reader may laugh at me if he will, but I,wish with all my heart the amiable jackass were by the Rhine at this moment—piano and all. German national .lauste, indeed! Why doesn't the unphtriotic idiot give us "Rnle, Britannia," occasionally? ihset7TiCeon don't y a,woyoii understand where I make the Printers' Errors. The lau ghable misprint quoted from a daily iyaper in a Paragraph headed "Near the truth" reminded me of severalthree others, and I may con- clude ree of these. There was that of a Devon which reported a native - as rising at a public function propose "tile spoim there was the death of the chairman.'. , to description of the duke's whoa al paper fetaeninpreonsidteorred has spent the festive season wain, g affair:f shooting peasants:" I was once on few friends, when one ,n;baesefa,,,tevAerToe wrote where am reminded of a denominational journal's it e"'" I refer- ence to the work of the Rev. Thomas Jackson •n the slums of Whitechapel, in which work t-e' in abstinence principles are very much to the front. The sentence should have been "a ,great burden has been lifted from our shoulders." It read, a great burden has beer lifted from our shoulders." At the time of Tariff Reform League activity one of their lecturers declared that the principles' of Tariff Reform were gradually leavening the constituencies. In the local Press it appeared thus: "The principles of Tariff Re- form are gradually leaving the constituencies." That in turn recalls how "The People" thus ' arinounced in its columns the appearance of a 'new pamphlet: "Tariff Reform is w • well dealt with in The Moaning of Tariff Reform,' by Stephen Walter." That was a misprint; still it remains true, that the term "printer's error" has .covered ' multitude of sins since the clays of (se terberg . • and Caxton, and for tfiose who think to blame tle printer exclusively for the blunders that amnse or exasperate I have plenty of proof that , .responsibillty must be much more widely</p>
        <p>!zili,, 1 i , 3 '..t: 4 ,11 , f ci--, ii 1 til it, ...1.‘:;. '4,..',''' ,‘ `1.!.,. .., 1, •• ':''':::,' 1...1 ...' i .. . ':',l:' 1, 1 „</p>
