      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <p>°;. CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE JACK. 1, WA2,(7-cis-we()=2xivw,</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,— Just one line, as the demand on space is very great this week, to say that I shall announce a new competition'next week. There- fore look out for next issue.</p>
        <p>Your loving</p>
        <p>UNCLE JACK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>CONUNDRUMS.</head>
        <p>CONUNDRUMS.</p>
        <p>What two animals do we sleep with?—Two calves. What time of day was Adam born?—A little before Eve. What vegetable is anything but agreeable on board ship?—A leak. Why should a poor man drink coffee?—Be- cause he has no property (tea). When is a soldier charitable When he pre- sents arms (alms). ' Why are cowardly soldiers like tallow candles?—Because they run when exposed to fire. What is that which a dumb man cannot crack?—A joke:</p>
        <p>THE WUFFLED DRESS. I want my dwess with wuffres, And I want my new stwaW hat, And I haven't got the snuffles, And I ain't a fussy-cat. And J will not take my Teddy, • And I will not take my doll, And no one can get me weedy To go anywhere at all.</p>
        <p>And 1 won't be spick and spandy, And I did not lose my cap, And I want some chocolate candy, And I did not miss my nap, • And I will not wear my bonnet, And I will not look at that, And I ain't a-sittin' on it, • And I want my new st,,v-aw hat</p>
        <p>And I will not stop my kiekin', - And I ain't a little tike, And I do not need a Bain', And I don't do what I like, And I won't be quiet, maybe, And I ain't wore out, I 'guess, And I ain't a naughty baby, But I want my wuffied dwess!</p>
        <p>WHEN I GROW UP. Our grocer's clerk comes every day, Though why he should 1 cannot say, For mother mostly orders beans, And soap and mustard, salt and greens. And tea and starch, and lard and rice— Not much of anything that's nice.</p>
        <p>Such food for some folks may seem best, But scarce exciteg`m-Y interest. When I grow up, my grocer's clerk Will 'very seldom need to work; The butcher's boy I'll ask to call Just once in spring and once in fall.</p>
        <p>I'll have the candy boy call twice Each day, and sometimes even thrice! Mornings it will be best, I judge, To order caramels and fudge; At night, a box of chocolate creams, To make sure of pleasant dreams.</p>
        <p>I mean to have the toy-shop man Stop just as often as he can. Now toys grow tiresome soon, you know: And then one's friends do break them so! Heigh-ho! what bliss will fill my cup When I grow up! When I grow up!</p>
        <p>THE 'SONG OF A PENNY. You look on me coldly, You peevishly frown, You frequently tell me I'm only a "brown." One-twelfth of a shilling,</p>
        <p>• Which no one would crave; Too paltry to spend And too meagre to save. •</p>
        <p>Ali"! don't you get fancies So foolish, I pray; Like baseless romances. They'll lead you astray, I wish not to brag, But I beg to declare That I've won many smiles From the shadows of care. And let me inform you My value you'll own. When out of one shilling I linger alone.</p>
        <p>Then frown not upon me, But treasure me, do; I might be, some day, Very welcome to you. I'm constant as can be, And this none will doubt, No sovereign of gold Is so handled about. Each hour in the day Finds a separate. job For the slave-driven Penny Poor twelfth of a—shilling.</p>
        <p>MAKING CALLS. When I go to call with mother</p>
        <p>I sit still in a chair, While ladies talk about my clothe.;, And -wonder at my hair. They ask me silly questions, And I don't know what to say; And there isn't anything to eat, Nor anything to play.</p>
        <p>I like to call on Annie, And go all by myself; For down in Annie's kitchen There is a lovely shelf, Where live big scalloped cookies, All sugary and sweet, And peppermints and lozenges, For Annie's friends to eat.</p>
        <p>And there's a dancing Johnny— She winds him with a key, And sets him on the table To whirl and hop Ter me. She doesn't ask me questions, Nor talk about my dress. But Annie knows what children like, And loves them, too, I guess!</p>
        <p>HE MOVED QUICKLY.</p>
        <p>Colonel Popgun was over in Dublin for his holidays, and found himself with only ten minutes to catch the steamer to England. He hastily hailed a " jarvey," and declared he would give him ten shillings if he performed the journey in time. The jarvey declared he could not do it, albei. his gee was an old war-horse. " War-horse!" sniffed the Colonel. give me the reins and I'll do it myself."</p>
        <p>"Here,</p>
        <p>- - So saying, he jumped on the side of the jaunting-car, and cried "Charge!" When they got to the ship, just in time, he cried, "Halt 1" Next year he happened to be in the same plight, and told the same jarvey to drive him in ten minutes to the quay. " Charge!" shouted the man with the reins, and off they went at a rattling pace. ' Ilia Paddy forgot what to say to stop the</p>
        <p>the Dassword :" " JunlP., yer honour," he cried; " I've forgoi</p>
        <p>JOHNSTON WATSON'S Scientilically titted GLASSES cure HEADACHES.</p>
        <p>SIGHT TESTING OPTICIAN, 84, BRIDGE ST., Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
