      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</p>
        <p>New Books.</p>
        <p>The -Great Society. By Graham Wallas. London: Macmillan and Co., Ltd. net.</p>
        <p>is. 6d.</p>
        <p>The development of mechanical invention has (luring the past hundred years proceeded with such rapidity and on so large a scale that the organisation of the social system has been re- volutionised. In the most advanced countries the main population has been gathered into huge industrial districts, or into.immense.corn7 mercial cities, connected by means of systems of mechanical traction. • Even a country so backward in many respects .as India, has not escaped ecintact with the transforming influ- ence. This enlargement of social scale has enormously increased the complexities of civil- ised life. The individual is faced with an environment of indefinitely larger extent than was the case fifty years ago. He cannot, how- ever greatly he desitzs it, • live as his prede- cessors -lived; he must join the great movement which is rapidly leaving the immediate past Only as a dim 'and distant memory. .For this Irw oganisation of society Mr. - Wallas suggests the convenient term "The Great Society," and he attempts, in the book before us, an analysis of it, from the psychologi- cal point ef view. The book is divided into two parts, the first dealing with the complex dispositions which go to make up what is called 'Human Nature," and the second, Organise: Lion in the Great Society from the three aspects of Thought, Will and • Happiness. Mr. Wallas discusses the present state of Our knowledge with regard to our instincts. . He dissents frcilm the view expressed by Dr. McDougall Hutt Thought is "a merely sub- ordinate mechanism -acting only in obedience to the previous stimulation of one of the simpler instincts," and urges in opposition to this that Thought is a true natural disposition. Mr. Wallas presents his views in an extremely in- teresting and suggestive manner, but we do not think that in a book such ,as this anything like a full discussion of this important question is possible. The chief interest, however, in Mr. Wallas' .book, in our opinion, lies in the second part.. Mr. Wallas bases his analysis of the forms of organisation in the Great Society upon the three forms of consciousness—Cognition, Cons- tion and Feeling—one of which may be dominant in any organisation. In the course of this analysis- he criticises with great pene- tration many of our most cherished institu- tions, and his suggestions for reform. are well worthy of the most careful consideration. The following passage is valuable as an in- stance of the effect which public discussion in the House of Commons has on ,a keen and dis- cerning mind as an Organisation of Thought:— "It was nay duty not long ago to Sit under the gallery of that House throughout a debate which was described by several speakers as exceptionally ' interesting ' and informing.' It was a. private member? day and there was no sign either of party spirit or of obstruction until the end, when the motion was talked- out.' Every one of the forty or fifty melnbers present was for nearly all the time honestly attempting to discuss a difficult question which had not yet become a matter of patty politics. But favourable as the conditions were, I had, as the hours went on, a maddening sense of the utter futility of the ostensible proceedings. When any member sat down after speaking, ten or a dozen 'others sprang up to catch the Speaker's eye.' Whoevei• succeeded delivered a speech which might . have been relevant at the begin- , ning of the debate, but every point of which had by that time usually been made by someone else. The speakers themselves, with their mixture of vague declamation, common-place facts, and the tags by which a practised orator fills up gaps in the sequence, of his thoughts, would never have been recognised by Socrates as belonging to the art of dialectic. One man obviously felt some- thing strongly which had no relation to .the typescript (prepared perhaps a month earlier), which he read with vibrating emphasis. An- other, an experienced open-air agitator, who spoke without notes, rambled on in that loose , manner of trusting that every sentence will sug- • gest another, which constitutes the special disease of working class rhetoric. Another spoke, it seemed to me, both well and to the point, but his arguments had absolutely no effect upon the prepared speeches that followed. The formal question of debate was for most of the time an amendment moved (as I was told across the rail Which divided me from the house) by a member who had to catch a train, and who thereby secured priority for a speech which itself had nothing to do with it. I strove to say to myself the best I could about the British Parliament as a place of concerted thought. I remembered a few occasions when the crowded House had received a real intel- lectual stimulus from the eloquence of a full- dress debate. . . . I thought of that uproar of which I read in some foreign Parliaments, and of the freely whispered accusation of per- smut] corruption in others. But I always re- turned to the point that the House of Commons was sitting for the purpose of organised discus- sion, and that organised discussion was not in any real sense of the term taking place." How just a criticism the above is of debate in the House of Commons only those who have sat through one can appreciate. It is impossible in a short notice to do any_ thins approaching justice to a book of this kind. It is stimulating in the highest degree, and is full of keen criticism of our modern civilisa- tion. Yet behind the criticism one feels the . depth of human sympathy which impels towards l reform and reconstruction, and we may fitly close this brief review of Mr. Wallas' book with this further Quotation : "That, indeed, which Chiefly angers and excites us now, as we eon- template- the society in which we live, is not a conviction that the world is a worse place than it has ever been, but the feeling that we have lost griP• over the course of events and are stupidly wasting the power over nature which ralgitt make the world infinitely better."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>CHURCHES' OPEN-AIR SERVICE.</head>
        <p>CHURCHES' OPEN-AIR SERVICE.</p>
        <p>Nonconformists Unite at a Bridge Foot Gathering.</p>
        <p>A well-attended meeting, promoted by the Warrington Free Church Council, was held on Sunday night on the open space at the Bridge Foot end of Knutsford-road, Mr. R. Corbett pre- siding. The Rev. H. Ogden and the Rev. A. J. Chessum were the speakers. The Rev. H. Ogden, referring to the war, asked if it was not possible that some chastise- ment was upon the country for the national sins. When the Son of Man was betrayed into, the bands of sinners it was Judas who gave Him the kiss, and many have said that if the Son of Man must' die and be sacrificed He must be betrayed. and Judas was only acting as an agent in that scheme. Let them remember that in tiod's Holy Word it was Stated that offences must come, hut woe unto that man by whom they came., That may be equally true of the Kaiser, and that God would punish the betrayer, and yet with the other would inflict punish- ment upon nations which had forgotten. Him. Who could say that their national life had been free from selfishness? They had fostered bar- barism in their minds and found a witness to their national laxity even in their attitude to the Sabbath—the Sabbath desecration was now almost becoming a national custom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>RETURN THANKS.</head>
        <p>RETURN THANKS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. J. Chessum asked the gathering to try to think of calmer things, and directed their attention to the New Testament narrative in which nine lepers who were cleansed went a way without returning a word of thanks, and the tenth went back and stated his gratitude unto His Lord. The preacher ventured to be- lieve that there were many in the assembly who 'belonged to the nine. The Church Was by no means guiltless; thankfulness amongst Chris- tians was not their leading virtue. A few days ago people were in a panic because of their daily bread. The rich and 3eesibtijelxietymleilyon fmsboe selfish loaded their cellars, and just for a moment the notion was faced deprived of their bread, hdthaendpos, churchgoers due regard for God's care did not eNcite them to thankfulness. Suitable hymns were sung.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>RELIGIOUS BELIEF.</head>
        <p>RELIGIOUS BELIEF.</p>
        <p>Rev. C. P. Pitt Commences his Ministry.</p>
        <p>AN IMPRESSIVE SERMON.</p>
        <p>, Large numbers attend4d the Elmwood-aYenue Congregational Church on Sunday, when the Rev. C. P. Pitt commenced his ministry. In the ,morning the iireacher took "for his text: St. John xii. 36—" While ye have light, believe in the light, that ye may be the children of light." Chiefly for "intuitive" reasons, con- tinued the Ereacher, he would alter the first word to "as," for the text as it stood gave one</p>
        <p>the impression that the "light" was only temporal, and the teaching of Jesus Christ showed it to be eternal. •</p>
        <p>Divine Methods.</p>
        <p>Had Jesus so desired, He could, by working upon the minds of the people, have made them believe Him to be king and•set Him up above the Roman Empire. This He preferred not to do, however; and .so tha Jews, 'looking for an earthly Messiah, were disappointed that He did not become reconciled with .their ideas.</p>
        <p>In their text they found three definite state- ments—Light, Belief, and Action in Becoming. As to the first: What did Jesus mean by "the ! light"? This He had Himself answered when He said "I am the light . . . and the way." The two sayings had been connected in the revised version by the alteration of the text to: "While ye have the light, ..tc.," which was, of course, more significant. With reference to belief, Jesus came to earth with a unictue message and an abstract prin- ciple. . There was a fact which existed in the world, and we must believe in that fact. We had to pass our lives upon some rock of belief, by nature, in the time of calamity on peril. At the same time we should not despise those who believed in God in ways differing from ours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>Belief and Transformation.</head>
        <p>Belief and Transformation.</p>
        <p>In; the third statement, our Lord just said that if we believed in a thing, we tended to become transformed by the object we had in mind. If we thought upon God, we should, unconsciously, become God-like. Sometimes we found men who desired to be- come religious in life, but who yet felt that religion was not logic; they could not under- stand it, and therefore they must pass it by. But what did we understand by logic? There was not only the reason of the mind; there was also the reason of the heart. We felt that religion rung true in our own lives, by the reason of the heart; the reason of the mind came after. And if we were going to lead a religious life, we must first answer the ques- tion—"Boes it ring true in the heart?" We could then believe in the light, that we might be the children of light.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>CHOICE OF LIFE. Service in the Evening.</head>
        <p>THE CHOICE OF LIFE. Service in the Evening.</p>
        <p>At the evening service Mr. Pitt took for his text : Daniel iii. 15—" Now if ye be ready that at what time ye hear . . . all kinds of musick, ye fall down and worship the image which I have made; well : but if ye worship not, ye shall be cast the same hour into the midst of a burning fiery furnace; and Who is that God that shall deliver you out of my. hands?" After recalling the Biblical story of how the. three Christians were 'cast into the fire, the preacher remarked that the anger of Nebuchadnezzar was the anger of the tyrant who had been opposed. The situation was a very difficult one for the three victims, and one which we had, found since those days. Men had been ordered to • bow down before the idols which the world had set up, and many noble men of our time had gone through suffering rather than betray their God. These men, then, realised that there was a flaw in the proposals made to them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Gift of Choice.</head>
        <p>The Gift of Choice.</p>
        <p>He wished to speak upon the choice of life. The child given a choice between two apples invariably chose the larger one; but the choice of life called for careful consideration. There often came a time when we felt that before proceeding further we must think where we were going. It was a difficult question to decide, \ and it needed a great deal of moral courage to decide rightly. Not only was the choice of life restricted to us, however. Even in the case of the Master the Devil came to tempt; but Jesus answered: "Get thee be- hind me, Satan." In the same way we always had the choice of two things, and the ansW"er lay within ourselves—the power to decide rightly. The choir, under the conductorship of Mr. W. H. Wallington, ably rendered the anthem "Day of Light" (Nichol).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>Pastor and His Work.</head>
        <p>Pastor and His Work.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the service Mr. Pitt said that before they departed he would like to refer to the commencement of his new work. Now, it seemed to him, was the time to thank all those who had worked so hard in the past to uphold their Church. Many Wyclifte friends, he knew, were present, and he wished also to thank them for the help they had so ungrudgingly given. They would soon be building a new church, and he felt sure that that help would again be forthcoming.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>ENGINEER TO PASTOR.</head>
        <p>ENGINEER TO PASTOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pitt, who is a native of Manchester, was formerly engaged in an enc drawing office, but' relinquishedneering works d that position after a time, and took a seven years' training course for the Congregational ministry at the Lancashire College, Manchester. At the end of that period he graduated Bachelor of Arts at Manchester University, and now commences his first pastoral work in the midst of the develop- ing locality a Pialgate, where he .has received an enthusiastic welcome. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>RUNCORN DISTRICT COUNCIL. Medical Officer's Report.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN DISTRICT COUNCIL. Medical Officer's Report.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. C. E. Linaker, sea,, presiding. The Clerk read a letter from Dr. T. A. Mur- ray, of Penketh, secretary of the Warrington Division of the British Medical Association, with respect to Dr. Adams' duties as Medical Officer of Health while away on active service. He stated that Dr. Adams had left the matter in their hands, and Dr. Joseph, Medical Officer of Health for Warrington, had kindly consented to do Dr. Adams' work, if this was agreeable to the Council. The Clerk further stated that Dr. Meredith Young, the County Medical Officer, had sent a communication recommending Dr. Burton, of Frodsham, to act in the stead of Dr. Adams, whilst b 4 was away. Mr. Woodward proposed Dr. Burton he aP- pointed, and this was agreed to. Mr. Woodward proposed, and Mr. G. Pilling seconded, that a vote of thanks be accorded the Warrington Division of the British Medical Association for their kind offer. This was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>FOR EMPLOYMENT'S SAKE.</head>
        <p>FOR EMPLOYMENT'S SAKE.</p>
        <p>A circular letter Was read from the Home Office enclosing copy of the following resolution, passed at a 'conference of the leading London retail traders at the Home Office:— 'That it is neither desirable nor necessary 41 existing circumstances to dismiss any assistants, but that if economies in the carrying on of business are necessary they should be Met by other means." The circular continued by stating that the Secretary of State hoped that the resolution would be generally acted upon by retail traders throughout the country. If this were done, any serious difficulties in connection with em- , ployment, so far as the retail trades were con- cerned. would he avoided, and there was every reason to suppose that in a short time these trades would return to normal conditions. A circular letter was also read asking that if there was any need for housing, the matter would be pressed forward. This was to enable builders to be employed. These and similar matters were left in the hands of a Special Executive Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>XGUST 29, 1914.</head>
        <p>XGUST 29, 1914.</p>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>AFTER A LOVERS' TIFF.</p>
        <p>Younu Man Found on the River Banh.</p>
        <p>A young man who was acting in a suspicious manner on the riverside in Wharf-street was arrested by a constable at midnight on Monday and brought before the local magistrates on Tuesday morning. The man, James Peter Marsh, a labourer, 2, Dutton-street, was charged with loitering with intent to commit a felony. The Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls) said that shortly before midnight the prisoner was seen by P.C. Willis to be acting in a sus- picious manner near Bishop's Wharf. Whilst</p>
        <p>the officer was keeping observation on the pris- oner two civilians passed the wharf, and on seeing them Marsh immediately hid hiMself. When the officer went towards the prisoner he ran away, but was subsequently captured and searChed. Amongst the articles found in his possession was a postcard—addressed to his mother—which the officer had seen prisoner writing, and which showed that he intended to jump in the river. It would appear, added the Chief Constable, that the prisoner had had some disagreement with the young woman with whom he had been keeping company. He suggested that a short adjourninent should be ordered in order to have a Medical examination. So far as the police' . knew, the prisoner's character Was very good, and he was really detained in the police build- ing so that be would do no harm to himself.. ' Prisoner's mother was called early that morn: ing and informed about her son's movements. and, added the Chief, she stated that if she had been there she would have pushed him in. When the mother was called at the court she said she did not think her son had any inten- tion of taking his life, and she promised to look after:, him. She said, in ansWer to the Chief Constable, that she did not Mean what she had said to the police about pushing her son in the river.</p>
        <p>The prisoner was committed to the Work- house on a 14 days' order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr, C. Jackson Ifolmes.(in the chair) and</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Pemberton.</p>
        <p>, Struck a baby.—What the magistrates char- ' acteriseil as a brutal and cowardly act came to light in connection with a charge of assault brought against Frank Allen, a man- .who travels about with a street organ, and at pre- sent lives in a lodging-house in Dial-street In addition to the charge of assaulting two women in Back Porter-street, it was stated that he struck a baby and he was also drunk and dis- orderly.—A police officer spoke to his arrest of the accused, who was 'very violent. —Sophia Conn, 29, Back Porter-street, said on Tuesday she heard that Allen had struck her baby and knocked it out of her sister's arms. When. she went to/him Allen struck her twice on the mouth and 'twice on the head.—Diana Smith, of the same address, said she Was a single woman, and used to live with Allen, who treated her .most cruelly, and she left him. On Tuesday she Met him. He knocked her sister's baby out of her arms and struck her repeatedly._ The Chief Constable said in 1912 Allen deserted from the 2nd Battalion South Lancashire Regi- ment, and since had been going about North Wales with an organ. He had been senten.,:s4 to nine months by the military authorities fca. deserting and then given his ticket. He had shamefully ill-treated the young woman.—Pri- soner was sentenced to two months, with hard labour.</p>
        <p>Tale of a Spade.—Charles Patrick Roddy, 74, an old-age pensioner, was charged with stealing a spade from an ironmonger's in Horsemarket- stVeet. This spade, which was Valued at 3s. 6d., he pledged for is., stating that he was a gardener, but was now unable to work.— Detective-Sergeant Reddington said when he arrested the old man he said that he bought the spade for 6d., pledged it for is., and sold the ticket in a public-house for 3d. The detective complained that the deputy-at Horn's lodging-house, 3, Cloth Hall, where accused lodged had tried to prevent him getting infor- mation about the case.—Roddy was dismissed with a caution and the deputy was called up and censured by the magistrates for not assist- ing the police as he was in duty bound to do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>FRIDAY.</head>
        <p>FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. S. Wright (in the chair), Aldermen Pierpoint, Smethurst nd Bolton, Hr. Joseph, Messrs. G. Pilling and W. Furness. " Like a Gentleman."—William Ashton; no fixed abode, was charged with being drunk in Buttermarket-street on Thursday.—A police constable said that accused was very drunk, ,kicked some children in the street, and injured them.—Accused: If I had been drunk I would have said so like a gentleman.—He was fined ss. and ordered to pay 4s. 6d. costs, with the option of seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>More Money Required for the Next Half-year.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly- meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held on Wednesday at the Workhouse, Mr. A. J. Jolley presiding. At a meeting of the Finance Committee it was recommended that the estimate of expendi- ture for the half-year ending March 30th, 1915, be £17,920. The estimated balance in favour Of the Guardians at the end of the present half- Year is .4:1,415. The committee desired to point out that was' half the amount of the grants which will be received in March next had been taken into account in the estimates, and owing to the increase in the amount required as compared with the amount levied upon the Overseers during the corresponding period of last year, they considered that the full amount of the grants should be taken into consideratiou on that particular occasion. They therefore recommended that calls be made upon . the Overseers for the several townships within the Union for the slim of .216',720. The call for the corresponding period was ,t 15,340.</p>
        <p>The committee, in recommending the min- utes, said they had carefully considered the estimates and from the information laid before them they were satisfied that the same shoule be approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>INCREASE ALL ALONG THE LINE.</head>
        <p>INCREASE ALL ALONG THE LINE.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell said, there was an . increase all along the line, except in the ease of vaccinations. He would like an explanation with regard to the 'large increase of £426 in the sundry payments and contingencies, and , also the profits on the wood, stone and farming account There was one other point he had drawn their attention to before, and that was ' that alcohol supplied to the hospital ought to be shown separately. The Local Government Board did not encourage its use, and had laid' down strong rules as to its use. The Chairman said the last point had been dealt with on so many occasions that he need not make a statement Mr. Mitchell had made his protest, as usual. With regard to the second item the estimate was the same as for last year, 'but if they could raise a profit in exess of the estimate it would all well and good. Referring to the sundry payments and contingencies' item it would be noticed that the estimate was -t5OO instead of the I'so pre- viously. The figure was put at ,t5OO to enable them to meet any trouble which might happen during the coming winter. Mr. Mitchell: I was wonderini; wiletliear aught had provided sufficient in view of what happen. The Chairman: We hope for the hest :Arr. 'Mather (jocularly): Mr. Mitchell might have said that at 'first. The Chairman' said that to satisfy uneasy ecnsciences he would mention that the, pinare Committee came to its decision after a long discussion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>DISTRICT iIEWS i</head>
        <p>DISTRICT iIEWS i</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH GREAT SANg:</p>
        <p>uesday, an Army-- along Liverpool-road shortly aaftere none Army Waggon Cellides.—Whilst7 tr 3 Arm .0001 eneed a • w. mishap just after arrival at ,s 0 temporary bridge, here the new St. HetlreanislsC7arntall;gatir,eP: fie:Liverpool-road turning t constructed. Having safely passeci,,°lo t the vehielo faila'a ~a successful's r recipitated into some railway's, a wary partlyto the demolished. Happily l no inja,:t., done to e driver, who proc afteeded ou al er a short *nal , p b,</p>
        <p>Money Borrowing Sanctioned.—At the meet- ing of the Runcorn Rural District Council at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday a letter was read from the Local Government Board sanc- tioning the borrowing of .2705 for street-making in the township of Walton Inferior.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Lady's Generosity.—At the meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians at ,Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday , the minutes of the Children's Home Committee contained a vote of thanks to Mrs. Vere Davies, of Stockton Heath, for the gift of a rocking horse to the home.</p>
        <p>Wesleyans' New Organ.—The s new y o Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church 1,-, on Thursday afternoon by the doliat Db3o, dtdhse, oßfePv .e ukklebtehri ElHild. wa serviceo f 0 aly s con, e 3.1.0rnf the organist being Mr. H. S. 13r°11e'rce ',Slum Wesleyan Church. Tea was s In the schoolroom at a nominal charge e . co vening a musical service was held, Mr. C. E. Parker presided, and an orgalibt was given by Mr. 0. G. Smith, of the °IigNII t(obnar Levi Richardson (tenor) and Ernest a"11 iCtoh:erch. Solos were rendered bY tt'l address. )s, easuecilatlhceirßcuevit. pre Aministers..'illickeerre</p>
        <p>Man Injured by Army Transport—During the week hundreds of motor waggons, requisi- tioned by the military authorities, passed through Stdckton Heath, driven by soldiers. To one of these an accident occurred on Tues- day, on the London-road. Samuel Sant, of 76, Walton-road, Stockton Heath, who is stated to have ridden as far as the Golf Links in one of the vehicles, was run over whilst trying to alight, and his leg was badly lacerated. After attention by Dr. Bowers, of Stratton, the in- jured man was conveyed to the Warrington Infirmary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>VILLAGE</head>
        <p>A VILLAGE</p>
        <p>Miss Margaret MelnlyTe Married to</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. Walton.</p>
        <p>A wedding which aroused much interest in I Stockton Heath was solemnised at St. Thomas' Church on Tuesday, and a large congregation witnessed the ceremony in the ancient church. The couple are well known in the village. The bride was Miss Margaret Mclntyre, eldest daughter of Councillor William Mclntyre, of the Red Lion Hotel, Stockton Heath, and the hridegroona was Mr. Charles Ernest Hatton, youngest son of Mrs. Shillcock, of the Mulberry Tree Hotel, Victoria-square. Councillor Mc- Intyre, the bride's father, is chairman of the Parish Council and a member of the Runcorn Rural District Council and Board of Guardians. He is also a sidesoian at the church where the ceremony took place. Mrs. Henry Grcenall had very kindly under- taken the decoration of the interior Of the church, and the altar was effectively and taste- fully decorated with marguerites, phlox, sweet peas, ferns, &amp;c. The officiating clergy were the Revs. H. V. Pigot (rector of Grappenhall) and E. W. Clarke (curate of Stockton Heath). Mr: -Fred Rose presided at the organ, and gave suitable musi- cal selections, including the Bridal March from " Lobengrin," and Mendelssohn's Wedding March. _ _</p>
        <p>The bride, who was given away by her father, was attired in a beautiful dress of ivory satin crepe de chene, with lace tunic and pearl trim- mings. The wreath she wore was of orange blossom and white heather. . She carried a bouquet of marguerites; lilies of the valley and whit:i heather, and wore a gold wrist watch, the gift of the bridegroom. The bridesmaids were the Misses Ethel and Mary Mclntyre (sisters of the bride). They wore dresses of shell-pink silk, with flowered ninon tunics. Their hats were of white satin broche, and they carried shower bouquets of sweet peas, wearing pearl necklets, also the gifts of the bridegroom. The duties of best man were discharged by Mr. Frank Mclntyre (brother of the bride), and Mr. Lynn Whitehouse acted as groomsman. The reception and wedding breakfast were held in the Hall Cafe, after which Mr. and Mrs. Hatton left for Llandudno, where the honeymoon is being 'spent. The bride's tailor-made travelling costume was of navy blue, trimmed with panne velvet; her hat was of black velvet, with pink feather. Many useful" and valuable presents have been received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>Church Collections.—On Sunda the collec- tions were taken for the National Distress Fund, when over ,150 was contributed. The Congregational Church is taking a collection for the same object to-morrow.</p>
        <p>Congregational Pastor's Return.—The Rev. J. A. Penaliggon preached at the Lymm Congrega- tional Church on Sunday for the first time since his prolonged illness. The previous Sunday he gave a discourse at the Buclaiow Hill Church. His return to active work is very much web er med..</p>
        <p>Prayers for the Soldiers.—An intercessory ser- vice was held in the Parish Church on Friday evening last week, when there was a very good attendance, composed of members of all the Churches in the-township. The Rector read a list of the men from the neighbourhood who had left for duty, either in the Territorials or the Regular Army.</p>
        <p>Funeral of the Late Dr. .Wright—The funeral of the late Dr. Jarvas Wright, of Broomedge, took place at Rostherne on Wednesday after- noon, when numerous tokens of respect were manifested. The deceased, who was in his 71st year, expired suddenly on Saturday, and when the news became known great regret was ex- Pressed with the relatives. Dr. Wright was exceedingly well known in the district, where he will be greatly missed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>Caught Up the free.—At the Warringte Occasional Court on Monday, Austin Monaghan and Albert Boswell, of Thelwall-lane,. Latch- ford. pleaded guilk to a charge of stealing a quantity of pears from the orchard of Mr. Albert Chamberlain, a Grappenhall farmer.— P.C. Jones said he found the men up a tree, taking the fruit, 'whilst three other men kept watch in the road.—The Chairman (Mr: J. A. Ransome) said no doubt a great deal of orchard robbery was committed. • Defendants seemed to go out for the purpose, and they would each be fined is. and costs (7s. 6d.).—The money was paid.</p>
        <p>Independent Methodist Church.—The anniver- sary sermons were held on Sunday. The special' preacher was the Rev. T. Sherwood, late princi- pal of West Didsbury College, who delivered ex- cellent discourses to large congregations. In the afternoon a young people's service was held. The Rev. J. S. Chedburn, of Latchford Baptist Church, gave the address. The soloist was Mr. A. Pearce, who rendered two baritone solos in an efficient style. Special hymns and anthems, "0 Lord my God" and "Make a joyful noise," were creditably rendered by the choir under the baton of Mr. Gough. Mr. T. Leah presided at the organ. The collections realised the satis- factory total of £l4, which were for church expenses.</p>
        <p>Signs Recognised By Every Boy. Bobby: "There's visitors in the front room." Polly; "How d' you know?" Bobby: "Ma's callin' pa ' dear ' ey'ry time she speaks to. him."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
