      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 29, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>t 1 40 0:0 4:4 •:* 40 •I + .?" • 4:0 41 14: 4:0 0,14 • 41: I 1.% 1..0 0:4 111 0:: 4:: I •4 4,0, *.:.• 0:0 *TO I f:* • 611 1:0 41 40 11,) 40/ 4" IC• 010 4:11 4) 4:1 •:* 4:,* • • 0:* • 0:0 40 .) CO 40 441* 4:1</head>
        <p>t 1 40 0:0 4:4 •:* 40 •I + .?" • 4:0 41 14: 4:0 0,14 • 41: I 1.% 1..0 0:4 111 0:: 4:: I •4 4,0, *.:.• 0:0 *TO I f:* • 611 1:0 41 40 11,) 40/ 4" IC• 010 4:11 4) 4:1 •:* 4:,* • • 0:* • 0:0 40 .) CO 40 441* 4:1</p>
        <p>PREPARATIONS FOR FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>THE N.U. GAME.</p>
        <p>The Professionals have a Preliminary</p>
        <p>Canter.</p>
        <p>PLAYERS WHO HAVE SIGNED ON.</p>
        <p>BY "SCRUM."</p>
        <p>To-day week witnesses the opening of the football season, and never in the history of the winter's pastime has it opened amidst such conditions as mark the advent of the present season. We have football with us each Septem- ber, but fortunately we have not a great Euro- pean war each season. The past few weeks have been a ,trying time for all football club officials. For a period they hardly knew where they stood, as at one time it looked as if the game would have to be aban- doned until the end of the war. The Warring- ton N.U. Committee have found themselves in a dilemma like all the rest of the football organisations, but they are now in the position to announce that the programme arranged will be carried through in its entirety. Personally, I think this is a very wise deci- sion, although one does occasionally run up against a croaker who exclaims: " Imagine playing football while our soldiers are playing with death on the battlefield." As a rule a little further investigation shows that such a croaker never goes to see a football match, and further he has no ambition to seek " glory " on the battlefield. Therefore it is satisfactory to learn that our local N.U. club are not to heed this type of objection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>BAD FOR THE CLUBS.</head>
        <p>BAD FOR THE CLUBS.</p>
        <p>One cannot ignore the fact that the war will prove bad for the clubs. So far as can be ascer- tained there is not one in the League which has not lost some of its prominent players, called up to fight for King and country. Warrington has lost at least four, two from the first and two from the second strings. Nicholas and Skel- home will be abs6ntees from the first team, both men whom the club can ill afford to lose. Naylor and Shepherd will be absent from the Combination team. _</p>
        <p>Judging from the nature of the practice games the Warrington Club have, however, plenty of reserves, and it will be possible to put a League team into the field able to do itself justice against even such a side as Huddersfield and Oldham promise to turn out. Of course, it is premature to say at present how the team will be built up, that will be decided by the committee after the full dress rehearsal this afternoon, but I am confident that the committee will put the best possible side forward, and will do everything possible to maintain interest locally in the game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>FINE FIXTURE CARD.</head>
        <p>A FINE FIXTURE CARD.</p>
        <p>The fixture card is an attractive one, although neither of the Hull teams figure in the list. On Saturday next we open the programme with the annual match at Leigh. This is quite an institution in the annals of the clubs, and this year the venue is at Leigh. Then on the Mon- day following comes the stiffest task of the season, the annual match at Huddersfield, 'where, as the Feast Holiday is on, there is sure to he the customary "gate" of over 20,000. The first borne match is that with Broughton Ran- gers on the 12th September, and after the lat- ter's sensational finish last season more than usual interest will attach to this match. Roch- dale are at Wilderspool the following Saturday, 19th September. The "A" team will give the local patrons a taste of the game at Wilderspool on Saturday, when they entertain Leigh, and the two next Saturdays they are at Broughton Rangers and Rochdale respectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>TO-DAY'S PRACTICE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S PRACTICE.</p>
        <p>There ought to be a good crowd at Virilderspool this afternoon to witness the final practice match. Two strong teams will take the field. These have not yet been arranged, and will be selected before the time of kick-off, which has been fixed for 3.30. An interesting game should ensue, and the on- lookers will be able to form a good judgment of the capabilities of the men. There is a small charge made for admission, and the takings will go to the Prince of Wales' War Fund, so roll up and help that noble cause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>M'arringlon NA'orking Men's</p>
        <p>Associatibn.</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>The Centre match took place at Norton last week-end, and although the winner had only 4oz. 6dr., it was full of interest throughout. The winner was found in H. Moore, with four fish, M. Evans coming second, with three ii i weigh- ing 3oz. 12dr. During the latter part of the contest ii . Moore was confident he would "get through,'' as he had the majority, and fish were off feed. He. said, "I'll get through if someone does not catch a riddler " (eel). How near those remarks were to being true! For J. Bryers, who had only one fish at the call of time, caught an eel of 6oz. or 70z. fifteen seconds after the secretary had called the closure. All competitors sympathised with him on his hard luck, but he took matters as a thorough sportsman, and said the right man was going to the championship to represent War- rington. The grand final takes place at Calveley, in the Shropshire Union Canal, on September 12th. The spot is a favourite place of H. Moore, so Warrington will be well represented, and all his clubmates wish him every success. It is some five or six years since the honour was brought to Warrington by the late Centre secretary, F. Singleton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>PROVINCIAL SHIELD COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>PROVINCIAL SHIELD COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Members desirous of entering the above com- petition must give in their names to the Centre secretary or J. Bryers. They must state at the time which house they intend to fish for; it must be understood that the competition is only for members who are connected with the provincial anglers of Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>GENERAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>GENERAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>A general meeting will be held at the Glad- stone Arms on Wednesday next, September 2nd, at 8 p.m., to decide where the annual match shall be fished. The N.A. of Warrington have always allowed members to decide these matters for themselves. There will be no postcards sent out atfer this notice, so it is up to members who wish the match taken to any particular place to put in an appearance.</p>
        <p>THE BAY HORSE MATCH. The above society\ hold their annual match at Aintree to-morrow. Therd is a fine array of Prizes to be competed for, which should draw a good entry i—First, tea urn; second, hunter watch; third, clock; the other prizes go, we take it, as usual: after the first three the next in weight has his choice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>NOTICE.</head>
        <p>NOTICE.</p>
        <p>Will members who are weighing in specimens from the Gowy state at what particular part the are taken, as there are specials on the list for species taken from the lengths rented by the Northern Anglers? There is no difficulty in knowing the length rented by the N.A., as they are described in all travelling cards. A.S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <p>CLABEE'S B 41 PILLS can be relied upon to ore in either sex all acquired or constitutional dis- charges from the Urinary Organs, Gravel and Paine in the back. Free from Mercury. Established up. wards of 60 years. In boxes 4s. 6d. each, of aIl chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors throultout the world, or sent for sixty stamps by the makers.— The Lincoln and Midland Counties Drug Cc, Li- .pls.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Players Help the Mayor's War</p>
        <p>Fund.</p>
        <p>CLUBS GETTING READY.</p>
        <p>BY "LINESMAN."</p>
        <p>Now that the advent of the winter pastime is at hand, all the football players throughout the town are eagerly waiting for the official note to commence hostilities. There still remains a week of the close season, and thus it gives the committee and players of the various clubs a splendid opportunity of playing trial games with each other, and devoting the receipts of such games W the Mayor's War Fund, and the same could be forwarded direct to the War Fund Committee or to the Warrington and District League secretary, Mr. R. Bvandreth, Orford, • Warrington, when the total amount received could he forwarded in a lump sum to the official headquarters in Warrington.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>At Stockton Heath on Saturday a match was</p>
        <p>played on the West Avenue enclosure between the Heathens and the Wilderspool Brewery. The game proved to be ve-1•37 one-sided, and the Heathens ran out easy winners by eight goals to none. The gate receipts amounted to 7s. 6d., and will be handed over to the Stockton Heath Committee of the Mayor's War Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>LATCHFORD CLUB.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD CLUB.</p>
        <p>The Latchford A.F.C. Committee have ar- ranged a football match to be played on the Thelwall-lane ground on September sth, the pro- ceeds of which are to be given to the Mayor's War Fund. If the conditions permit they have arranged to play a team selected from the Royal Welsh Fusiliers, who are encamped near. the Latchford Locks. This should prove to be a very attractive fixture and draw a good crowd to witness the game. There is sure to be some promising new players on view who have signed on for the Latchford Club, and will endeavour to show their best form in trying to gain a place in the club's first team ranks to fill the vacan- cies caused by the departure of E. Webb, P. Webb and Downs, who have been called up to join the Territorials in the present crisis.</p>
        <p>ELM WOOD AVENUE CONGREGATIONAL. The annual general meeting of the above club proved to be a very enthusiastic gathering, when the following gentlemen were elected as officers for the coming season: President, Rev. T. T. James; vice-presidents, Messrs. Ziha Armitage, W. H. Wallington, J. Longland, Rev. C. P. Pitt, Councillor J. Hawthorn, J. A. Slack, B. Farrar, J. Reid, A. Berry, F. Morrall, G. T. Rushton, C. A. Shaw, C. Eccles and E. S. Garland; chair- man, Mr. A. Ashton; treasurer, Mr. Jos. Phoe- nix, sen.; hon. secretary, Mr. John Phoenix, 82, Dalton Bank, Warrington; a committee of ten; captain, James Phoenix; sub-captain, H. Hazel- dine.</p>
        <p>Last season the club only played friendly fix- tures, and after an interesting discussion as to the welfare of the club and its players, it was decided to join the Warrington and District League (Second Division), for which applica- tion had been successful. The team will have the use of one of the schoolrooms for dressing accommodation, which is near to their ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>ORFORD WHITE STAR.</head>
        <p>ORFORD WHITE STAR.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the above club was held on Friday night, when there was a good attendance of members. The report for last season's .accounts was accepted as very satisfactory. The following gentlemen were appointed officers for the coming season: President, Mr. J. Ashton; treasurer, Mr. F. Eaton; committee, Messrs. Gleaves, Buckley, Rudge and Jones; hon. secre- tary, Mr. T. Turner, 37, Forshaw-street, War- rington. Application has been made and accepted for membership to the Warrington and District League in the Second Division, and fixtures for the coming season are arranged. The signing on of players has been very satisfactory to the club's committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>ORFORD ST. JOHN'S.</head>
        <p>ORFORD ST. JOHN'S.</p>
        <p>A general meeting of the Orford St. John's Club was held on Tuesday night at the Orford Hotel. Mr. H. Ashton presided. The following were elected as officers: President, Lord Wol- mer ; chairman, Mr. H. Ashton; hon. secretary and treasurer, Mr. J. Smith, 21, Norris-street, Warrington; groundsman, Mr. G. Tomlinson; captain, A. Meachin ; sub-captain, William Millward ; and a committee of eight. The chili has joined the Warrington and District League 'First Division, and also the Widnes Association League. The present war crisis has affected the club's playing strength in several positions, hut good substitutes have been signed on, including Messrs. S. Caldwell, P. Pilling, R. Spencer, F. Horrocks, J. Brown and G. Ogden. The following teams have been selected for a trial match to-day : Blues : J. Warburton; P. Pilling and W. Mill- . ward ; J. Marshall, J. Denny and J. Devine; L. Thomas, W. Stretford, A. Meachin, W. nankin- son and W. Smalley. Clarets: T. Kay; C. Harrison and J. Brown; R. Spencer, W. Stratford and S. Caldwell; W. Warburton, W. Collier, G. Ogden, C. Dunbob- bin and J. Smith. Referee, Mr. S. C. Essenhigh. Reserves: R. Millward, J. Dooley, J. Savage and Spilsbury. It is intended to give in our next issue (if space permits) a few notes on the. clubs and their prospects for the coming season in the Warring- ton and District League (Second Division).</p>
        <p>GOLF.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>WARRINGTON CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CLUB.</p>
        <p>The fourth and last qualifying round for the Henry Fairclough Memorial was played on Saturday, and resulted in the three following members qualifying with scores as under:- 0. H. N. J. Hepherd F. Barber A. J. Monks For the fourth place, three members tied with scores as below :</p>
        <p>G. H. N. E. A. Connor B4 6 SO J. Fairclough A. Knowles This tie will be played off as soon as possible. The remaining scores were as follow :</p>
        <p>H. H. N. W. Greenhouse 92 40 82 C. W. Moore 9B 16 82 A. Bottomley J. M. Murray The final will be 36 boles, medal play, and wi: take place on Saturday, September 26th.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON GOLF CLUB. Ladies' Competition. The qualifying round for the captain's prize was played against " bogey " on Wednesday, with the following results : Miss M. Hepherd Miss M. Dolan Miss M. Bainbridge Mrs.. Bedell Mrs. E. A. White Mrs. Birtles • Miss D. Picton Miss G. Sloane</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <p>T CAN'T BEAR TO HEAR YOUR CHILD CRY. Keep his stomach in order and you will soon find his temper improved. Next time he seems irritable, try a Steedman's Powder. Nothing else is just as good,, Observe the EE in Steedman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <p>.• t</p>
        <p>ishly ignoring the new regulations dealing with aliens. They think that long residence in this country renders them immune from interfer- ence, but this is not the case; and last week an English-born woman, the widow of a Ger- man, was fined in London for failing to register herself. A married woman is of the same nationality as her husband, even though she may have become a widow, but a British or Irish-born woman in this position may obtain a certificate of re-admission to British nation- ality without taking out naturalisation papers, which cost 4:3. German women, or those in the position of a German alien, are liable to a penalty of six months' imprisonment, or a fine of .tlOO, for failing to register themselves, and afterwards they can be arrested, and even shot, if found at a distance of more than five miles from their registered address without a special permit.</p>
        <p>The Jubilee of the Duke of Athol. An interesting and unusual celebration, that has been shorn of its chief functions by the war, was the jubilee of the accession of the Duke of Athol to the title and estates. The Duke received numerous gifts, and among them was his portrait, painted by Sir J. Guthrie. The large garden party the Duke had arranged to give was only one of the social functions that had to be abandoned. The heir, Lord Tune- bardine, and his two brothers are all absent with the Army in France.</p>
        <p>Economy in Mourping. All black materials will, in view of the war, doubtless advance in price, and the cost of mourning will fall heavily on thousands of families if the war lasts for any length of time. It has been suggested that those families whose relatives fall in battle should wear purple bands only on the sleeves of ordinary coats and dresses. But at such a time there is something incongruous in coloured garments that there is little likelihood of the idea being generally adopted. The dyer, like the manu- facturer, has made great strides in his -art of late years, and many people will prefer to send their coloured dresses and suits—if unable to afford new mourning—to be dyed, rather than wear garments of gladness with minds so little</p>
        <p>attuned to gaiety in any form. The Wedding Dress. Soutache is seen again On autumn and winter coats, suits, and dresses. It is often employed in very bright colours and in gold; passemen- terie and incrustations of various kinds have also been revived for the decoration of elaborate garments for outdoor wear. Very handsome designs in Soutache appeared on some dresses that formed part of a beautiful trousseau, pre- pared for a bride whose marriage was hastened on account of the war. The wedding dress of the moment is rarely without slight touches of colour, that relieve the monotony of pure white, which some girls find trying to the com- plexion. Lace is used very much for trains and ils, and the many transparent materials, now so much in' vogue, make ideal wedding dresses. - Styles also adapt themselves specially to the bride's most important gown, the tunic adding to the dignity of the whole, whilst the sash makes a becoming finish to the corsage and skirt.</p>
        <p>This Season's Bathing Costumes. Now that confidence is in a measure restored, and the seaside resorts of these islands are pronounced perfectly safe from attack, many families are going to the seaside, and bathing is in full swing. Bathing dresses always re- flect more or less the styles of every-day clothes, and this year the long tunic, the broad sash, and the kimono bodice have invaded the bathing outfit. The bathing cloak also is cavalier in shape. Many serviceable bathing dresses designed for swimming, not for mere show on the beach, are quite becoming cos- tumes, when made of soft, fine serge, good alpaca, or fine stockingette. Tussore, and more especially the stronger kinds of shantung, in royal blue, and other charming colours, make ideal bathing gowns in the tunic style, with kimono bodices. The seams in these pretty garments are veined with a cord-like edge, that is very ornate, and for which a crochet needle is requisitioned. The caps worn with these dresses are no longer weird and unsightly affairs, but pretty silk and satin covered head- gear, made to match the gowns in colour, and trimmed with bright ribbon bows.</p>
      </div>
