      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTII. . Some Useful Information.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTII. . Some Useful Information.</p>
        <p>An example of how hard labour might have been saved, and by a little foresight and due consideration, may be gathered from the fol- lowing :—An agent of the Department of Agri- culture recently learned of a farm woman in Pennsylvania who had been carrying coal from the barn to the kitchen for years and had finally I broken down from overwork. When the hus- band was 'asked if there was any reason why a coal bunker could not have been provided near the cookstove and filled directly from the waggon, Ile answered that there was none, but that he had never thought about it. Just a little remedy that could have easily been applied.</p>
        <p>More Simplicity in Furnishing. The modern tendency to live out of doors as far as possible has done a good deal to simplify our ideas of housekeeping, and rectify our ideas of house furnishing. We have begun to realise that our houses are places to eat and sleep in, and to protect us from the exigencies of the weather, and that our real life is something more than keeping a needless accumulation of household goods (or gods it may be) in unim- peachable order. It is not that we wish to do things less perfectly than our grandmothers did, or that we regard the great art of house- wifery as in any degree less important. Only we have begun to understand that it is not the only thing in life—even for the busy wife and mother—and that there is no sound reason why we should deliberately make enough domestic work to crush out wider interests from our lives, if, by reducing the number of our "not-essential" possessions, and re-arranging some of our ideas, we may widen our horizon and find a new zest in life. Nor does this mean lessening the happiness or comfort of the other members of our households. Husbands, brothers, sons, and daughters,' unless they have been shamefully spoiled, have far more pleasure in life when the woman at the domestic helm has enough vitality left and interest in life generally to be \ cheerful.—" North British Agriculturist."</p>
        <p>Her Objection. "By the way, Elder Browne, why is it that you always address your congregation as 'brethren,' and never mention the women in your sermons?" "But, my dear madam, the one embraces the other." "Oh, but, elder, not in church !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>MEMS. FOR MOTHERS. A Baby Girl's Dutch Bonnet.</head>
        <p>MEMS. FOR MOTHERS. A Baby Girl's Dutch Bonnet.</p>
        <p>Cast on 136 stitches, knit 8 rows rib (1 stitch knit and 1 stitch purl), then proceed in double patent knitting for 21- inches. Double patent knitting is as follows :-Ist row: Slip 1, knit 1, *make 1, slip 1, as if to purl it, knit 2 together, and repeat from *, ending with knitting 2. Ist row: Slip 1, knit 1, * m 1, s. 1 as if to purl it, k. 2 tog.; repeat from * and end' with k. 2. 2nd row: K. all stitches except the made ones, these are slipped on to the knitting needle.</p>
        <p>3rd row: S. 1, k. I, * take the two stitches that are crossed together, m. 1, and slip the single stitch and k. 2.</p>
        <p>Repeat Ist, 2nd and 3rd rows for length re- quired.</p>
        <p>Work 9 rows, 1 row k. and 1 row purl. 1 Next work 41 inches of the double patent knitting, then 11 inches of (k. 1, p. 1). Begin no to narrow in for the crown in the following manner :- Ist row: * Take 2 tog., p. 1, k. 1, p. 1, repeat from * to end of row. 2nd row: Rib (k. 1, P. 1). 3rd row: * K. 1, s. 1, take 2 tog:, pass slipped stitch over; repeat from * to end of row. 4th row: Purl. sth row: Same as third row. Break off the wool about half a yard from the stitches, and with a wool needle thread it through the stitches on the needle, and draw up very firmly and fasten off. Sew the two edges from the centre downwards for about 21 inches to 3 inches. Turn back the front, and seam it back for about an inch. Finish off the centre of crown with a small silk button. This is a specially comfortable little bonnet for rough weather.</p>
      </div>
