      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>ATTACK ON GERMAN TRADE. Enemies' Patent and Trade Mark Rights Suspended.</head>
        <p>ATTACK ON GERMAN TRADE. Enemies' Patent and Trade Mark Rights Suspended.</p>
        <p>The Board of Trade have issued rules under the Patents, Designs, and Trade Marks (Tem- porary Rules) Act, 1914, regulating the pro- cedure for the avoidance or suspension of any patent or licence granted to or trade mark the proprietor of which is a subject of any State at war with His Majesty. Where an application is made to the Board they must be satisfied that the applicant intends to manufacture the article concerned or carry on the patented process, and also that it is in the public interest that this should be done. The rules provide also for the avoidance or suspension of any application for a patent or trade mark by the subject of an enemy State. This means that British trade is not to be hampered during the war by reason of patent rights, &amp;c., having been granted to Germans and Austrians. The holders of these rights cannot exercise them now in this country, and other people will be allowed to step in.</p>
        <p>A WIDE FIELD IN CHINA. We may fairly assume that the joint action now being taken by Great Britain and Japan in China will result in the evacuation of Biacchan, and the "leased territory" hinterland of that colony, by its German military and naval forces. Our object, as now succinctly defined by the Japanese Prime Minister, is "to elim- inate from continental China the root of Ger- man influence." To attain that object it will be necessary, in the first place, to put an end to the German "sphere of influence" in Shan- tung, together with all the exclusive claims which have there been exercised since 1898 in violation of China's sovereignty and the treaty rights of other nations, and in the second place to take advantage of every opportunity which may present itself for diverting German com- merce to our advantage. It must be borne in mind that, although the situation presents excellent opportunities, British traders cannot expect to have the field to themselves. The Japanese, by reason of their geographical position and knowledge of local conditions, are likely to derive large and immediate profit from the suspension of Ger- man business, especially in inland provinces, such as Su-chuan, where British manufacturers are practically unrepresented. As regards rail- ways and mines, engineering works and indus- trial undertakings on a large scale, the Japan- ese are necessarily handicapped by lack of capital; here, if British enterprise be wisely guided, lies a wide field, from which systematic culture should extract a rich harvest.—" The Times."</p>
        <p>TRADE WITH FRANCE. A Press Association War Special Service tele- g fam from Paris says a number of French firms are anxious to know whether British manufacturers of tools, machine parts, and iron ware can supply goods which can replace simi- lar articles which have previously been ex- ported from Germany to France. The inquiry seems to have especial interest for Sheffield, Birmingham, and Coventry. The British Chamber of Commerce is prepared to assist British manufacturers in getting into touch with French firms. o.n the other hand several Paris houses, which are engaged in the export of dresses, millinery, embroidery, &amp;c., announce that, not- withstanding the war, they are carrying on business and are in a position to fulfil orders in a reasonable time. The British Chamber of Commerce in Paris is ready to facilitate com- munications with buyers in England.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>OPENINGS IN INDIA.</head>
        <p>OPENINGS IN INDIA.</p>
        <p>In a Blue Book relating to the trade of British India issued by the India Office it is Stated that amongst the imports of merchandise (exclusive of Government stores) from principal countries in 1912-13, Austria-Hungary accounted for goods to the value of £2,351,688, whilst Ger- many figured for £6,868,933. The exports for the same year were: Austria-Hungary, £4,834,774; Germany, £16,575,543. Under ship- ping the number of vessels entered in 1912-13 was: Austro-Hungarian 117, of 333,828 tonnage:* German 264, of 807,010 tonnage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>PROSPECT IN JAPAN.</head>
        <p>THE PROSPECT IN JAPAN.</p>
        <p>The following are German or Austro-Hungar- ian goods with which British manufacturing interests should be able to compete: Drugs, galvanised iron wire, worsted yarns, and elec- trical machinery. The iron and steel supply is calculated to suffice for six months. British 3nanufacturing interests )(ire strongly recom- mended to take advantage of the opportunity which must offer for the supply of goods, but it is absolutely essential that experts should pay a visit to Japan. The present stock of yarns is believed to be small.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>CHASING GERMAN CRUISERS. AVarrington _Seaman's Account of thi</head>
        <p>CHASING GERMAN CRUISERS. AVarrington _Seaman's Account of thi</p>
        <p>Mediterranean Hunt.</p>
        <p>The following letter has been received this week from Mr. John Tilling, son of Mr. Alfred Tilling, Lower Walton H.M.S. Duke of Edinburgh, Platea. "10/8/14. "We are just about to fill up with coal again behind the islands of Greece, with the other ships patrolling outside, as there is a German Dreadnought and cruiser in the vicinity. Our duty lately has been to stop them from joining the Austrian Fleet in the Adriatic. We had a shock the night before last, as we got a wireless to say we had declared war with Austria (after- wards the message was cancelled). "P.S.—We have had a decent job aboard, rip- ping down all the woodwork, as they find there are more lives lost by splinters than by the actual shells. All our stools and tables are gone, so we are living and sleeping (when we get a chance) on the decks." A letter received a few days previously stated: "Every man seems to be ready just the same as in Nelson's time."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>Summary of Authentic News From All Involved Areas.</head>
        <p>Summary of Authentic News From All Involved Areas.</p>
        <p>SPECIALLY COMPILED FOR "THE EXAMINER."</p>
        <p>FOURTH</p>
        <p>WEEK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>IN BELGIUM. THE ALLIES WITHDRAW BEFORE</head>
        <p>IN BELGIUM. THE ALLIES WITHDRAW BEFORE</p>
        <p>THE GERMANS. British In Action.</p>
        <p>The Germani having occupied Brussels, and, incidentally, inflicted a fine of ..t8,000,000 upon its inhabitants, passed on, and, for spectacular purposes, visited Ghent and threatened the coast. Paris explained the Belgian retreat to Ant- werp as a withdrawal, made "in conformity ' with the plan of defence drawn up many years ago." Antwerp, according to this view, be- came "a base of operations from which the Belgian Army can threaten the flank of the German Army penetrating into Belgium, and co-operate efficiently in the operations of the Allied arms." _ _</p>
        <p>It was given out that the Liege forts had not been reduced, and that those at Namur, which are equally powerful, had not as yet even been attacked.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>FIRST GREAT BATTLE. British Troops Stand Their Ground and Inflict Punishment.</head>
        <p>FIRST GREAT BATTLE. British Troops Stand Their Ground and Inflict Punishment.</p>
        <p>Leaving Brussels :and Antwerp to take care of themselves, the German Army began to press southward towards the French frontier. On Monday we learnt that the Germans had partially invested Namur and had opened upon its forts with artillery. On Monday afternoon this country was startled to receive the news that Namur, which was supposed to be the pivot upon which the Allies were to work, had fallen, and that the British forces—the first official mention of them—had been thereby obliged to retire to a defensive position. The official account ran:—</p>
        <p>Press Bureau, Monday, 2.30 p.mt The British forces were engaged all day on Sunday and after dark with the enemy in the neighbourhood of Mons, and held their ground. News has been received that the first line of defence of Namur has been taken. This necessitates the withdrawal of a por- tion of the allied troops from the line of the Sambre to their original defensive position on the French frontier. No information has been received regarding casualties, which will be published as soon as known. • 3.45 p.m. It is announced that Namur has fallen. Tuesday, 2 a.m. The British forces have successfully reached their new position. Fighting has gone on more or less continuously, but the enemy has not effectively harassed our operations, and the movement has been executed with great skill by the commanders of the First and Second Army Corps. Casualties cannot be estimated exactly, but are not heavy. Our forces were opposed by two German army corps and two cavalry divisions. The enemy suffered very heavily. The position now occu- pied is well protected.</p>
        <p>A DESPERATE STRUGGLE. It was clear that the Allies in Belgium were meeting the full shock of the German Array. It is essential for Germany that she should deal a paralysing blow in this quarter in order , to gain time to detach troops to resist the menacing Russian attack on the Prussian frontier. A "Times" correspondent who saw much of the fighting in Southern Belgium telegraphs from Paris an account of events up to Sunday. lle describes the struggle for Charleroi as des- perate. The town was taken and re-taken and taken again by French, and,German troops, who taught for hours to obtain control of the canal 'brido'e. Dead soldiers choked the streets, in some places still standing because they had not room to fall. Finally the French withdrew to the south, not beaten, but confident of vic- tory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>BRITISH STEADY AND DOGGED.</head>
        <p>BRITISH STEADY AND DOGGED.</p>
        <p>Losses Over 2,000, But Undaunted.</p>
        <p>The British Army round Mons was neces- sarily involved in the withdrawal of the Allies. Tributes to its steadiness and dogged resistance against superior forces came from all sides. In the House of Lords on Wednesday, Lord Kitchener, making his first statement as Secre- tary for War, announced that he had tele- graphed to Sir John French :—" Congratulate the troops on their splendid work. We are all proud of them."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>OFFICIAL REPORTS.</head>
        <p>OFFICIAL REPORTS.</p>
        <p>A Successful Retirement.</p>
        <p>The Prime Minister made the following announcement on the re-assembling of the House of Commons on Wednesday afternoon:— We have heard this morning from Sir John French that the withdrawal already an- nounced of his troops 0 their new position has been successfully effected. It was not accomplished without considerable loss. They were pressed.hard by the enemy,' who were,</p>
        <p>however, shaken off.. It is not desirable to say anything more for the moment, except that the Field Mar- shal reports that in spite of some hard marching the best of spirits. He provisionally esti- and fighting the British force is in mates the casualties at something over 2,000, but we have as yet received no names.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>FRENCH EXPLANATIONS.</head>
        <p>FRENCH EXPLANATIONS.</p>
        <p>The following official despatch was received by the French Embassy in London lath on Tuesday night:— The movement which was begun yesterday by order of the Commander-in-Chief was con- tinued to-day without successful opposition by the enemy. It is confirmed that the Prussian Corps of the Guard was roughly handled in the recent fighting. It was attacked by the Turcos (Algerian riflemen), and in the hand-to-hand fighting which ensued the Germans suffered heavily. The attacks against Nancy have failed. The Belgians in a sortie from Antwerp have re•taken Malines and driven the Germans back to yilvoerde. . French accounts of the fighting by the British at Mons say that our troops between Saturday and Sunday bore the brunt of six furious attacks by German columns. All were repulsed suc- cessfully. Wounded soldiers declare that the allies raised a "veritable hecatomb" of Ger- man dead. BACK ON THE FRENCH FRONTIER. A summary Of this first trial of strength, which went uniformly against the Allies, is that the French, not content with remaining on the defensive, and influenced doubtless by their desire to attack the German invaders in Belgium itself, attempted to take the offensive in the neighbourhood of Namur and Charleroi and to drive a wedge into the German advance. The forward movements of their armies in Lorraine and Alsace earlier in last week were part of this general plan of campaign and had some temporary success. But on the whole long frontier, from Mons in the north to Belfort in the south, this attempt to carry the war into the enemy's country has been thrown back, and everywhere the German armies were hammering at the French frontier.</p>
        <p>MARINES FOR OSTEND. In the House of Commons on Thursday Mr. Churchill announced that a strong force of Marines had been landed at Ostend and had occupied the town and the surrounding dis- tricts.</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>FRANCE INVADED.</head>
        <p>FRANCE INVADED.</p>
        <p>GRADUAL ADVANCE OF THE GERMANS IN GREAT FORCE.</p>
        <p>Prospects Satisfactory.</p>
        <p>The German forces, after a short breathing space, came on in great force, and the Allies had to fall back in places in face of superior numbers. The situation has been obscure all week, but early yesterday morning the Press Bureau issued the folbswing : The French operations of war over a dis- tance of some 250 miles have necessitated certain changes in the positions of our troops, who are now occupying a strong line to meet the German advance, supported by the French Army on both flanks. . .</p>
        <p>- Tie morale of both Armies appears to be</p>
        <p>excellent, and there is little doll-1)1 that they will give a good account of themselves in the Positions they now hold. On Thursday evening the Prime Minister had stated :</p>
        <p>Sir John French reports that he was en- gaged yesterday against a superior force. He says his troops fought splendidly, and he con- siders the position and prospects of the Army in the now impending battle satisfactory. He speaks in high terms of the quality and effi- ciency of the French regular troops and of their officers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>BRITISH ASSAILED BY 200,000 GERMANS.</head>
        <p>BRITISH ASSAILED BY 200,000 GERMANS.</p>
        <p>Behaviour Admirable, But Losses</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons last night, Mr. Asquith, replying to Mr. Bonar Law, said he had received a further message from Sir John French of the fighting which took place between our army and the enemy on Wednes- day in the neighbourhood of Cambrai. The British troops were exposed to the attack of five German army corps, two cavalry divi- sions of reserve corps of the guard of cavalry, and the second cavalry division. Our Second Army Corps bore the brunt of the cavalry attack, while the First Army Corps was attacked on the right and inflicted very I heavy loss on the enemy. • Our casualties were heavy. The exact figures were not yet known. The behaviour of the troops was in all re- spects admirable, and General Joffre had con- veyed his congratulations and sincere thanks I for the protection so successfully given by our I army to the French flank.</p>
        <p>REINFORCEMENTS FOR OUR TROOPS. Lord Kitchener in the House of Lords yester- day afternoon announced that in addition to reinforcements which will shortly proceed from this country to France the Government had de- cided that our Army in France should be re- inforced by two divisions and a cavalry division, besides other troops from India.</p>
        <p>The first division of these troops was now on its way. He added that all the wastage in the army in France was being immediately filled up, and there were some 12,000 men waiting for that purpose on the lines of communication.</p>
        <p>NATIVE INDIAN TROOPS DESIRE TO ASSIST.</p>
        <p>Lord Crewe stated that it was the urgent desire of native Indians that they should be allowed to fight side by side with English troops in the same manner that Africans were fighting Nzith the French. If they refused them they would have grievously disappoinled a most loyal people.</p>
        <p>BRITISH FLEET'S MESSAGE TO THE • ARMY.</p>
        <p>The following' telegram has been sent to Field Marshal Sir John French by Admiral Sir John Jellicoe on behalf of the Home Fleet:—</p>
        <p>" Officers .and men of the Grand Fleet wish to express to their comrades of the. Army ad- miration of magnificent stand 'made against great odds, and wish them the brilliant suc- cess which the, Fleet feels sure awaits their further efforts." GENERAL JOFFRE'S THANKS.</p>
        <p>The Secretary of State has received the fol- lowing telegram, which has been addressed to ,Sir John French by General Joffre : "The British Army did not hesitate to throw its whole .strength against forces which had a great numerical superiority. In so doing it contributed in the most effective manner to securir'r, the left flank of the French Army. It exhibited in this task a devotion, energy and perseverance to which I must now pay my tribute qualities which will be shown again to-morrow and make certain the triumph of our common cause. The French Army will never forget the service rendered it. That Army is inspired with the same spirit of self-sacrifice and determination to conquer which animates the British Forces and will make good to it its debt of gratitude in the battles of the near future."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>ALSACE-LORRAINE</head>
        <p>ALSACE-LORRAINE</p>
        <p>CHANGING FORTUNES AND FRENCII RETIREMENTS.</p>
        <p>In Alsace and Lorraine, where the French had assumed the aggressive from the outbreak of hostilities, this has been a momentous week. Last week--end it was reported from Alsace that the French advance continued, and the Ger- mans were said to have lost 24 gulls and to be retreating on the Rhine, leaving behind them numbers of prisoners. The following official communique was issued on Thursday last week:— Our troops have gained a brilliant success, particularly between Mulhausen and Altkirch. The Germans are falling back on the Rhine, and have left in our hands a large number of prisoners; 24 guns have been captured by our troops—six of them by the Infantry—in the course of this battle. The Germans, driven out of Mulhausen for the second time, suffered severely. On Monday night the French Government issued an official statement dealing with the whole position. According to this the array in Alsace and the Vosges was holding its own.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>FRENCH CHECK IN LORRAINE,</head>
        <p>FRENCH CHECK IN LORRAINE,</p>
        <p>In Lorraine the French advance was chee by a vigorous counter-attack on the front be- tween Metz and the Vosges mountains. The Germans delivered a counter-attack, which threw it back on the main body, hohyg a strong position on the River Seille. It was admitted in Paris that Frencli out- posts flung themselves unsuccessfully upon several strong positions, and were borne back by a counter-attack on the main' force, which had taken up a strong position on the Seille and along the Marne Canal towards the Rhine. Later, it ,was found that the French Army had been forced to fall back, and that Lune- ville, which is well within the French frontier, had been occupied by the Germans. Thus the French on this frontier had been compelled to abandon the line from Mount Donon to Toul, whi'ph they had held for some days. The Germans claimed to have taken 10,000 prisoners, colours, and 150 guns. This was stated by the French to be an exaggeration, the French successes in Alsace having been as great as the German success in Lorraine.</p>
        <p>kecl</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>GERMANS REPULSED.</head>
        <p>GERMANS REPULSED.</p>
        <p>The Press Bureau intimated on Wednesday afternoon that it was officially announced that on August 25 the French on their southern frontier were attacked in force by the Germans. The attack was repulsed, and the enemy retired all along the line.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>RUSSIAN ADVANCE.</head>
        <p>RUSSIAN ADVANCE.</p>
        <p>VICTORY IN PRUSSIA.</p>
        <p>A Nasty Surprise for the Germans.</p>
        <p>Last week-end came the welcome news that Russia was still showing signs that she had moved sooner than either her best friends had hoped or her worst enemies had feared. On Saturday we learned that the Russian advance in East Prussia was being pushed towards Lotzen, a fortified post; and on Monday came the news that Russian troops had completely routed three German Army Corps near Gum- binnen on Thursday and Friday. The defeat of the Germans by the Russian Army moving from Vilna was converted into a rout by the • timely arrival of another powerful Army com- ing from Warsaw in the rear of the enemy's position. The German 20th Army Corps, thus taken between the Warsaw Army at Alleustein and the Vilna Army at Insterburg, was com- parable to a nut placed within crackers. M. Viviani, the French Premier, telegraphed on Sunday to the Russian Commander-in-Chief: "This victory is an 'augury of the crushing of the tyranny under which you are at present suffering."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>JAPANESE COMPARISONS.</head>
        <p>JAPANESE COMPARISONS.</p>
        <p>General Rennenkamf, the successful comman- der, was one of the few Russian officers to do well against the Japanese in Manchuria. Russian observers praise the enemy's equip- ment and artillery, but say the rifle shooting is much inferior to the Japanese. The Japanese, they say, did in an hour what the Germans take a day to perform.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>AUSTRIA.</head>
        <p>AUSTRIA.</p>
        <p>EAVILIT DEFEATED BY SELMA</p>
        <p>On August 16th and 17th the Servians de- feated the Austrians, 60,000 strong, at Shabatz- Lenznitza, and then invaded their territory. Reports say that the 13th, 15th and 16th Corps of the Austro-Hungarian Army were completely routed, with great loss of men, guns and mate- rial of war. _ _</p>
        <p>The battle took place in the hills fronting the River Drina, beyond which lies Bosnia. The Austrian loss in men is unknown, but Servia,n estimates give it as thousands, and the annihilation of whole battalions is spoken of. The captures can be more accurately given. These the Servian Press Bureau states as fol- lows :-4,500 prisoners, 53 Cannon, eight howit- zers, 114 ammunition carts, three field hospi- tals, one sanitary column with material, 14 field kitchens, six provision waggons, four carts con- taining material for telegraphy, five powder vans, a large quantity of carts, trains, guns, engineers' material, and regimental strong boxes. The Servian Army followed up this success by inflicting another severe defeat upon the Austrians. An official statement issued in Vienna admits these reverses, but explains them on the ground that it has been necessary to concentrate the main Austrian force upon the north-east frontier to resist the Russian advance. With rare grace, in the circumstances, the Austrians announced that the campaign against Servia must in future be regarded more as a "Punitive expedition" than a "definite war."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>FAR EAST. JAPAN DECLARES_WAR ON</head>
        <p>FAR EAST. JAPAN DECLARES_WAR ON</p>
        <p>GERMANY.</p>
        <p>Japan, having received no reply from Ger- many to her ultimatum, formally declared that since noon on Sunday (Japanese time) a state of war has existed between Japan and Ger- many. The blockade of Tsing-tau, the port of Kiao- chau, by the Japanese Fleet has begun. The Kaiser is reported to have sent a message to the garrison instructing them to defend the position at Tsing-tau to their utmost. Diplomatic relations between Austria and Japan have been broken off. The Austrian Am- bassador in Tokio has been recalled, and the Japanese Ambassador in Vienna has been handed his passports.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>BRITISH DESTROYER SHELLED.</head>
        <p>BRITISH DESTROYER SHELLED.</p>
        <p>While chasing a German destroyer on Satur- day—before Japan had declared war on Ger- many—the British destroyer Kennet (river class) approached to close to a battery at Tsing.tau, and although the Kennet was not materially damaged, a petty officer and two able seamen were killed, and three ;nen were severely and four slightly injured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>SOUTH AFRICA. A GERMAN RAID INTO UNION</head>
        <p>SOUTH AFRICA. A GERMAN RAID INTO UNION</p>
        <p>TERRITORY.</p>
        <p>A raid from German South-West Africa into Union territory is reported. Before entering British Namaqualand the raiders lost a sergeant and several men in a conflict with Dutch far- mers settled in German territory. The raid is regarded as unimportant, but effective mea- sures are to be taken by the Union Govern- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>NAVAL SUCCESSES GERMAN COMMERCE RAIDER SUNK.</head>
        <p>NAVAL SUCCESSES GERMAN COMMERCE RAIDER SUNK.</p>
        <p>The German armed merchant cruiser Kaiser Wilhelm der Grosse, of 14,000 tons and armed, with 10 guns of approximately 4in. calibre, has been sunk by H.M.S. Highflyer off the Oro River, on the West African Coast. This is the vessel which has been endeavour-</p>
        <p>ing to arrest traffic between this country and the Cape, and is one of the very few German armed auxiliary cruisers which has succeeded in getting to sea. The survivors were landed before the vessel sank.</p>
        <p>The Press Bureau issued the following state- ments later in the evening The following message has been sent to the cruiser Highilyer : "Admiralty to Higliflyer. Bravo! You have rendered a service not only to Britain but to the peaceful commerce of the world. The German officers and crew appear to have carried out their duties with humanity and restraint, and are therefore worthy of all seamanlike consideration."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>HIGHFLYER.</head>
        <p>THE HIGHFLYER.</p>
        <p>The Highflyer, which is commanded by Cap- thin H. T. Buller, and has been used as a training ship for naval cadets, is a 20-knot light cruiser completed in 1900. She has a displacement of 5,600 tons, the length being 330 ft., breadth 54ft., and draught 201-ft. The indicated h.p. is 10,000. The Highflyer carries eleven 6in., nine 3in., one three-pounder, and two machine guns, as well as two torpedo tubes, and her complement is 456 men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>POINT TO RUSSIA. German Cruiser Blown Up.</head>
        <p>A POINT TO RUSSIA. German Cruiser Blown Up.</p>
        <p>The German light cruiser Magdeburg ran ashore in a fog on the island of Odensholm at the entrance of the Gulf of Finland. Owing to the thick weather the other German war-. ships in the vicinity were . unable to render assistance, and all efforts to refloat the vessel having failed, the captain decided to sacrifice her, as a superior Russian naval force was Preparing to attack. Under heavy fire from the Russian Fleet, the majority of the cruiser's crew was saved by torpedo-boat V 26. Seventeen men were killed and 21 wounded. Eighty-five are missing, including the captain. The Mag- deburg was blown up. The Magdeburg was one of the fastest of the German cruising ships, of which about ten were supposed to be attached to the squadron in the Baltic, in addition to twenty engaged as com" merce-destroyers. The Magdeburg's speed was put at 27 knots. She was built at Bremen in 1911-12, and carried a crew of 370, of whom about 250 were apparently saved. She carried twelve 41n. guns and two submerged torpedo tubes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-29_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>FURNISH</head>
        <p>FURNISH</p>
