        <p>will be bound to draw all the supplies they get from the outside. Dutchmen could therefore go to various centres in Germany and make it kiloWn through the hotels and even the news- papers that they were there to afford facilities to British subjects to reach Holland. • VICTIM IDENTIFIED. . The gentleman referred to as Mr. Mackenzie, it has been found, was the Rev. John Munro Mackenzie, a native of Ross-shire, and formerly of Wick and afterwards of the Mount Pleasant Presbyterian Church, Liverpool. For several years Mr. Mackenzie had been living in London. He was 62 years of age. His wife, who predeceased him, was a daughter of the late Mr. Thomas Droysdale, of Liverpool, a well-known South American merchant.</p>
        <p>A AVarning. The Chief Constable of Lancashire (Mr. p. P. Lane) has warned the public that all' waterworks in the county are guarded by armed police, and that any unauthorised person ap- proaching or trespassing on the works will ren- der himself liable to be fired at. All persons," says the notice, "are warned to keep at a dis- tance from any reservoir, pipe track, or water course, and on no account to loiter in the vicinAy. Any person found damaging or at- tempting to damage or interfering with or at- tempting to interfere with any of the works will be immediately arrested."</p>
