        <p>Tommy Atkins, Traveller. Some idea of how well the secret of their Ultimate destination was kept even from the soldiers of our Expeditionary Force may be gathered from the fact that their favourite song on arriving in France was "It's a long way to Tipper-ary."_" Punch." • The Misfortune of War. Tired Tim (reading poster "Work for all"): "'Ere, I don't arf like the look o' this, Bill." Work-shy : "No, more don't I, mate. Cuss-that there Kaiser I" The Landlady's Impression. Dissatisfied Lodger: "And I know something about' apartments, Mrs. Pincher. You don't suppose I've lived in them 20 years for nothing. do you?" Mrs. Pincher: "Hi shouldn't be at all sur- prised." _ • .</p>
        <p>A New Overseer. Councillor Locker has been appointed an over- seer of the poor for Warrington in place of the late Alderman Forshaw. New Grammar School Governor. The Mayor (Alderman Peacock) has been ap- pointed a representative governor of the War- rington Grammar School in place of the late Alderman Forshaw. Insurance Committee Member. Miss Mary Roberts, 2, Kendrick-street, has been appointed a member of the Insurance Com- mittee in place of Mrs. Simpson, who has left the district. Sale of Furniture. Mr. Frank Hickman will sell by auction on Tuesday next at 4, Ellesmere-road, Stockton Heath, a large quantity of valuable household furniture. A Methodist I%lemOrig. At the Methodist Free Church, Buckley. street, on Monday evening, Mrs. J. Dodds will unveil the memorial tablet erected to the memory of faithful servants of the church. The Mayor (Alderman Peacock) will preside. A Paduate Sensation. A sensation was caused in the Padgate-road district early this week when it was alleged that .a man attacked two women walking through a field. An alarm was given and the man was chased. At Paddington Locks he was held up and arrested by the lock-keeper, who was armed with a rifle. The man was brought up on a charge of alleged assault at Newton Petty Ses- sions on Thursday, and remanded to a special Court at Warrington on Tuesday.</p>
        <p>THE NATION'S DUTY,</p>
        <p>CRICKET. Wahrmgton V. Wigan. Played at Warrington on Saturday. Scores: Warrington.-W. Richardson run out 9. S. L. Partridge h Urmson 37, G. Woodcock b Uns- worth 2, B. Silcock c Tyler h Unsworth 0, 3. Thompson 1) Unsworth 6,, H. Hawthorn c Good- win b Tyler 24, Rev. J. M. Buckmaster b Tyler 3, Hannam c Roocroft b Unsworth 1, W. G. Hodgson not out 4, Blomley c Urmson b Tyler 0, W. Bennett b Tyler 0, extras 15; total 101. Wigan.-Tyler b Partridge 33, S. G. Urmson b Hannam 5, N. A. Selkirk b Bennett 21, W. Smith 1) Bennett 59, N. Dowswell b Bennett 0, G. Roocroft h Bennett 0, W. F. Goodwin b Ben- nett 15, Meadows not out 6, C. Armstrong, H. R. Ellis and E. E. Unsworth did not bat, extras 5; total 144. Warrington 2nd v. Wigan 2nd. Played at Wigan on Saturday. Scores:- Warrington.-H. Partridge lbw b Roocroft 1, T. Dixon c Roocroft b Dawber 5, G. Whitlow st. Laithwaite; jun. b Roocroft 4, J. Wills b Roocroft 5, C. Jenkinson b Roocroft 6, W. V. Turton c Laithwaite, jun., b Dawber 2, W. Entwistle e G. C. Healey h Dawber 5, J. Mclntyre c Laithwaite, jun., 1) Roocroft 9, W. H. Bell not out 11, A. D. Heys 1) Roocroft 17, A. Holbrook c Laithwaite, sen., Ii Roocroft 0, extras 2; total 67. Wigan.-J. Healey b Jenkinson 0, H. P. Close e Bell b Johnson 25, G. C. Healey not out 62, A. Dawber b Jenkinson 14, H. Prescott b Heys 23, W. Roocroft not out 20, extras 11; total (for four wickets) 155.</p>
        <p>THIS YEAR'S CROPS. Wheat Better Than For Last Ten Years. The Board of Agriculture and Fisheries have issued a return showing the estimated produc- tion of each of the crops in the United Kingdom. The wheat crop is 10 per cent, larger than that of 1913, and is considerably above the average of the last 10 years. The production of barley, although less than in 1913, is larger than in either of the three preceding years. Oats are under average. The potato crop in Ireland is estimated at about 10 per cent. less than last year, so that the total for the United Kingdom falls about 5 per cent. below 1913, whcn the crop was the largest on record. The number of cattle in the 'United Kingdom is larger than in any previous year.</p>
        <p>RIFLE SHOOTING. APPLETON CLUB. All the members on the range on Saturday took part in a full charge competition at 100 yards, with the service weapon. Six shots were fired, the best five to count, at a decimal target, possible score 50, handicap to be added. Mr. A. Down kindly provid,d the prizes. Score. H'cap T'L 1. F. Nicholl, Ist prize 2. F. C. Bennion, 2nd „ (tie) 47 1 48 3. P. Harrison, 3rd prize (tie) 44 4 48 4. A. Shepherd 5. C. Jackson 6. S. Massey .7. J. Grocott 8. IL M. Swinton 9. C. Unsworth 10. W. Featherstone 11. L. Fairclough • 12. J. Towers 21 1 22 13. A. Down 14: J. Hartley 15. I. Broady 16. E. Caldwell 17. C. J. Smith Skirmisher (Small Bore) Competition. A sweepstake took place at 25 yards at a green target containing four black bulls,, on any one not more than four shots to be fired, unlimited firing for one minute. No practice or sighting. Hits. I. A. Shepherd 2: P. Harrison 3. J. Towers 4. F. C. Bennion 5. J. Hartley 6. C. Unsworth 7. C. Jackson 8. C. Caldwell 9. R. Swinton 10. F. Nicholl 11. S. Massey 12. W. Featherstone • 13. .I. Broady All the prize money and the cost of the ammunition in the full charge competition (205.) has been given to the local war fund. LAWN TENNIS. CAUSEWAY v. LEVENSHULtiIE. Played at Levenshulme on Saturday. Details: J. Bowen and S. Woods beat F. Towns and W. Hobson, 6-2, 6-4; beat H. Towns and Hop- kinson, 6-1, 6-1; beat Cooper and Ellis, 6-4, 6-4. 11. Bibby and J. Paterson lost to F. Towns and W. Hobson, 5-6, 6-4, 4-6; beat H. Towns and Hopkinson, 6-2, 6-1; beat Cooper and Ellis, 5-6, 6-0, 6-1. C. E. B. Sutton and H. Holt lost to F. Towns ' and W. Hobson, 1-6, 4-6; beat H. Towns and Hopkinson, 6-4, 6-4; lost to Cooper and Ellis, 5-6, 3-6. Mrs. Hope and Miss Wilson lost to Miss Tunaley and Miss Turner, 5-6, 3-6; beat Miss Hargreaves and Miss Smith, 6-2, 6-3. Miss D. Isaac and Mrs. Woods lost to Miss Tunaley and Miss Turner, 2-7-6, 0-6; lost to Miss Hargreayes and Miss Smith, 2-6, 2-6. Result: Causeway won 7 events to 6; 15 sets . to 13; 131 games to 115. • A</p>
        <p>WHITE HEATHER DAY. To Help the, Poor Children's Camp and Nursing Association. WORKS v. SCOTS. To harness usefulness to pleasure was the object of the annual bowling match between the Warrington Caledonians and the Works Associa- tion, which, this year, was played on the green adjoining the Brooklands Hotel in Lovely-lane. The proceeds, which are to be divided between the Poor Children's Camp and the Nursing Association, were considerably augmented by the sale of sprigs of white heather " frae Bonnie Seotland." In this direction a number of you% ladies had busied themselves in the streets of the town, and their commendable efforts earned the gratitude of the promoters of the event. A large crowd fringed the green at the Lovely- lane hostelry. Amongst those present were the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Peter Peacock), Councillor Wilkinson, Councillor Locker, and Mr. J. Fulton-Smith. gverybody wore white heather, and everybody was enthusi- astic, especially when the Mayor stepped on to the green to compete in his game. MR. A. H. CROSFIELD DISAPPOINTED. Mr. A. H. Crosfield had intended to be present, and on the previous night had despatched a tele- gram from London stating "Great pleasure join- ing you Brooklands Hotel th-morrow afternoon." However, Mr. Crosfield was unable to make an appearance, and a later telegram, read by Mr. J. Fulton-Smith, stated: "To my very great regret unforeseen circum- stances compel me to remain in London to-day. Please give most cordial greetings to all friends. —ARTHUR CROSFIELD." (Applause.) Mr. Fulton-Smith added that they were all very sorry Mr. Crosfield was not present. They also regretted the absence of the piper, who, in years past, had given selections. "QUITE IN ORDER." The Mayor, who was received with cheers, said it was a pleasure to be amongst his friends to engage in a game of bowls, and if his opponents had not got too near the " jack " he was afraid he would have given a better account of him- self. (Laughter.) • Although they had the great war across the water looming up before them, and they had men fighting their battles, he thought the game that day was quite in order, for they were for charitable organisations. (Hear, hear.) If it was necessary, no doubt the Caledonian and the Works Association would join together later on for another cause. (Hear, hear.) The Mayor jocularly remarked that he did not know sufficiently enough of his pedigree to say whether he had any Scotch in him, but he sup- posed that naturally he took anything he could get hold of, and therefore could be counted as a Scotchman. (Loud laughter.) The present event had entailed a very large amount of work on the officials, who deserved all praise, and he voiced the thanks of the company to. Messrs. Tennant and Patrick, the two secretaries. He felt cer- thin that the result of their efforts would he a success, and that the two charitable objects would benefit very considerably. Mr. Tennant said that the event this year had exceeded expectations, and that next year they hoped to have a greater number ,selling heather. (Hear, hear.) THANKS TO THE MAYOR. Mr. Willett proposed a vote of thanks, which, he stated, needed no seconder, to the Mayor and Mayoress and the "lassies who had been selling the heather." The latter deserved all praise, for they had volunteered most willingly. Their thoughts, however, must wander to the soldiers across the water, and he wished them a speedy return. (Applause.) The Mayor, who replied on behalf of the Mayoress, added his testimony to the work of the girls, to whom, he said, great credit was due. Mr. Patrick, who was called upon for a speech,. said he had always taken a great interest in the Nursing Association and the Poor Children's Camp, because he knew :bat those who got the benefit were the poorest of the poor. Later, the two Associations who were engaged in the com- petition might be called upon to do work in another sphere—in reference to the war—for he was hoping to help to raise a fund to assist those at the front. (Applause.) They had only to get two teams, make the Mayor the captain of one team and Mr. Harold Smith the captain of the cther, and they could realise .t 25 on any green in the town. (Loud applause.) He had recently been to a town where he asked why people were all wearing little flags, and was informed thit they were bought for a copper and the money given to the war fund. They should copy that examoe, for, he concluded, they had the mak- ings of a great fund in Warrington—even with the pennies. (Applause.) THE MATCH. The match was then concluded. It resulted in a very narrow victory for the Works Associa- tion, whose score was 144, against 142 secured by the Scotsmen. The details were (the Works Association names coming first) : Councillor J. Wilkinson (captain) and Council- lor Locker, 8; Mr. J. Fulton-Smith (captain) and (1. Bowman, 13. J. Willett (Crosfield's, sub-captain) and H. Webster (Cable Works), 13; J. L. Davenport (sub-captain) and W. Webster, 10. T. Boyd (Assurance Agents) and H. Rathbone (Firth), 13; T. Weir and F. Redfern, 12. C. H. Hibbert (Armitage and Rigby's) and J. Stanley (Grappenhall Tannery), 13; P. Lyle and J. Buchanan, 8. W. H. Dutton (P. and K.) and W. Warburton (Richmond's), 13; W. Murray and the Mayor, 9. Bouchier (Whitecross) and E. Seymour (Cable Works), 11; M. W. Grant and Watson, 13. E. Lunt (Fennel-street Tannery) and E. Walker (Cheshire Lines), 2; R. Mitchell and E. Sanders, 13. T. Reddish (Rylands') and T. Cooper (Cheshire Lines). 13; W. Hutchison and H. Bell, 6. T. Horrabin (L. and N.-W.) and R. Straw (Greening's), 13; G. Vallance and W. Houghton, 2. A. Lingard (Thewlis and Griffiths') and G. Clive (Wire Ropers), 5; F. Plumpton and J. T. Smith. 13. A. Peckett (County Asylum) and G. Ward (White Lead), 2; Brooks and J. Aitken. 13. W. Deakin (Monk's, Hall) and J. Welsby, ; Clegg and P. H. Brown, 5. T. Bennett and S. Shave (Warrington Tan- nery). 12; R. B. Burns and J. Bell, 13. F. Dale and J. Watts, 13; Councillor Jenkins find Thomason, 12. Total—Works Association, 144; Caledonians. 142. Majority for Works Association team, 2.</p>
